// SplitLiveRanges — the cold-spill live-range splitter (design: ARCHITECTURE.md's
// register-allocator section, Rule 2). It is what relieves COLD pressure, so the colorer
// never faces "pressure > pool": a value that is idle across a
// region of high register pressure is SPLIT — it lives in memory across the gap and in a
// register only where it is used — so the colorer then always finds `maxPressure ≤ pool`.
// HOT pressure (a value a loop USES that still cannot fit) is the E5001 diagnostic —
// `chooseVictim` returns `none` and the driver throws `raiseRegisterPressure` (M5.7,
// RegisterPressureDiagnostic.maxon).
//
// THE TWO INVARIANTS THIS RESTS ON (design Rules 1 + 2):
//
//  • BELADY SPLIT AT THE EVICTION POINT — a spilled value's live range is split ACROSS THE
//    PEAK, not shipped whole to memory. The store goes after the value's LAST use BEFORE the
//    peak (or at its def when it is idle before the peak); the reload goes before its NEXT
//    use AFTER the peak. Only the uses AT/AFTER the split point are rewritten to the fresh
//    reload id — the uses BEFORE it keep the ORIGINAL value in its original register. This is
//    what makes the value DEAD across the peak (freeing its register there), so a split
//    actually LOWERS peak pressure and the loop converges. Splitting whole-range and reloading
//    before a block's FIRST use (the earlier design) is WRONG: a value used both before AND
//    after a peak would get its fresh reload before the pre-peak use, leaving that fresh value
//    live ACROSS the peak — pressure never drops and `chooseVictim` re-picks the same lineage
//    forever.
//
//  • DOMINATING RELOADS — the key insight that retires v1's SplitKit failure with NO SSA
//    reconstruction. Every reload is placed in a use's own block, before the first AFTER-peak
//    use in that block — which DOMINATES the after-peak uses it serves. So no merge ever sees
//    two definitions, NO phi is ever needed, and each reload just DEFINES A FRESH ValueId whose
//    after-peak uses are rewritten to it (keeping the program in SSA and Rule 1 intact). The
//    price is occasional duplicate reloads (one per after-peak use-block), all provably COLD.
//
//  • RULE 2 (spill placement) — a store or reload may be inserted at a point `q` only if,
//    for every loop containing `q`, the value has NO use or def inside that loop. This is
//    what makes cold spills cold: spill code is NEVER added to a loop body for a value the
//    loop uses. We guarantee it by only ever choosing a victim whose def AND every use are
//    at loop depth 0 (`isColdSpillable`), and placing the store and each reload at (depth-0)
//    use/def sites — so no store/reload ever lands in any loop at all. `assertPlacementLegal`
//    re-verifies this on every inserted op: a violation would be a HOT spill that should have
//    been E5001, so it panics rather than ship. A value idle across a loop it does not touch
//    is exactly the depth-0-def, depth-0-uses shape, split with ZERO instructions in the loop
//    body — the design's signature case.
//
//    RULE 2 AS AMENDED — refuse the SEARCH, not the SPILL. The paragraph above is the rule for a
//    FULL-POOL overflow only. At a CONFINED one (Hall's condition against a proper subset of the
//    pool: values a call denies the 9 caller-saved registers, or an `idiv` denies RAX/RDX) the value
//    CANNOT stay in a register across the clobbering op, so memory is not a choice the allocator
//    searches for — it is the only placement that exists. Such a bracket is therefore emitted at ANY
//    loop depth (`SpillPlacement.forced`), and it is never E5001: the program fits the machine, and
//    the restructuring E5001 would demand emits the same load and store at every use anyway. See
//    `SpillPlacement`, `SplitScope`, and `noVictimAtPeak`.
//
// REMAT (load-bearing, not an optimization): an integer constant (`movRegImm`/`movRegImm32`)
// is REMATERIALIZED rather than spilled — re-emitted at each use with a fresh ValueId, never
// a register across the gap and never memory. A constant is always PREFERRED over spilling a
// real value (remat is free — no store, no slot, no reload). shv2 has no `xor reg,reg` zero
// idiom yet (StdToX64Conversion emits `movRegImm32 0`), so only the two `movRegImm` forms are
// rematerializable here; rdata/witness-table address remat is a later-milestone class.
//
// ALGORITHM (iterative Belady/MIN with the dominating-reload restriction): recompute
// liveness; while some program point OVERFLOWS ITS POOL, find that PEAK op, choose a
// relievable value crossing it — a constant to rematerialize if one is available, else
// the value with the FARTHEST next use (Belady/MIN) — and split its live range AT the peak;
// repeat. Splitting at the peak makes the victim DEAD across the peak op, so pressure there
// drops by one and the loop converges (a store/reload only READS an already-live value / only
// SHIFTS which value is live, so neither ever raises the function's max pressure).
//
// A victim whose live range the eviction-point split does NOT cut — a loop-invariant a call inside
// the loop reads on BOTH sides of itself, so it is live across the call around the back edge no matter
// how its after-peak uses are rewritten — is relieved at a CONFINED peak by rewriting EVERY use
// instead (`SplitScope`). That is the case `noVictimAtPeak`'s confined panic used to hit.
//
// "OVERFLOWS ITS POOL" is per-point AND per-VALUE, because a clobber SHRINKS a value's pool: a value
// live across a call is forbidden all 9 caller-saved registers, so its only home is one of the 5
// callee-saved; one live across an `idiv` is confined to `pool ∖ {RAX,RDX}`. Values at one point can
// therefore be shopping in differently sized register sets, and no single number decides whether they
// fit. `analyzePressure` tests HALL'S CONDITION on those per-value pools (`HallCondition`): no
// register subset may have more values CONFINED to it than it has registers. That subsumes the old
// "compare the live count against `pool ∖ op.implicitDefs` AT the clobber op" check and additionally
// sees what that check structurally could not — values confined by DIFFERENT calls, colliding at a
// point that is not itself a call. `maxPressure ≤ pool` is NECESSARY but far from SUFFICIENT.
//
// ==========================================================================
// PERFORMANCE: EVERY PER-VALUE QUESTION IS ANSWERED FROM AN INDEX, NEVER A SCAN
// ==========================================================================
//
// `chooseVictim` asks the same handful of questions — "where is it defined?", "where is it
// used?", "is it a phi?", "is it edge-passed?", "is it already stored?", "does it touch a
// loop?" — of EVERY value crossing the peak. Answering each with its own walk of the function
// made the splitter O(candidates × blocks × ops) PER SPLIT, and it was 61% of register
// allocation whenever it ran. The function's `UseIndex` (TargetLiveness) answers all of them in O(1) (or O(uses of the one
// value)) from dense columns + a CSR use list keyed by value id, built in ONE pass over the
// function — the same counting-sort idiom as `TargetLiveness.buildUseIndex`, and for the same
// reason. The general rule that section states holds here too: **a sparse set is never walked
// by iterating the whole value space** — `chooseVictim`'s candidates come from
// `bitsetCollectRow` over the peak's live set, not from `for v in 0 upto valueCount`.
//
// ==========================================================================
// THE DRIVER IS INCREMENTAL: A SPLIT COSTS WHAT IT CHANGED, NOT WHAT THE FUNCTION IS
// ==========================================================================
//
// The driver used to rebuild liveness AND re-sweep the whole function for pressure after every split.
// K splits × O(function) is O(function × splits), and K grows with the function, so it was a genuine
// QUADRATIC — and pure waste. The scale suite's `pressuredLoops` shape says why: ONE function with N
// loops, each holding 8 accumulators live across a call, each needing ~5 forced spills. Loop i's
// accumulators are live ONLY inside loop i — they are disjoint from loop j's — so spilling one in loop
// 3 cannot change the pressure anywhere in loops 0..2 or 4..63. The old driver re-read all 64 loops
// after every one of the ~320 splits.
//
// **WHAT A SPLIT ACTUALLY CHANGES.** `spillOneValue` / `rematerializeConstant` insert store/reload/const
// ops into a few blocks, rewrite the victim's after-peak uses to fresh value ids, and mint those ids.
// Therefore:
//
//  • LIVENESS changes for the VICTIM (its range shrinks) and for the FRESH ids (new short ranges), and
//    for NO OTHER VALUE. That is exact, not approximate: `solveLivenessSsa` is a PER-VALUE backward
//    path exploration, so a value whose uses and def the split did not touch walks the same blocks and
//    marks the same sets.
//  • `forbidden[w]` is unchanged for every other `w`. The inserted ops are `storeSlotReg` /
//    `loadRegSlot` / a constant materialization, and all three carry `implicitDefs == 0` and no
//    physical def operand (TargetDialect), so a value newly live across one is forbidden nothing.
//  • SEQUENCE NUMBERS move only in blocks that received an op, and only past the insert: they are
//    block-relative (`seqAt`), so no other block is touched. `UseIndex`'s per-block record thread is
//    the index that repairs them in O(block).
//  • the per-block DEAD-PHI count and REGISTER DEMAND are functions of that block's own ops and live
//    sets.
//
// So the driver computes a DIRTY REGION per split — the victim's whole pre-split live range, its use
// and def blocks, every block an op was spliced into, and the new ranges of the victim and the fresh
// ids — and re-derives only that. See `refreshAfterSplit`, which states the region and why it is a
// SUPERSET of what moved. A superset is CORRECT (just slower); a subset is a SILENT MISCOMPILE, which
// is why `VerifyIncrementalSplit` exists.
//
// The DECISIONS are untouched. Same peak, same victim, same order, same code — the byte-exact
// specs-shv2 IR goldens do not move. The global peak is found by a TOURNAMENT over the per-block peaks
// (`PeakTree`), because a per-split fold over every block would merely MOVE the quadratic.
//
// ⭐ AND BLOCK GRANULARITY IS NOT ENOUGH, WHICH IS WHAT `PressureIndex` IS FOR. On a function that is
// ONE basic block the dirty region IS the whole function — `dirty` holds one entry and `PeakTree` has
// one leaf — so everything above buys nothing and each of the four per-split walks is Θ(the function).
// MEASURED on `tests/ladders/genwidelive.sh <N> sum` (Θ(N) values live at once in one block, hence
// Θ(N) splits over a Θ(N) block): those four walks were 91% of the compile, and the whole splitter grew
// ×3.20 then ×3.37 over a DOUBLING ladder. The FULL-POOL peak of a block is a max-suffix-sum over its
// ops' own contributions to the live population, so it is a segment tree read in O(1) at the root and a
// repair in O(log ops) per op the split actually touched — see `PressureIndex`.
//
// HOW THE INDEX STAYS CORRECT WHILE ONE SPLIT IS BEING MATERIALIZED: nothing in the function moves
// until the split is finished. `spillOneValue` / `rematerializeConstant` LOG their insertions
// (`SplitEdits`) rather than perform them, so every block's op layout — and therefore every use
// record's sequence number, which IS `block × SeqStride + position` — stays exactly as the mutator
// found it. One coordinate system, for the positions it computes and the sequence numbers it reads.
// The splices and the sequence repair are both REPLAYED AFTERWARDS from that log, and neither is ever
// applied under a mutator that is still partitioning uses around `peakSeq`. It is also what makes a
// split cost Θ(block + insertions) rather than Θ(insertions × block).
//
// ==========================================================================
// WHAT THE PER-SPLIT RE-ANALYSIS IS ALLOWED TO COST — `SplitScratch`
// ==========================================================================
//
// Everything the per-split path touches has to be proportional to the dirty region, so NOTHING on it
// may be sized by the function — including the buffers. SIX costs were superlinear and none of them
// was the traversal:
//
//  • `hallVerdictAt` HEAP-ALLOCATED its five working columns on every call, two of them PER LIVE VALUE
//    — and it is called at nearly every op of a pressured loop, because a value confined by a call is
//    confined at EVERY op it is live at. (`HallScratch`; see `HallCondition`'s header.)
//  • `betterPeak` SNAPSHOTTED the live row on every TIE, because `peakOutranks` replaces on an exact
//    tie — and in a loop whose ops all carry the same confined pressure, every op is a tie. That is an
//    allocation plus a `wordsPerRow` copy per OP, per split. (`PeakRank` + `fillLiveBeforeOp`.)
//  • `EffectivePools`, `defLiveAfter` and the sweep's working row are all sized by the VALUE SPACE,
//    which itself grows with every split — so re-initializing them per iteration is Θ(splits × values).
//    They are now GROWN (only the added entries are written) and MAINTAINED per value.
//  • `defLiveAfter`'s "no store anchor" sentinel used to be `valueCount + 1`, which changes whenever a
//    split mints an id — so the whole column had to be re-stamped. It is a constant
//    (`NoStoreAnchorPressure`), and nothing reads it: see there.
//  • `materializePeak` took a FRESH `wordsPerRow` row per analysis to carry the peak's live-before set,
//    and `refreshPeak` materializes a peak after EVERY split — O(splits × values) BYTES per function.
//    (`SplitScratch.peakLiveBefore`; `usesAtPeakOp`'s row is the same shape and shares `zeroRow`.)
//  • `markBlocksReachableFromPeak` allocated its BFS worklist INSIDE the walk, and the walk pushes a
//    block per IN-EDGE — an O(edges) allocation per un-memoized (peak block, def block) pair.
//    (`ReachCache.worklist`.)
//
// ⚠ THE LAST TWO HID BEHIND A LINEAR ALLOCATION COUNT, which is why they outlived the four above them:
// one allocation per split IS linear, and only the BYTES carry the value space it is multiplied by. So
// they are visible in `scale-test`'s byte column and in nothing else. MEASURED on the P1.7a-era corpus,
// 8 rungs: together they took `regalloc:splitting` bytes from ×192.1 over a ×128 ladder (per-rung ratio
// climbing monotonically 1.77 → 2.61, allocations linear at ×2.03) to ×111.7 (per-rung 2.04, flat) —
// the peak row 48.5 MB of the saving at rung 7, the worklist 104.2 MB. The old corpus could not see
// either: its splits were confined to small functions, so splits and values did not grow together.

// One memoized reachability ROW of `ReachCache` — one per distinct DEF BLOCK the peak's candidates
// have, so there are at most `numBlocks` of them and `numBlocks` is the "not walked yet" sentinel.
// It shares the CFG's dense numbering space but is NOT a block index: row 3 is "the walk for the
// third def block we met", not "block 3".
typealias ReachRow = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias ReachRowColumn = Array with ReachRow

// A flat index into `ReachCache.rows`, which lays its rows out ROW-MAJOR as
// `row * numBlocks + blockLocal`. A row BASE is one of these with `blockLocal = 0`.
typealias ReachSlot = int(0 to u64.max)

// The outcome of a lineage lookup: `source(id)` for a splitter-minted fresh value (the
// value it derives from), `none` for any value the splitter did not mint. A union rather
// than a sentinel id — a real ValueId 0 would be indistinguishable from "not found".
export union SplitLineageLookup
	none
	source(sourceId ValueId)
end 'SplitLineageLookup'

// SplitLineage — maps every fresh ValueId the splitter mints back to the ORIGINAL user value
// it derives from: a reload's `loadRegSlot` def -> the value it reloads, a rematerialized
// constant's re-emitted def -> the constant it re-emits. This is what lets the E5001
// diagnostic resolve a fresh id in a blocking set to a SOURCE origin (Rule 3): a fresh id has
// no `ValueOrigin` (it is minted after parsing) and is not a phi, so without this chase it
// would hit the Rule-3 "no origin" panic even though the value the author wrote — the spilled
// variable, the constant — is right there in source. The chase is transitive (a reload of a
// reload, a value spilled then its reload rematerialized), which the diagnostic follows to a
// value that DOES have a source origin (the original constant / spilled user value / a phi).
//
// Two dense scalar columns (fresh id, source id), pushed in lockstep, scanned linearly. This
// one genuinely IS cold and stays a scan: the ONLY caller is the E5001 diagnostic, which runs
// once per compile and then THROWS — it is not on any path a successful compile takes.
export type SplitLineage
	var freshIds as ValueIdArray
	var sourceIds as ValueIdArray

	export static function create() returns SplitLineage
		return Self{freshIds: ValueIdArray.create(), sourceIds: ValueIdArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Record that `freshId` (a reload or rematerialized def) derives from `sourceId`. The two
	// columns are only ever pushed together here, so they stay the same length by construction.
	export function record(freshId ValueId, sourceId ValueId)
		self.freshIds.push(freshId)
		self.sourceIds.push(sourceId)
	end 'record'

	// The value `freshId` derives from, or `none` when `freshId` is not splitter-minted (a
	// parser value / phi). First match is authoritative — each fresh id is recorded once.
	export function sourceOf(freshId ValueId) returns SplitLineageLookup
		for i in 0 upto self.freshIds.count() 'each'
			let fresh = try self.freshIds.get(i) otherwise panic("SplitLineage.sourceOf: freshIds.get OOB at {i}")
			if fresh == freshId 'match'
				return SplitLineageLookup.source(try self.sourceIds.get(i) otherwise panic("SplitLineage.sourceOf: sourceIds.get OOB at {i}"))
			end 'match'
		end 'each'
		return SplitLineageLookup.none
	end 'sourceOf'
end 'SplitLineage'

// The peak-pressure program point: the op with the maximum live-before population, its owning
// local block, and the live-before set there (the candidate spill pool). `found` is false when
// no op's live-before count exceeds the pool (nothing left to split).
export type PressurePeak
	export var found as bool
	export var pressure as RegCount
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var liveBefore as BitWordArray
	export var peakSeq as OpSeq
	// The peak op's `module.ops` index, resolved once when the peak is materialized. `peakSeq` cannot
	// stand in for it: sequence numbers are block-relative and SPARSE (`seqAt`), so there is no dense
	// seq -> op map to look one up in — and there should not be, because maintaining one is exactly
	// the whole-function rebuild an insert used to force. The ops array is append-only, so an op's
	// index never moves and this stays valid across the mutations the split then makes.
	export var peakOpIdx as OpIndex
	// Which of the two overflows this point is — the distinction the amended Rule 2 turns on.
	//
	//  • CLOBBER-ONLY (`true`): the live values fit the FULL pool, but more of them are CONFINED to
	//    some proper subset of it than that subset has registers (Hall's condition, `HallCondition`).
	//    They fit the machine — they simply cannot all sit in the registers left to them by the ops
	//    they are live across (a call clobbers the caller-saved set; `idiv` takes RAX/RDX). Relief is
	//    a FORCED store/reload bracket: a confined value cannot stay in a clobbered register, so
	//    memory is the ONLY placement — there is nothing to search. Permitted at ANY loop depth, and
	//    never E5001.
	//
	//  • FULL-POOL (`false`): pressure exceeds the full pool outright. This is genuine register
	//    demand — the loop's working set is larger than the machine — and only COLD splitting
	//    (a value idle across the region) relieves it. When nothing is cold, THAT is E5001.
	//
	// `analyzePressure` always prefers the higher-pressure peak, and a full-pool overflow is by
	// definition higher than any clobber-only one, so full-pool peaks are relieved FIRST. That
	// ordering is what lets `storeFitsPool` be sound at a clobber peak (see `chooseVictim`).
	export var clobberOnly as bool
	// The register set the overflow is measured against — Hall's WITNESS. `pool` itself for a
	// full-pool peak (every value is confined to the pool, so the overflow is plain pigeonhole);
	// a PROPER SUBSET for a clobber-only one — the 5 callee-saved registers when the confining op is
	// a call, `pool ∖ {rax,rdx}` at an `idiv`.
	//
	// Two things read it, and both would be wrong without it. `chooseVictim` relieves the peak by
	// spilling a value CONFINED to the witness — spilling one that could have taken a register
	// outside it frees nothing the confined values were able to use. And the E5001 deficit is
	// `pressure - popcount(witness)`: measured against any other pool it is not the number of values
	// the author must actually remove.
	export var witness as RegMask
	// The register FILE the witness names. A witness is a bag of bits and cannot say which file it
	// belongs to, but every question asked of it is per file: `chooseVictim` may only offer values of
	// THIS class, because a value of the other file can never relieve this overflow however it is
	// spilled. Carried rather than derived, because an EMPTY witness (a float live across a call is
	// forbidden every XMM, so Hall's tight set can be ∅) names no bits at all and would be
	// underivable.
	export var witnessClass as RegClass
	// Is the peak op ITSELF a fixed-register point (does it carry implicit clobbers)? The ranking
	// tie-break — see `peakOutranks`.
	export var atFixedRegisterOp as bool

	static function create(found bool, pressure RegCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, liveBefore BitWordArray, peakSeq OpSeq, peakOpIdx OpIndex, clobberOnly bool, witness RegMask, witnessClass RegClass, atFixedRegisterOp bool) returns PressurePeak
		return Self{found: found, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: blockLocal, liveBefore: liveBefore, peakSeq: peakSeq, peakOpIdx: peakOpIdx, clobberOnly: clobberOnly, witness: witness, witnessClass: witnessClass, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp}
	end 'create'
end 'PressurePeak'

// ⭐⭐ **THE REGISTER ALLOCATOR'S ONE REFUSAL, AND IT CARRIES NO PAYLOAD ON PURPOSE.**
//
// A hot-loop overflow the splitter cannot relieve is E5001 — a diagnostic that names a source span for
// every value blocking the loop. Building it needs `project.opRanges` and `project.valueOrigins`, and the
// allocator's core runs where there is no `Project`: on a pool worker, whose cone may not reach a module
// `var` (E3143) and which owns no compile. So the core throws THIS, and whoever holds a `Project` renders.
//
// One case, no fields: everything the renderer needs is the analysis state, which cannot be squeezed into
// an error value and is left in a `RegAllocRefusalSite` instead.
module enum RegAllocRefusal implements Error
	hotOverflow
end 'RegAllocRefusal'

// What a refused allocation leaves for the renderer: the liveness the refusal was measured against, the
// fresh-id lineage the diagnostic chases a blocking reload back through, and the peak itself.
//
// ⚠ **NONE OF IT CAN CROSS A MESSAGE BOUNDARY**, and it is not asked to. A worker's refusal comes home as
// the bare `RegAllocRefusal`; the main thread then runs the core AGAIN on the ORIGINAL function — which
// the parallel path never mutated, because what it handed the worker was a copy — and renders from the
// site that second run fills. A compile that reaches here is failing, so the second run is free.
module union RefusedAllocation
	none
	at(liveness LivenessResult, lineage SplitLineage, peak PressurePeak)
end 'RefusedAllocation'

// The one-slot mailbox a refusal writes into. A record rather than a return value because the refusal
// leaves by a `throw`, and Maxon has no way to carry a second value out of one.
module type RegAllocRefusalSite
	export var refused as RefusedAllocation

	export static function create() returns RegAllocRefusalSite
		return Self{refused: RefusedAllocation.none}
	end 'create'
end 'RegAllocRefusalSite'

// The peak's RANK and LOCATION — everything the sweep's argmax is actually over, and NOTHING it is
// not. It carries no live row, and that omission is the point.
//
// `peakOutranks` REPLACES on an exact tie (it is how the tie-break is implemented), so every op that
// merely MATCHES the running best becomes a new best. In a loop holding N accumulators across a call
// those values are confined at every op of the loop, so Hall reports the same `tight` everywhere and
// EVERY op of the loop ties — and the old code took a fresh heap row plus a `wordsPerRow` copy at each
// one, on every splitter iteration, to hold a snapshot all but the last of them would discard.
//
// The row is derived ONCE, for the winner, by `fillLiveBeforeOp` — from the same backward transfer
// function the sweep steps with, so it is bit-for-bit the row the snapshot would have taken.
type PeakRank
	export var found as bool
	export var pressure as RegCount
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	// The winner's FORWARD position within its block. `peakSeq` cannot stand in for it: that is a
	// function-GLOBAL number, and recovering a position from it would need the block's base sequence
	// carried back out of the sweep.
	export var forwardPos as BlockOpPos
	export var peakSeq as OpSeq
	export var clobberOnly as bool
	export var witness as RegMask
	// The register FILE the witness names. A witness is a bag of bits and cannot say which file it
	// belongs to, but every question asked of it is per file: `chooseVictim` may only offer values of
	// THIS class, because a value of the other file can never relieve this overflow however it is
	// spilled. Carried rather than derived, because an EMPTY witness (a float live across a call is
	// forbidden every XMM, so Hall's tight set can be ∅) names no bits at all and would be
	// underivable.
	export var witnessClass as RegClass
	export var atFixedRegisterOp as bool

	static function create(found bool, pressure RegCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, forwardPos BlockOpPos, peakSeq OpSeq, clobberOnly bool, witness RegMask, witnessClass RegClass, atFixedRegisterOp bool) returns PeakRank
		return Self{found: found, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: blockLocal, forwardPos: forwardPos, peakSeq: peakSeq, clobberOnly: clobberOnly, witness: witness, witnessClass: witnessClass, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp}
	end 'create'

	// ⚠ THE RUNNING BEST IS MUTATED, NOT RE-MINTED, and the reason is the same one `PeakTree` gives for
	// holding its leaves as flat columns one level up: A `PeakRank` IS A HEAP OBJECT. `peakOutranks`
	// replaces on an exact TIE, so on the shape that splits — a block where nearly every op overflows —
	// `betterPeak` won at a large fraction of the ops, and minting there is Θ(block) allocations per
	// block sweep, hence Θ(block × splits) per function. The three methods below are what let ONE object
	// carry the argmax across a whole block sweep, with exactly one `create` at the end of it.

	// Back to "nothing offered yet", which is what the sweep's `best` starts each block as and what an
	// unpublished tournament leaf reads back as.
	//
	// `witness` IS THE CALLER'S TO NAME AND NOTHING READS IT HERE: while `found` is false no ranking
	// looks at it (`rankOutranks` rejects an unfound candidate outright; `peakOutranks` takes its
	// `firstPeak` exit against an unfound incumbent), so the block sweep passes the pool it is measuring
	// against and the tournament passes the empty set its own empty rank has always carried — and the
	// two stay byte-identical to what each used to mint by hand.
	export function clear(atBlock CfgIndex, pool RegMask)
		self.found = false
		self.pressure = 0
		self.blockLocal = atBlock
		self.forwardPos = 0
		self.peakSeq = 0
		self.clobberOnly = false
		self.witness = pool
		self.witnessClass = RegClass.gpr
		self.atFixedRegisterOp = false
	end 'clear'

	// Become this candidate. The caller has already decided it outranks what is here (`betterPeak`).
	export function takeOver(demand RegCount, atBlock CfgIndex, atPos BlockOpPos, seq OpSeq, confined bool, tightSet RegMask, tightClass RegClass, atClobber bool)
		self.found = true
		self.pressure = demand
		self.blockLocal = atBlock
		self.forwardPos = atPos
		self.peakSeq = seq
		self.clobberOnly = confined
		self.witness = tightSet
		self.witnessClass = tightClass
		self.atFixedRegisterOp = atClobber
	end 'takeOver'

	// An INDEPENDENT copy, which a block sweep's result must be: its caller compares one block's rank
	// against the running best of the blocks before it, and if both were the one scratch object that
	// comparison would be an object against itself — an exact tie, which `peakOutranks` resolves in the
	// candidate's favour, so the fold would silently return the LAST block every time.
	export function snapshot() returns PeakRank
		return PeakRank.create(self.found, pressure: self.pressure, blockLocal: self.blockLocal, forwardPos: self.forwardPos, peakSeq: self.peakSeq, clobberOnly: self.clobberOnly, witness: self.witness, witnessClass: self.witnessClass, atFixedRegisterOp: self.atFixedRegisterOp)
	end 'snapshot'
end 'PeakRank'

// How a spill's store/reload placement is constrained — the amended Rule 2 in one type.
//
//  • `cold` — the classic shape: every inserted op must land at loop depth 0 (asserted by
//    `assertPlacementLegal`), so no spill code EVER enters a loop body. This is what relieves a
//    FULL-pool overflow, and it is free: the store runs once before the loop, the reload once
//    after it.
//
//  • `forced` — MEMORY IS THE ONLY HOME LEFT, so the spill is permitted at any loop depth and the
//    allocator makes no choice and searches nothing. ⚠ **THREE DIFFERENT THINGS MAKE IT THE ONLY HOME,
//    AND ONLY THE FIRST IS THE ABI'S** — each is stated at its own site, because collapsing them into
//    "the ABI forces it" would claim a justification two of them do not have:
//      · a CONFINED point — the value cannot survive in a register the ops it is live across clobber.
//        Rule 2 admits it because the code is CHEAP AGAINST THE OP IT BRACKETS: a load and a store
//        against a call that costs far more. Refusing it would send the author to hand-write the same
//        load and store (a value hoisted into an array is loaded and stored at each use) — strictly
//        worse code, for an error the compiler could have avoided emitting.
//      · the COLORER'S EVICTION REPAIR, where the register is not clobbered at all, merely TAKEN, and
//        what makes memory the only home is that the alternative is a compiler panic on a program that
//        fits the machine — see `SplitDriver.repairByEvictingOccupant`.
//      · a FULL-POOL peak in a function the COMPILER EMITTED, where the refusal (E5001) is an
//        instruction to an author who does not exist — see `SplitDriver.relievePressure`'s
//        `noAuthorToRefuse`.
//
//    PLACEMENT is not SCOPE. This says the spill code may land inside a loop; `SplitScope` says which
//    uses it serves. A value read only AFTER the clobber gets the store/reload bracket around it; one
//    read on BOTH sides of the clobber, and so live across it around the back edge, gets a load at
//    each use (`SplitScope.everyUse`) — still forced, still unsearched, just paid per use.
enum SpillPlacement
	cold
	forced
end 'SpillPlacement'

// WHICH of a victim's uses a split moves to memory — the second half of a relief decision, and the
// half the peak's PLACEMENT does not decide.
//
//  • `afterPeakUses` — the Belady split at the eviction point, and the shape every relief has when it
//    is available: the after-peak uses are rewritten to reloads, the before-peak uses keep the
//    ORIGINAL value in its original register. It leaves the value dead across the peak — which is the
//    entire point — PROVIDED no before-peak use is still reachable FROM the peak (`killsValueAtPeak`).
//
//  • `everyUse` — EVERY use is rewritten, before-peak ones included, so the original's only remaining
//    reader is its own store and it is dead from the def onward. This is what a value whose before-peak
//    use is reachable from the peak AROUND A BACK EDGE needs, and it is the only thing that works
//    there: a loop-invariant read both before and after a call inside the loop is live across that
//    call on the back edge no matter how its after-peak uses are rewritten, so the partial split
//    relieves NOTHING (`killsValueAtPeak` correctly refuses it). Six such values against the five
//    callee-saved registers fit the machine easily — the ABI simply denies them the nine caller-saved
//    ones — so refusing to relieve them is neither E5001 (the program fits) nor a panic: it is the
//    forced bracket, paid at every use instead of once. The value is loaded before each use and lives
//    in a register only there, which is exactly the code an author would hand-write for it.
//
// The cost difference is why `chooseVictim` prefers an `afterPeakUses` victim whenever one exists.
enum SplitScope
	afterPeakUses
	everyUse
end 'SplitScope'

// Does a split of this scope leave the BEFORE-peak uses reading the original value in its register?
function keepsOriginalBeforePeak(scope SplitScope) returns bool
	return match scope 's'
		afterPeakUses gives true
		everyUse gives false
	end 's'
end 'keepsOriginalBeforePeak'

// WHERE A SPLIT CUTS — the whole of what the MUTATION needs to know about the point it relieves, and
// nothing else.
//
// A `PressurePeak` is an ANALYSIS RESULT: a population, a Hall witness, a live-before row, a rank. A CUT
// is three facts — which block, which sequence number, and whether the spill code may land inside a
// loop. Separating them is what lets the COLORER's repair (`SplitDriver.repairAtExhaustion`) drive the
// same mutator: it holds a stuck VALUE and the CLOBBER op confining it, and it has run no pressure
// analysis at all. Handing `spillOneValue` a fabricated `PressurePeak` there would mean inventing a
// population, a witness and a live row that nothing measured — four lies to satisfy a parameter whose
// readers use three honest fields.
// ⚠ THE ONE IN FLIGHT IS MUTATED, NOT RE-MINTED, and the reason is `PeakRank`'s: A `SplitAnchor` IS A
// HEAP OBJECT, and there is exactly one cut in flight at a time. Minting it where it is USED would be an
// allocation per split on the pass the scale ladder watches — and two of them, because `chooseVictim`
// and the mutator each need it. `SplitScratch` holds the one, created once per function.
type SplitAnchor
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var seq as OpSeq
	export var placement as SpillPlacement

	// "No cut yet", which is what `SplitScratch.create` starts with and what no reader ever sees: every
	// path sets it through one of the two mutators below before anything reads it.
	static function create() returns SplitAnchor
		return Self{blockLocal: 0, seq: 0, placement: SpillPlacement.cold}
	end 'create'

	// Become the cut this PEAK names: its op, and the placement the DRIVER decided for it.
	//
	// The placement is passed rather than derived here because it is not a property of the peak alone:
	// `placementOf` is the rule for a peak in AUTHORED code, and a full-pool peak in a function the
	// compiler emitted overrides it (see `relievePressure`'s `noAuthorToRefuse`). Deriving it in here
	// would put that decision in two places, and the one in here cannot see whose code it is.
	export function takePeak(peak PressurePeak, cutPlacement SpillPlacement)
		self.blockLocal = peak.blockLocal
		self.seq = peak.peakSeq
		self.placement = cutPlacement
	end 'takePeak'

	// Become a FORCED cut at one op — the colorer's repair, which has no peak. `forced` rather than
	// derived: the value being cut is CONFINED (that is what made the colorer fail), and a confined
	// value's only home across the clobber is memory, so the placement is the ABI's and not a choice.
	export function takeForcedCutAt(cutBlock CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos)
		self.blockLocal = cutBlock
		self.seq = seqAt(cutBlock, pos: pos)
		self.placement = SpillPlacement.forced
	end 'takeForcedCutAt'
end 'SplitAnchor'

// A chosen victim + how to relieve it.
union SpillChoice
	// No value crossing the peak can be relieved. At a FULL-POOL peak that is the HOT overflow the
	// design exists to report (E5001, `raiseRegisterPressure`); at a CONFINED one it is a splitter
	// bug — see `noVictimAtPeak`.
	none
	// Rematerialize a constant (re-emit it before each rewritten use, no slot, no store).
	remat(valueId ValueId, scope SplitScope)
	// Spill a value, splitting its live range so it is dead across the peak: a store at its def
	// (skipped when an earlier split already wrote it to its slot) and a reload before each
	// rewritten use.
	spill(valueId ValueId, scope SplitScope)
end 'SpillChoice'

// The `defLiveAfter` entry of a value with NO def point at all — one this function neither defines by
// an op nor carries as a phi. `storeFitsPool` is its only reader, and reads it as "no register-free
// store anchor", so it need only EXCEED any pool.
//
// It is a CONSTANT, and it used to be `valueCount + 1`. The value space GROWS with every split, so a
// sentinel derived from it goes stale the moment an id is minted, and keeping it current means
// re-stamping the whole column — O(values) per split, which is exactly the term the incremental driver
// exists to remove.
//
// No decision moves. Only a value LIVE at the peak is ever asked, a live value has a use, and a used
// value has a def (Rule 1, SSA) — so what `storeFitsPool` reads is always a real def-point pressure and
// never this. The sentinel exists so that malformed IR fails the gate rather than passing it by
// accident, and any value above the pool size does that.
let NoStoreAnchorPressure = RegisterFileSize + 1

// The op INSERTIONS and REMOVALS one split made — RECORDED as it makes them, MATERIALIZED in one pass
// per block when it is done.
//
// The sequence repair is REPLAYED FROM THIS AFTERWARDS, rather than applied as the ops go in, and that
// is load-bearing. `spillOneValue` walks the victim's use thread while it records these edits,
// partitioning those uses around `peakSeq` — and a sequence number bumped underneath it could carry a
// use ACROSS the peak and flip which side of the split it lands on.
//
// THE SPLICES THEMSELVES ARE HELD BACK TOO, and that is what makes this the whole edit log rather than
// a repair log. Three separate Θ(block) costs used to be paid on EVERY op a split inserted: the search
// for its anchor op's position, the suffix shift `Array.insert` does, and the walk of the block's use
// thread that restates the sequence numbers past it. A split placing K ops into a block of M ops cost
// Θ(K × M) — invisible to `scale-test`, because none of the three ALLOCATES. Held back, the batch costs
// Θ(M + K): one backward pass over `opRefs` (`spliceOpRefs`) and one walk of the block's records.
//
// It also puts every position in ONE COORDINATE SYSTEM — the block's PRE-SPLIT layout — because nothing
// moves until `commit`. That is what lets a reload's anchor position come straight out of the use record
// that asked for it (`UseIndex.usePosAt`) instead of a fresh search for the anchor op.
//
// THE INSERTS MUST BE RECORDED IN ASCENDING (block, position) ORDER, and they are, by SSA. A spill
// anchors its store at the value's DEF, which dominates every use of it and therefore precedes every one
// of them — in RPO block order, and in position within a block they share. The reloads then follow the
// value's use thread, which is held ascending by sequence, hence ascending by (block, position). A remat
// re-emits along that same thread. `recordInsert` refuses a batch that is not ascending, because the
// backward pass would otherwise place an op silently in the wrong slot.
//
// THE REMOVAL is a scalar rather than a list entry: a split removes at most ONE op — a fully
// rematerialized constant's def, which nothing reads any more — and it is applied AFTER the splices,
// exactly where it was applied when it was applied by hand.
export type SplitEdits
	var blocks as CfgIndexArray
	var positions as BlockOpPosArray
	var opIdxs as OpIndexArray
	// The SLOT each spliced op was seated at, filled as the batch is materialized. It is what
	// `reindexSplitValues` re-reads them from: an op index is module-wide and a position moves under the
	// next splice, while a slot is the coordinate that does not move (see `PressureIndex`).
	var insertedSlots as DenseColumn
	var hasRemoval as bool
	var removalBlock as CfgIndex
	var removalPos as BlockOpPos
	// WHICH op the removal means, so `commitBlock` can prove it is dropping that one and not a
	// neighbour. The position alone cannot: it is stated in PRE-SPLIT coordinates and carried forward
	// by `splicesAtOrBefore`, so an off-by-one there would delete a LIVE op out of the block, silently.
	var removalOpIdx as OpIndex

	static function create() returns SplitEdits
		return Self{blocks: CfgIndexArray.create(), positions: BlockOpPosArray.create(), opIdxs: OpIndexArray.create(), insertedSlots: DenseColumn.create(), hasRemoval: false, removalBlock: 0, removalPos: 0, removalOpIdx: 0}
	end 'create'

	function clear()
		self.blocks.clear()
		self.positions.clear()
		self.opIdxs.clear()
		self.insertedSlots.clear()
		self.hasRemoval = false
	end 'clear'

	// Op `opIdx` is to be spliced into `blockLocal` at PRE-SPLIT forward position `pos`.
	export function recordInsert(blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos, opIdx OpIndex)
		let recorded = self.blocks.count()
		if recorded > 0 'notTheFirst'
			let lastBlock = try self.blocks.get(recorded - 1) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.recordInsert: blocks.get OOB at {recorded - 1}")
			let lastPos = try self.positions.get(recorded - 1) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.recordInsert: positions.get OOB at {recorded - 1}")
			if blockLocal < lastBlock or (blockLocal == lastBlock and pos < lastPos) 'outOfOrder'
				panic("SplitEdits.recordInsert: a splice at block {blockLocal} position {pos} follows one at block {lastBlock} position {lastPos} — one split's insertions must be recorded in ASCENDING (block, position) order, which SSA guarantees (the store anchors at the def, which dominates every use; the reloads follow the use thread, which is ascending by sequence). Materializing them takes one backward pass per block, so an out-of-order entry would land in the wrong slot")
			end 'outOfOrder'
		end 'notTheFirst'

		self.blocks.push(blockLocal)
		self.positions.push(pos)
		self.opIdxs.push(opIdx)
	end 'recordInsert'

	// Op `opIdx`, at PRE-SPLIT forward position `pos` of `blockLocal`, is to be removed (a fully
	// rematerialized constant's def).
	export function recordRemoval(blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos, opIdx OpIndex)
		if self.hasRemoval 'alreadyHaveOne'
			panic("SplitEdits.recordRemoval: a second removal (block {blockLocal}, position {pos}) — a split drops at most ONE op, the def of a constant every use of which it rematerialized, and the removal is a scalar because of it")
		end 'alreadyHaveOne'

		self.hasRemoval = true
		self.removalBlock = blockLocal
		self.removalPos = pos
		self.removalOpIdx = opIdx
	end 'recordRemoval'

	// Materialize every recorded edit: splice the pending ops into their blocks, drop the removed one,
	// repair the index's sequence numbers and op count, and register every block that took an edit as
	// DIRTY — those are the only blocks whose op layout can have changed.
	//
	// ONE PASS PER BLOCK, walking the recorded edits as runs of one block. Blocks are INDEPENDENT — a
	// splice into one moves nothing in any other, which is the whole reason sequence numbers are
	// block-relative — so the runs may be taken in any order, and the removal's block is picked up at
	// the end when it took no splice of its own.
	function commit(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, index UseIndex, dirty BlockSet, pressure PressureIndex, relayout PressureRelayoutScratch, opFacts OpVariantFacts, buf OperandList)
		var removalPending = self.hasRemoval
		var k = 0 as SpliceBatchIndex
		while k < self.blocks.count() 'eachBlockRun'
			let blockLocal = try self.blocks.get(k) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.commit: blocks.get OOB at {k}")
			var runEnd = k + 1
			while runEnd < self.blocks.count() and (try self.blocks.get(runEnd) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.commit: blocks.get OOB at {runEnd}")) == blockLocal 'sameBlock'
				runEnd = runEnd + 1
			end 'sameBlock'

			let withRemoval = removalPending and self.removalBlock == blockLocal
			self.commitBlock(module, topo: topo, index: index, dirty: dirty, pressure: pressure, relayout: relayout, opFacts: opFacts, buf: buf, blockLocal: blockLocal, from: k, to: runEnd, withRemoval: withRemoval)
			removalPending = removalPending and not withRemoval
			k = runEnd
		end 'eachBlockRun'

		if removalPending 'removalBlockTookNoSplice'
			self.commitBlock(module, topo: topo, index: index, dirty: dirty, pressure: pressure, relayout: relayout, opFacts: opFacts, buf: buf, blockLocal: self.removalBlock, from: 0, to: 0, withRemoval: true)
		end 'removalBlockTookNoSplice'
	end 'commit'

	// Materialize ONE block's edits: `[from, to)` of the recorded splices, plus the removal when it
	// belongs to this block.
	function commitBlock(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, index UseIndex, dirty BlockSet, pressure PressureIndex, relayout PressureRelayoutScratch, opFacts OpVariantFacts, buf OperandList, blockLocal CfgIndex, from SpliceBatchIndex, to SpliceBatchIndex, withRemoval bool)
		var block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: blockLocal)
		spliceOpRefs(block, positions: self.positions, opIdxs: self.opIdxs, from: from, to: to)
		index.addOps(((to - from) as OpSeq))

		// The POSITIONAL index takes the same ops, in the same ascending order, each at the position the
		// backward splice above just put it at: entry `j` of this block's run was recorded at a PRE-split
		// position and the `j` entries ahead of it each pushed it along by one. Seating them here rather
		// than after the whole batch is what keeps every later `slotOfPosition` in this loop reading the
		// layout that actually exists.
		for j in 0 upto to - from 'eachSplice'
			let entry = from + j
			let opIdx = try self.opIdxs.get(entry) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.commitBlock: opIdxs.get OOB at {entry}")
			let at = (try self.positions.get(entry) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.commitBlock: positions.get OOB at {entry}")) + j
			// The slots already recorded are handed down, because a re-space MOVES the ops they name — see
			// `PressureRelayoutScratch.applyMoves`, which is the repair that keeps them valid.
			self.insertedSlots.push(pressure.insertOp(module, topo: topo, scratch: relayout, opFacts: opFacts, blockLocal: blockLocal, pos: at, opIdx: opIdx, buf: buf, pending: self.insertedSlots))
		end 'eachSplice'

		if withRemoval 'dropTheDeadDef'
			// Stated in PRE-SPLIT coordinates like every other position here, so it is carried through the
			// splices that just went in ahead of it — landing exactly where it landed when the removal was
			// applied by hand, after the inserts.
			let at = self.removalPos + self.splicesAtOrBefore(self.removalPos, from: from, to: to)
			let standing = try block.opRefs.get(at) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.commitBlock: opRefs.get OOB at {at} of block {blockLocal}")
			// The carry-forward and `spliceOpRefs`' backward pass are two statements of ONE shift rule,
			// and this is the one place a disagreement between them would DELETE something. Removing a
			// live op is not a crash — the block simply stops computing a value someone reads.
			if standing != self.removalOpIdx 'wouldDropTheWrongOp'
				panic("SplitEdits.commitBlock: the removal meant op {self.removalOpIdx} at pre-split position {self.removalPos} of block {blockLocal}, but after {to - from} splice(s) that position carries forward to {at}, which holds op {standing} — the splice shift `spliceOpRefs` performs and the one `splicesAtOrBefore` predicts have diverged")
			end 'wouldDropTheWrongOp'

			// Its LEAF is vacated first, while `at` still names it: freeing it afterwards would resolve the
			// position against a block the op has already left.
			pressure.removeOp(blockLocal, slot: pressure.slotOfPosition(blockLocal, pos: at))
			_ = try block.opRefs.remove(at as ElementIndex) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.commitBlock: opRefs.remove OOB at {at} of block {blockLocal}")
			index.removeOp()
		end 'dropTheDeadDef'

		self.resequence(index, blockLocal: blockLocal, from: from, to: to, withRemoval: withRemoval)
		dirty.add(blockLocal)
	end 'commitBlock'

	// Restate the sequence number of every use record sited in `blockLocal`.
	//
	// A record's number IS `block × SeqStride + position`, so it moves by exactly the net shift its own
	// PRE-SPLIT position took: up one for every op spliced in at or before it, down one when the removed
	// op preceded it. It touches NO OTHER BLOCK, and it cannot disturb the ascending order a value's
	// thread is held in, because the shift is monotone in position — records that were ordered stay
	// ordered.
	//
	// It walks records that are DETACHED from their value too, exactly as the per-insertion shift it
	// replaces did: the block thread holds every record ever sited here (see `UseIndex`), and there is
	// no point repairing sequence numbers selectively.
	//
	// The block thread is UNORDERED, so each record's shift is LOOKED UP against this block's ascending
	// splice positions rather than accumulated along the walk.
	function resequence(index UseIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex, from SpliceBatchIndex, to SpliceBatchIndex, withRemoval bool)
		let records = index.blockUseCount(blockLocal)
		if records == 0 'noRecords'
			return
		end 'noRecords'

		var record = index.firstUseInBlockOf(blockLocal)
		for _ in 0 upto records 'eachRecord'
			let pos = index.usePosAt(record)
			var moved = pos + self.splicesAtOrBefore(pos, from: from, to: to)
			if withRemoval and pos > self.removalPos 'afterTheRemovedOp'
				moved = moved - 1
			end 'afterTheRemovedOp'

			if moved != pos 'shifted'
				index.setUseSeq(record, seq: seqAt(blockLocal, pos: moved))
			end 'shifted'
			record = index.nextUseInBlockOf(record)
		end 'eachRecord'
	end 'resequence'

	// The slots this batch's spliced ops were seated at, appended to `out`.
	//
	// ⚠ A RE-SPACE INVALIDATES THEM, and one CAN happen between the seat and this read: `insertOp`
	// re-spaces whenever the gap it wants is used up, which is mid-batch as soon as a batch puts a second
	// op into one gap. So the column is handed to every re-space and REPAIRED there
	// (`PressureRelayoutScratch.applyMoves`). It used to say instead that a re-space "re-derived" them,
	// which nothing did — see that function for what the missing repair actually cost.
	export function appendInsertedSlots(out DenseColumn)
		out.append(self.insertedSlots)
	end 'appendInsertedSlots'

	// How many of the splices in `[from, to)` — ascending by position — land at or before block position
	// `pos`, and so shift whatever sat there. Binary search: a block can take as many splices as it has
	// ops, and a linear count per record would put back the Θ(records × splices) this batching removes.
	function splicesAtOrBefore(pos BlockOpPos, from SpliceBatchIndex, to SpliceBatchIndex) returns BlockOpPos
		var lo = from
		var hi = to
		while lo < hi 'narrow'
			let mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2
			let at = try self.positions.get(mid) otherwise panic("SplitEdits.splicesAtOrBefore: positions.get OOB at {mid}")
			if at <= pos 'landsAtOrBefore'
				lo = mid + 1
			end 'landsAtOrBefore' else 'landsAfter'
				hi = mid
			end 'landsAfter'
		end 'narrow'
		return lo - from
	end 'splicesAtOrBefore'
end 'SplitEdits'

// The per-block peak ranks, and the TOURNAMENT that turns them into the function's peak.
//
// A split changes the pressure profile of only the blocks it dirtied — but folding a per-block max over
// EVERY block on every split would merely MOVE the quadratic, because the block count grows with the
// function. This is a segment tree: one leaf per block, each internal node the winner of its two
// children. A dirtied block costs O(log blocks) to re-publish, and the function's peak is the root, in
// O(1).
//
// THE COMBINE IS `peakOutranks`, WITH THE RIGHT CHILD AS THE CANDIDATE — so the right child wins an
// exact tie, and the root is the RIGHTMOST maximal rank. That reproduces the full sweep's answer
// exactly: the sweep visits blocks in ASCENDING index and `peakOutranks` REPLACES on a tie, so its
// winner is the last maximal rank it saw. In a pressured loop nearly every op is an exact tie (a
// confined value is confined at every op it is live at), so a peak-finder that resolved ties any other
// way would pick a different op, hence a different victim, hence different code. Anything that
// re-derives this ranking must reproduce the ORDER — see `peakOutranks`.
type PeakTree
	// Doubles as the "no peak in this subtree" marker for `winner`: no block index can equal it.
	var numBlocks as CfgIndex
	// One leaf per block, as FLAT COLUMNS. A `PeakRank` object per block would be a heap allocation per
	// block, on every function the allocator sees — and almost none of them ever split.
	var found as BoolArray
	var pressure as RegCountColumn
	var forwardPos as BlockOpPosArray
	var peakSeq as OpSeqArray
	var clobberOnly as BoolArray
	var witness as RegMaskColumn
	var witnessClass as ValueClassColumn
	var atFixedRegisterOp as BoolArray
	// `winner[node]` — the BLOCK whose rank wins that subtree, or `numBlocks` for "no peak in it". Node
	// 1 is the root; leaf `b` is at `leafBase + b`.
	var winner as CfgIndexArray
	// The two ranks ONE combine compares, refilled from the columns above at every combine and never
	// re-minted. `betterBlock` used to materialize a fresh `PeakRank` for each side — two 72-byte heap
	// objects to read four fields — and `promote` runs O(log blocks) combines for every dirty block of
	// every split, so that was a per-split allocation term proportional to the FUNCTION rather than to
	// what the split changed. Two objects per tree instead, for the same reason the leaves are columns.
	var leftRank as PeakRank
	var rightRank as PeakRank
	// A power of two >= numBlocks, so the tree is complete and a node's children are `2n` and `2n+1`.
	var leafBase as ColumnLength

	static function create() returns PeakTree
		return Self{numBlocks: 0, found: BoolArray.create(), pressure: RegCountColumn.create(), forwardPos: BlockOpPosArray.create(), peakSeq: OpSeqArray.create(), clobberOnly: BoolArray.create(), witness: RegMaskColumn.create(), witnessClass: ValueClassColumn.create(), atFixedRegisterOp: BoolArray.create(), winner: CfgIndexArray.create(), leftRank: emptyRank(), rightRank: emptyRank(), leafBase: 1}
	end 'create'

	// Size the tree for a function, with every leaf empty. The full sweep then sets each leaf and calls
	// `build`.
	export function reset(blockCount CfgIndex)
		self.numBlocks = blockCount
		var base = 1 as ColumnLength
		while base < blockCount as ColumnLength 'toPowerOfTwo'
			base = base + base
		end 'toPowerOfTwo'
		self.leafBase = base

		self.found.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: false)
		self.pressure.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.forwardPos.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.peakSeq.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.clobberOnly.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: false)
		self.witness.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.witnessClass.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: RegClass.gpr)
		self.atFixedRegisterOp.refill(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: false)
		// Every node — including the PADDING leaves past `blockCount` — starts empty.
		self.winner.refill(((base + base) as ElementIndex), value: blockCount)
	end 'reset'

	// Record one block's rank at its leaf. `build` (a whole-function seed) or `promote` (an incremental
	// repair) is what publishes it to the root.
	export function setLeaf(blockLocal CfgIndex, rank PeakRank)
		try self.found.set(blockLocal, value: rank.found) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: found.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.pressure.set(blockLocal, value: rank.pressure) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: pressure.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.forwardPos.set(blockLocal, value: rank.forwardPos) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: forwardPos.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.peakSeq.set(blockLocal, value: rank.peakSeq) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: peakSeq.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.clobberOnly.set(blockLocal, value: rank.clobberOnly) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: clobberOnly.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.witness.set(blockLocal, value: rank.witness) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: witness.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.witnessClass.set(blockLocal, value: rank.witnessClass) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: witnessClass.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.atFixedRegisterOp.set(blockLocal, value: rank.atFixedRegisterOp) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: atFixedRegisterOp.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		try self.winner.set(self.leafBase + (blockLocal as ColumnLength), value: blockLocal if rank.found else self.numBlocks) otherwise panic("PeakTree.setLeaf: winner.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
	end 'setLeaf'

	// Build every internal node from the leaves, in DESCENDING node order so a node's children are
	// always already combined. O(blocks), once per function.
	export function build()
		for ri in 0 upto self.leafBase - 1 'eachInternal'
			self.combineAt(self.leafBase - 1 - ri)
		end 'eachInternal'
	end 'build'

	// Re-publish ONE block's leaf up to the root — O(log blocks), which is what keeps a split's cost
	// proportional to what it dirtied.
	export function promote(blockLocal CfgIndex)
		var node = (self.leafBase + (blockLocal as ColumnLength)) / 2
		while node >= 1 'upToRoot'
			self.combineAt(node)
			if node == 1 'atRoot'
				return
			end 'atRoot'
			node = node / 2
		end 'upToRoot'
	end 'promote'

	function combineAt(node ColumnLength)
		let left = try self.winner.get(node + node) otherwise panic("PeakTree.combineAt: winner.get OOB at node {node}")
		let right = try self.winner.get(node + node + 1) otherwise panic("PeakTree.combineAt: winner.get OOB at node {node}")
		try self.winner.set(node, value: self.betterBlock(left, right: right)) otherwise panic("PeakTree.combineAt: winner.set OOB at node {node}")
	end 'combineAt'

	// The winner of two subtrees. `right` holds the HIGHER block indices, so it is the CANDIDATE and
	// `left` the incumbent — and `rankOutranks` is TRUE on an exact tie, which is how the full sweep
	// resolves one (the last block offered wins).
	function betterBlock(left CfgIndex, right CfgIndex) returns CfgIndex
		if right == self.numBlocks 'rightEmpty'
			return left
		end 'rightEmpty'
		if left == self.numBlocks 'leftEmpty'
			return right
		end 'leftEmpty'

		self.fillRank(right, into: self.rightRank)
		self.fillRank(left, into: self.leftRank)
		if rankOutranks(self.rightRank, best: self.leftRank) 'rightWins'
			return right
		end 'rightWins'
		return left
	end 'betterBlock'

	// One leaf's columns, gathered into `into`. THE one place a leaf is read back, so a combine (which
	// fills a reused slot) and the root query (which fills a fresh object) cannot disagree about what a
	// leaf says. A leaf nothing has been published to yet is a real state, and it comes back EMPTY.
	function fillRank(blockLocal CfgIndex, into PeakRank)
		into.clear(blockLocal, pool: 0)
		if not (try self.found.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: found.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")) 'noPeakInThisBlock'
			return
		end 'noPeakInThisBlock'

		let demand = try self.pressure.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: pressure.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		let pos = try self.forwardPos.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: forwardPos.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		let seq = try self.peakSeq.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: peakSeq.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		let confined = try self.clobberOnly.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: clobberOnly.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		let tightSet = try self.witness.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: witness.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		let tightClass = try self.witnessClass.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: witnessClass.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		let atClobber = try self.atFixedRegisterOp.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillRank: atFixedRegisterOp.get OOB for block {blockLocal}")
		into.takeOver(demand, atBlock: blockLocal, atPos: pos, seq: seq, confined: confined, tightSet: tightSet, tightClass: tightClass, atClobber: atClobber)
	end 'fillRank'

	// The function's peak — the root of the tournament.
	//
	// A FRESH object, and NOT because it "outlives the next combine" — that reason was stated here and
	// is false. What it becomes is a `PressurePeak`, which `materializePeak` fills by COPYING these
	// scalars out, so nothing holds this rank past the call it is handed to. The reason is the other
	// way round: this is the ONE rank of the three that leaves the tree, so a caller may NAME it, and
	// `refreshPeak` does — across a loop that re-sweeps and PROMOTES every block. Handing back
	// `leftRank` or `rightRank` would give that binding a lifetime of "until the next combine", which
	// nothing here states and nothing checks, and the first reader to move a use below that loop would
	// read a rank belonging to some other block with no diagnostic anywhere. One allocation per
	// analysis is the price of a value with no expiry date on it.
	export function best() returns PeakRank
		var out = emptyRank()
		self.fillBest(out)
		return out
	end 'best'

	// The root, into a rank the CALLER owns — for the one caller that already has an empty one to
	// spend (`analyzePressure`'s fold seed, which the tournament path never uses). It is the same read
	// `best` does, so the two cannot come to disagree about what an empty tree answers.
	export function fillBest(into PeakRank)
		let champion = try self.winner.get(1) otherwise panic("PeakTree.fillBest: the tree has no root — `reset` must run before any query")
		if champion == self.numBlocks 'noPeak'
			into.clear(0, pool: 0)
			return
		end 'noPeak'
		self.fillRank(champion, into: into)
	end 'fillBest'
end 'PeakTree'

// A rank offering nothing — what a tournament slot holds before anything is published to it, and the
// state `PeakRank.clear` returns one to.
function emptyRank() returns PeakRank
	return PeakRank.create(false, pressure: 0, blockLocal: 0, forwardPos: 0, peakSeq: 0, clobberOnly: false, witness: 0, witnessClass: RegClass.gpr, atFixedRegisterOp: false)
end 'emptyRank'

// ==========================================================================
// THE POSITIONAL PRESSURE INDEX — the FULL-POOL peak as a STRUCTURE, not a sweep
// ==========================================================================
//
// `PeakTree` one level up removed the per-split fold over every BLOCK. This removes the per-split walk
// over every OP OF A DIRTY BLOCK, which is the term that actually bites: on a function that is ONE
// basic block the dirty region IS the whole function, so block-granular incrementality buys nothing and
// Θ(splits) × Θ(ops) is a genuine quadratic. MEASURED on `tests/ladders/genwidelive.sh <N> sum`
// (Θ(N) values live at once in one block, hence Θ(N) splits over a Θ(N) block): the four
// O(block)-per-split walks were 91% of the whole compile, and `analyzeBlockPressure`'s own share grew
// ×3.18 then ×3.91 over a DOUBLING ladder.
//
// WHAT IT HOLDS. The backward pressure sweep is a running population: `L(p) = L(p+1) + e(p)`, where
// `e(p)` is what `stepRowBackwardOverOperands` adds and removes at op `p` and `L(opCount) = liveOut`.
// So the live-before population of EVERY op is a SUFFIX SUM of one per-op number, and the block's peak
// is `max over p of (L(p) + transient(p))` — a max-suffix-sum, which a segment tree answers in O(1) at
// the root and repairs in O(log) per changed op.
//
// ⭐ WHY PER-OP AND NOT PER-VALUE-INTERVAL, WHICH IS THE OBVIOUS SHAPE. A value's live range is an
// INTERVAL, so "pressure" reads as a stabbing-count and the obvious structure is range-add / range-max.
// It is the wrong one here, and the reason is what a SPLIT changes: it cuts ONE value's range over a
// stretch that is Θ(the function) wide, so the per-value form needs the interval's ENDPOINTS in every
// block of that range — including the PASS-THROUGH blocks, where the value is neither defined nor used
// — and each endpoint has to be re-derived from the live sets it is in the middle of being edited out
// of. The per-op form needs nothing of the kind: `e(p)` is a function of op `p`'s OWN OPERANDS and
// their live-after status, so it can only move at an op that NAMES the victim or one of the ids the
// split minted. That set is O(uses of one value), never O(blocks in its range).
//
// ⭐⭐ AND `e(p)` IS SEPARABLE PER VALUE, which is what makes the repair a DELTA rather than a
// recomputation. Reading `stepRowBackwardOverOperands` (defs removed if live, then uses added if
// absent), each distinct virtual operand value `w` of op `p` contributes exactly
//
//     contrib(w, p) = (w is a USE operand of p) - (w is live AFTER p)
//
// — one line covering all three shapes (def-only, use-only, and the read-modify-write value that is
// both, which the def pass removes and the use pass re-adds). Everything else at that op cancels, so a
// split's repair only ever has to state what the victim and the fresh ids contribute, before and after.
//
// SLOTS, NOT POSITIONS, and this is the choice PLAN.md left open. An op's block POSITION shifts when a
// split splices an op in ahead of it — which is precisely what a split does — so a structure keyed by
// position would have to move every leaf after the insert. A SLOT is gap-labelled (`SlotGap` free slots
// per op, the same idiom `seqAt` uses between blocks with `SeqStride`) and therefore STABLE: a spliced
// op takes a free slot BETWEEN its neighbours and no existing leaf moves. Positions are recovered from
// the tree's own occupancy count (`positionOfSlot` / `slotOfPosition`), so there is exactly one
// statement of the order. The alternative PLAN.md named — a lazily applied cumulative shift, as
// `SplitEdits.resequence` uses — REPAIRS the coordinates of records that already exist and has nothing
// to say about where a NEW op goes, which is the whole question a splice asks.
//
// A block's slots are a POWER-OF-TWO run ALIGNED to its own length, so the block's range is exactly one
// node of the tree: its peak and its live-out population are single node reads rather than range walks.
// The LAST slot of a block's run is its EXIT leaf — not an op, never a peak candidate, carrying the
// block's live-out population as its `e`, so that a suffix sum from any op IS that op's live-before
// count with no separate live-out term to add (and to keep current).

// A slot in the positional pressure index — the STABLE coordinate an op keeps for as long as it is in
// the function. Unlike a block POSITION it does not move when an op is spliced in ahead of it.
export typealias PressureSlot = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias PressureSlotColumn = Array with PressureSlot

// A per-op contribution to the BACKWARD-running live population — SIGNED, because an op that defines a
// value that outlives it LOWERS the population as the sweep steps back over it.
typealias PressureDelta = int(i64.min to i64.max)
typealias PressureDeltaColumn = Array with PressureDelta

// How many slots each op is given room in. A spliced op takes a FREE slot strictly between its
// neighbours', so `SlotGap - 1` ops can land in one gap before the space between two neighbours is
// used up and the whole layout is re-spaced (`PressureIndex.relayout`). It is a power of two so a
// block's run stays one.
let SlotGap = 4 as PressureSlot

// The smallest stride a re-space may leave between neighbouring ops. `freeSlotAt` takes the MIDPOINT of
// two neighbours, so two slots apart is exactly the point at which one free slot separates them and a
// further splice has somewhere to go; anything less is a range that cannot absorb the splice at all.
let MinRespaceStride = 2 as PressureSlot

// The distance between neighbouring ops when `count` of them are spread EVENLY over `len` slots — what
// a RE-SPACE places by, as against `evenSlot`, which places by a fixed gap into a run the layout has
// just sized for exactly that. A run of `count` ops has `count + 1` gaps, one ahead of each op and one
// after the last, so the divisor cannot be zero.
//
// BOTH ENDS OF THE RANGE STAY FREE by construction: op 0 lands a whole stride in, and op `count - 1`
// lands at most `len - stride` in. That is precisely what `freeSlotAt` needs at each end of a block — a
// free slot ahead of the first op for a position-0 splice, and the exit leaf clear of the last.
function spreadStride(len PressureSlot, count BlockOpPos) returns PressureSlot
	let gaps = (count + 1) as PressureSlot
	let stride = try (len / gaps) otherwise panic("spreadStride: a run of {count} op(s) was said to have {gaps} gaps")
	return stride
end 'spreadStride'

// `best` for a subtree holding no op at all — it must lose every comparison against a real op and stay
// negative after the O(log slots) `sum` additions the combines fold into it, so it sits far below any
// pressure a function can reach and far above the delta range's floor.
let NoOpInSubtree = -1152921504606846976 as PressureDelta

// The winner of one block's max-suffix-sum: the peak's SLOT and the value there. `found` is false for a
// block with no ops at all (nothing to rank).
type BlockPeakSlot
	export var found as bool
	export var slot as PressureSlot
	export var pressure as PressureDelta

	static function create(found bool, slot PressureSlot, pressure PressureDelta) returns BlockPeakSlot
		return Self{found: found, slot: slot, pressure: pressure}
	end 'create'
end 'BlockPeakSlot'

// Does the max suffix of a node START IN ITS LEFT child? `left` covers the EARLIER slots, so a suffix
// opening there runs through the whole of `right` — which is the `rightSum +` term.
//
// ⚠ THE ONE STATEMENT OF THE TIE, AND IT HAS TWO CALLERS THAT MAY NOT DIFFER. `combine` folds it into
// `nodeBest`, which is the PRESSURE the peak-finder reads; `blockPeak`'s descent re-asks it at every
// level to recover the SLOT that pressure sits at. Written twice — which it was — a later edit to one
// would hand back a peak whose slot is not where its own value is: a different victim, no crash, no
// failing assertion, and nothing outside `VerifyIncrementalSplit` to notice.
//
// TIE GOES LEFT — the EARLIER op — because the sweep this replaces visits a block's ops in DECREASING
// position and `peakOutranks` replaces on an exact tie, so its winner is the earliest op of the tying
// run. Reproducing the ORDER is the whole contract (see `peakOutranks`).
//
// It takes the two children's AGGREGATES rather than their node indices so that `combine`, which has
// already read all three for its own sum, pays no extra column read to ask it.
function suffixBestIsInLeft(leftBest PressureDelta, rightSum PressureDelta, rightBest PressureDelta) returns bool
	return rightSum + leftBest >= rightBest
end 'suffixBestIsInLeft'

export type PressureIndex
	// A power of two: leaf `s` is node `slotCount + s`, node 1 is the root, node `n`'s children are `2n`
	// and `2n + 1`.
	var slotCount as ColumnLength
	// Per block: the first slot of its aligned run, the run's length, and the node that covers the run
	// EXACTLY. All three are derived from one layout pass so no reader re-derives the arithmetic.
	var blockFirst as PressureSlotColumn
	var blockLen as PressureSlotColumn
	var blockNode as ColumnLengthColumn
	// LEAVES. `holdsOp` is what makes a slot a POSITION and a peak candidate; the exit leaf and every
	// free slot carry `false`. `opAt` is meaningful only under it.
	var holdsOp as BoolArray
	var opAt as OpIndexArray
	var leafDelta as PressureDeltaColumn
	var leafTransient as PressureDeltaColumn
	// NODES. `nodeOps` is the occupancy count the position <-> slot mapping descends on; `nodeSum` and
	// `nodeBest` are the suffix-sum aggregate.
	var nodeOps as BlockOpPosArray
	var nodeSum as PressureDeltaColumn
	var nodeBest as PressureDeltaColumn
	// The OR of every op's clobber mask in the subtree — the second aggregate over the same slots, and
	// the one `forbidden` is rebuilt from. A value live across a stretch of a block must avoid every
	// register any op in that stretch writes, which is a RANGE OR over exactly this, so the mask a split
	// has to restate costs O(log ops) per block of the range instead of a walk of it.
	//
	// ⚠ IT IS STATIC, and that is what makes one column enough: every op a split INSERTS carries an empty
	// clobber (a `storeSlotReg`, a `loadRegSlot` and a constant materialization each have `implicitDefs`
	// of 0 and no physical def operand — the same fact `sweepBlockPressure`'s idempotence rests on), so a
	// splice never changes any range's answer. It is set once, where the op is seated.
	var nodeClobber as RegMaskColumn
	// Where each value is DEFINED, as a slot. `UseIndex` records a value's def OP and BLOCK, and neither
	// answers "which leaf is that?": a leaf is addressed by slot, an op index is module-wide (a column
	// over it would be Theta(module ops) per function — the shape `OpVariantFacts` refuses), and a
	// POSITION is exactly the coordinate a splice moves. So the slot is recorded where it is known for
	// free: at the one place an op is seated. `storeFitsPool` is what reads it.
	var defSlot as PressureSlotColumn

	export static function create() returns PressureIndex
		return Self{slotCount: 1, blockFirst: PressureSlotColumn.create(), blockLen: PressureSlotColumn.create(), blockNode: ColumnLengthColumn.create(), holdsOp: BoolArray.create(), opAt: OpIndexArray.create(), leafDelta: PressureDeltaColumn.create(), leafTransient: PressureDeltaColumn.create(), nodeOps: BlockOpPosArray.create(), nodeSum: PressureDeltaColumn.create(), nodeBest: PressureDeltaColumn.create(), nodeClobber: RegMaskColumn.create(), defSlot: PressureSlotColumn.create()}
	end 'create'

	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// LAYOUT
	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------

	// Give every block a run long enough for its ops, their gaps, the free slot every insertion at
	// position 0 needs AHEAD of the first op, and the exit leaf — rounded up to a power of two and
	// ALIGNED to its own length, which is what makes the run exactly one node of the tree. Every leaf
	// comes back EMPTY; the caller seats the ops.
	//
	// THE ONE LAYOUT, taking the per-block op counts as data: the first build reads them off the IR and
	// `relayout` reads them off the index's own occupancy, and neither may lay a run out differently
	// from the other — a second copy of this arithmetic would put an op at a slot the queries do not
	// expect it at, which is a wrong peak and no crash.
	function placeRuns(topo FuncTopology, opCounts DenseColumn)
		self.blockFirst.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.blockLen.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.blockNode.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: 0)

		var next = 0 as PressureSlot
		for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
			let ops = try opCounts.get(b) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: opCounts.get OOB for block {b}")
			let run = roundUpToPowerOfTwo((((ops + 1) * SlotGap + 1) as PressureSlot))
			let first = roundUpToRunBoundary(next, runLength: run)
			try self.blockFirst.set(b, value: first) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: blockFirst.set OOB for block {b}")
			try self.blockLen.set(b, value: run) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: blockLen.set OOB for block {b}")
			next = first + run
		end 'eachBlock'

		self.slotCount = roundUpToPowerOfTwo(next) as ColumnLength
		for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachNode'
			// The node covering `[first, first + len)`: the leaves start at `slotCount`, so dividing the
			// leaf index by the run length climbs exactly `log2(len)` levels.
			let first = try self.blockFirst.get(b) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: blockFirst.get OOB for block {b}")
			let run = try self.blockLen.get(b) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: blockLen.get OOB for block {b}")
			// ⚠ The divide needs a `try` because the RANGE does not survive the column: `run` was written a
			// line above as `roundUpToPowerOfTwo(…)`, which is 1 or more, but `blockLen` is a `PressureSlotColumn`
			// (`Array with PressureSlot`, `int(0 to u64.max)`) and a read out of it carries the ELEMENT's range,
			// not the writer's. So `divisorProof` sees a divisor that may be zero and is right to.
			let node = try (self.leafNodeOf(first) / (run as ColumnLength)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: block {b}'s run length is a power of two and so never 0 — it was written from roundUpToPowerOfTwo one loop above")
			try self.blockNode.set(b, value: node) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.placeRuns: blockNode.set OOB for block {b}")
		end 'eachNode'

		self.holdsOp.refill(self.slotCount as ElementIndex, value: false)
		self.opAt.refill(self.slotCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.leafDelta.refill(((self.slotCount * RegClassCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		self.leafTransient.refill(((self.slotCount * RegClassCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		self.nodeOps.refill(((self.slotCount + self.slotCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		self.nodeSum.refill((((self.slotCount + self.slotCount) * RegClassCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		self.nodeBest.refill((((self.slotCount + self.slotCount) * RegClassCount) as ElementIndex), value: NoOpInSubtree)
		self.nodeClobber.refill(((self.slotCount + self.slotCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
	end 'placeRuns'

	// Size the index for a function, from the IR's own op counts.
	function layOut(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, opCounts DenseColumn)
		opCounts.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
			let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: b)
			try opCounts.set(b, value: blockOpCount(block)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.layOut: opCounts.set OOB for block {b}")
		end 'eachBlock'
		self.placeRuns(topo, opCounts: opCounts)
	end 'layOut'

	// The slot an op at forward position `pos` of `blockLocal` occupies in a FRESHLY laid-out run. The
	// first op sits one gap IN, so an insertion ahead of it has somewhere to go — `freeSlotAt` treats the
	// run's first slot as the lower bound and never seats an op there.
	function evenSlot(blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos) returns PressureSlot
		return self.firstSlotOf(blockLocal) + ((pos + 1) as PressureSlot) * SlotGap
	end 'evenSlot'

	// The tree node that is slot `slot`'s leaf: the leaves start at `slotCount`.
	function leafNodeOf(slot PressureSlot) returns ColumnLength
		return self.slotCount + (slot as ColumnLength)
	end 'leafNodeOf'

	function slotOfLeafNode(node ColumnLength) returns PressureSlot
		return (node - self.slotCount) as PressureSlot
	end 'slotOfLeafNode'

	export function firstSlotOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns PressureSlot
		return try self.blockFirst.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.firstSlotOf: block {blockLocal} out of range")
	end 'firstSlotOf'

	// The block's EXIT leaf — the last slot of its run. It is not an op: it carries the block's live-out
	// population so that a suffix sum from any op IS that op's live-before count.
	export function exitSlotOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns PressureSlot
		return self.firstSlotOf(blockLocal) + self.runLengthOf(blockLocal) - 1
	end 'exitSlotOf'

	function nodeOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns ColumnLength
		return try self.blockNode.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.nodeOf: block {blockLocal} out of range")
	end 'nodeOf'

	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// LEAVES
	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------

	// Seat `opIdx` at `slot`: record its CLOBBER mask, and record the slot as the DEF slot of every value
	// the op defines. Both come out of one `opClobberMask`, which fills `buf` with the op's operands as
	// its side effect — so the seat is O(operands) and the two facts are read from one extraction.
	//
	// The caller publishes the leaf's population numbers and the change with `promote`.
	function seatOp(slot PressureSlot, op TargetOp, opIdx OpIndex, opFacts OpVariantFacts, buf OperandList)
		try self.holdsOp.set(slot, value: true) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.seatOp: holdsOp.set OOB at slot {slot}")
		try self.opAt.set(slot, value: opIdx) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.seatOp: opAt.set OOB at slot {slot}")
		try self.nodeClobber.set(self.leafNodeOf(slot), value: opClobberMask(op, buf: buf, opFacts: opFacts)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.seatOp: nodeClobber.set OOB at slot {slot}")
		for o in buf 'eachOperand'
			if operandIsDef(o) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'virtualDef'
				try self.defSlot.set(operandValueId(o), value: slot) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.seatOp: value {operandValueId(o)} is defined at slot {slot} but is outside the index's value space")
			end 'virtualDef'
		end 'eachOperand'
	end 'seatOp'

	// The slot of the op that DEFINES `valueId`. Only asked of a value `UseIndex.defKindOf` has already
	// said has an op def, so an unwritten entry is unreachable rather than a sentinel to test.
	export function defSlotOf(valueId ValueId) returns PressureSlot
		return try self.defSlot.get(valueId) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.defSlotOf: value {valueId} is outside the index's value space")
	end 'defSlotOf'

	// Follow the value space the split minted ids into. The added entries are never read before an op
	// seats them: a fresh id is DEFINED by an op the same split inserts.
	export function growValueSpace(newValueCount ValueId)
		self.defSlot.growFilled(newValueCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
	end 'growValueSpace'

	// Does `slot` hold an op at all? The one question a caller holding a REMEMBERED slot has to ask: a
	// split can drop the op that was there (a fully rematerialized constant's def), and the slot then
	// names nothing.
	export function slotHoldsOp(slot PressureSlot) returns bool
		return try self.holdsOp.get(slot) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.slotHoldsOp: holdsOp.get OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'slotHoldsOp'

	export function opAtSlot(slot PressureSlot) returns OpIndex
		if not (try self.holdsOp.get(slot) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.opAtSlot: holdsOp.get OOB at slot {slot}")) 'notAnOp'
			panic("PressureIndex.opAtSlot: slot {slot} holds no op — only a slot the layout seated one in names one")
		end 'notAnOp'
		return try self.opAt.get(slot) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.opAtSlot: opAt.get OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'opAtSlot'

	export function deltaAt(slot PressureSlot, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return try self.leafDelta.get(classRowIndex(slot as CfgIndex, class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.deltaAt: leafDelta.get OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'deltaAt'

	export function transientAt(slot PressureSlot, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return try self.leafTransient.get(classRowIndex(slot as CfgIndex, class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.transientAt: leafTransient.get OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'transientAt'

	function setDelta(slot PressureSlot, class RegClass, value PressureDelta)
		try self.leafDelta.set(classRowIndex(slot as CfgIndex, class: class), value: value) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.setDelta: leafDelta.set OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'setDelta'

	function setTransient(slot PressureSlot, class RegClass, value PressureDelta)
		try self.leafTransient.set(classRowIndex(slot as CfgIndex, class: class), value: value) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.setTransient: leafTransient.set OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'setTransient'

	// Add `by` to the per-op contribution at `slot` for ONE file, and republish. THE edit the incremental
	// repair makes — see the type header on why a delta is enough.
	export function addDelta(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot, class RegClass, by PressureDelta)
		self.setDelta(slot, class: class, value: self.deltaAt(slot, class: class) + by)
		self.promote(blockLocal, slot: slot)
	end 'addDelta'

	// THE ONE STATEMENT OF WHAT A BLOCK'S EXIT LEAF HOLDS: its live-out population, per file. It is not
	// an accumulation of per-value terms like every other leaf — it is REPLACED outright — and it is the
	// number every suffix sum in the block runs through, so every `liveBefore` in that block is offset
	// by it.
	//
	// ⚠ TWO CALLERS, ONE STATEMENT. `rebuild` publishes it from the row it seeds its own backward sweep
	// with; `refreshExitLeaves` publishes it from `seedClassLive` over the same list, after a split has
	// re-marked the live sets. Written out at both sites — which it was, one of them re-tallying the
	// list by hand rather than through `seedClassLive` — the two are free to drift, and a block's whole
	// pressure profile moves by the difference.
	export function setExitPopulation(blockLocal CfgIndex, population RegCountColumn)
		let exit = self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal)
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			self.setDelta(exit, class: class, value: classRowGet(population, class: class) as PressureDelta)
		end 'eachClass'
		self.promote(blockLocal, slot: exit)
	end 'setExitPopulation'

	// Replace the reuse-copy transient at `slot` for ONE file, and republish.
	export function putTransient(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot, class RegClass, value PressureDelta)
		self.setTransient(slot, class: class, value: value)
		self.promote(blockLocal, slot: slot)
	end 'putTransient'

	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// THE TREE
	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------

	// One leaf's aggregate, recomputed from its own columns. A slot holding no op is never a peak
	// candidate — that covers the free slots AND the exit leaf, which carries a population but is a block
	// BOUNDARY rather than a program point the peak-finder visits.
	function refreshLeaf(slot PressureSlot)
		let node = self.leafNodeOf(slot)
		let isOp = try self.holdsOp.get(slot) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.refreshLeaf: holdsOp.get OOB at slot {slot}")
		try self.nodeOps.set(node, value: 1 if isOp else 0) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.refreshLeaf: nodeOps.set OOB at leaf {node}")
		// The clobber leaf is NOT restated here: it is a property of the op, written once by `seatOp`, and
		// a leaf this vacates is zeroed by `removeOp` instead.
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			let delta = self.deltaAt(slot, class: class)
			try self.nodeSum.set(classRowIndex(node as CfgIndex, class: class), value: delta) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.refreshLeaf: nodeSum.set OOB at leaf {node}")
			try self.nodeBest.set(classRowIndex(node as CfgIndex, class: class), value: (delta + self.transientAt(slot, class: class)) if isOp else NoOpInSubtree) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.refreshLeaf: nodeBest.set OOB at leaf {node}")
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'refreshLeaf'

	// One internal node from its two children. `left` covers the EARLIER slots, so a suffix that starts
	// in it runs through the whole of `right` — which is the `right.sum +` term, and the whole of the
	// aggregate.
	function combine(node ColumnLength)
		let left = node + node
		let right = left + 1
		try self.nodeOps.set(node, value: (try self.nodeOps.get(left) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeOps.get OOB at {left}")) + (try self.nodeOps.get(right) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeOps.get OOB at {right}"))) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeOps.set OOB at {node}")
		try self.nodeClobber.set(node, value: (try self.nodeClobber.get(left) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeClobber.get OOB at {left}")) or (try self.nodeClobber.get(right) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeClobber.get OOB at {right}"))) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeClobber.set OOB at {node}")
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			let leftSum = self.sumOf(left, class: class)
			let rightSum = self.sumOf(right, class: class)
			let leftBest = self.bestOf(left, class: class)
			let rightBest = self.bestOf(right, class: class)
			try self.nodeSum.set(classRowIndex(node as CfgIndex, class: class), value: leftSum + rightSum) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeSum.set OOB at {node}")
			// ⚠ THE PARENTHESES ARE LOAD-BEARING: `a + b if c else d` binds as `a + (b if c else d)`, which
			// on the not-taken arm adds `rightSum` to `rightBest` and inflates the whole subtree's peak by
			// the population to its right. Measured before they were there: a block peak of 31 where the
			// same leaves scan to 17.
			//
			// The tie is `suffixBestIsInLeft`'s, which `blockPeak`'s descent asks in the same terms — see
			// there for why the two may not be separate sentences.
			try self.nodeBest.set(classRowIndex(node as CfgIndex, class: class), value: (rightSum + leftBest) if suffixBestIsInLeft(leftBest, rightSum: rightSum, rightBest: rightBest) else rightBest) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.combine: nodeBest.set OOB at {node}")
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'combine'

	function sumOf(node ColumnLength, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return try self.nodeSum.get(classRowIndex(node as CfgIndex, class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.sumOf: nodeSum.get OOB at node {node}")
	end 'sumOf'

	function bestOf(node ColumnLength, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return try self.nodeBest.get(classRowIndex(node as CfgIndex, class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.bestOf: nodeBest.get OOB at node {node}")
	end 'bestOf'

	// Every leaf of `node`'s subtree and then every internal node of it, bottom-up — O(the subtree's
	// slots). `node` covers `[first, first + len)`, and `len` is a power of two.
	//
	// THE ONE REBUILD. The whole tree is the case `node == 1, first == 0, len == slotCount`, and a local
	// re-space is the same walk over one subtree. A second copy of the level walk for the small case
	// would be a second chance to fold a level the wrong way while every leaf stays right — the one
	// error `verifyPressureIndex` cannot see and `blockPeakByScan` exists to catch.
	function rebuildRange(node ColumnLength, first PressureSlot, len PressureSlot)
		for k in 0 upto len 'eachLeaf'
			self.refreshLeaf(first + k)
		end 'eachLeaf'
		// The subtree's internal nodes DEEPEST FIRST. The level `w` levels below `node` holds exactly `w`
		// nodes starting at `node * w` — so `w == 1` is `node` itself, and halving `w` down from `len / 2`
		// walks the levels bottom-up.
		var width = len / 2
		while width >= 1 'eachLevel'
			let base = node * (width as ColumnLength)
			for i in 0 upto width 'eachNodeAtLevel'
				self.combine(base + i)
			end 'eachNodeAtLevel'
			width = width / 2
		end 'eachLevel'
	end 'rebuildRange'

	// Re-publish ONE leaf up to its BLOCK's node — O(log slots in the block).
	function promote(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot)
		self.refreshLeaf(slot)
		self.promoteFrom(blockLocal, node: self.leafNodeOf(slot))
	end 'promote'

	// Re-publish every parent of `node` up to its BLOCK's node — the parents a subtree rebuild did not
	// reach. It stops at the block node because nothing above one is ever read: a node spanning two
	// blocks aggregates two suffix sums that no query joins, so maintaining it would be work with no
	// reader.
	function promoteFrom(blockLocal CfgIndex, node ColumnLength)
		let top = self.nodeOf(blockLocal)
		var parent = node / 2
		while parent >= top 'upToTheBlockNode'
			self.combine(parent)
			if parent == top 'atTheBlockNode'
				return
			end 'atTheBlockNode'
			parent = parent / 2
		end 'upToTheBlockNode'
	end 'promoteFrom'

	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// QUERIES
	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------

	// The block's peak: `max over its ops p of (liveBefore(p) + transient(p))`, and the SLOT it sits at.
	// One node read plus one descent, and the descent resolves a tie through `suffixBestIsInLeft` — the
	// SAME predicate `combine` folded the value with, not a second spelling of it.
	export function blockPeak(blockLocal CfgIndex, class RegClass) returns BlockPeakSlot
		let top = self.nodeOf(blockLocal)
		let best = self.bestOf(top, class: class)
		if (try self.nodeOps.get(top) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.blockPeak: nodeOps.get OOB at node {top}")) == 0 'noOpsAtAll'
			return BlockPeakSlot.create(false, slot: 0, pressure: 0)
		end 'noOpsAtAll'

		var node = top
		while node < self.slotCount 'descend'
			let left = node + node
			let right = left + 1
			node = left if suffixBestIsInLeft(self.bestOf(left, class: class), rightSum: self.sumOf(right, class: class), rightBest: self.bestOf(right, class: class)) else right
		end 'descend'
		return BlockPeakSlot.create(true, slot: ((node - self.slotCount) as PressureSlot), pressure: best)
	end 'blockPeak'

	// `blockPeak`'s answer, recomputed by SCANNING the block's run backward. VERIFY MODE's own oracle for
	// the tree: `verifyPressureIndex` compares the leaves of two indexes and the ROOT of each, and neither
	// catches a combine that folds the wrong way while every leaf is right. This does — it is the same
	// suffix accumulation the aggregate claims to summarise, with no aggregate in it.
	export function blockPeakByScan(blockLocal CfgIndex, class RegClass) returns BlockPeakSlot
		let first = self.firstSlotOf(blockLocal)
		let run = self.runLengthOf(blockLocal)
		var suffix = 0 as PressureDelta
		var found = false
		var bestSlot = 0 as PressureSlot
		var best = 0 as PressureDelta
		for k in 0 upto run 'eachSlotBackward'
			let slot = first + run - 1 - k
			suffix = suffix + self.deltaAt(slot, class: class)
			if not (try self.holdsOp.get(slot) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.blockPeakByScan: holdsOp.get OOB at slot {slot}")) 'notAnOp'
				continue
			end 'notAnOp'
			let here = suffix + self.transientAt(slot, class: class)
			// Scanning BACKWARD reaches later ops first, so `>=` keeps the EARLIEST op of a tying run —
			// which is the order `combine` resolves a tie in, and the order the sweep resolves one in.
			if (not found) or here >= best 'aNewBest'
				found = true
				best = here
				bestSlot = slot
			end 'aNewBest'
		end 'eachSlotBackward'
		return BlockPeakSlot.create(found, slot: bestSlot, pressure: best)
	end 'blockPeakByScan'

	// How many ops the block lays out, as the index sees it.
	export function opCountOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns BlockOpPos
		return try self.nodeOps.get(self.nodeOf(blockLocal)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.opCountOf: block {blockLocal} out of range")
	end 'opCountOf'

	// The block's LIVE-IN population of one file — the suffix sum over its whole run, which is what a
	// PHI's spill store faces (a block-arg's def point is the block's entry).
	export function blockLiveIn(blockLocal CfgIndex, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return self.sumOf(self.nodeOf(blockLocal), class: class)
	end 'blockLiveIn'

	// The block's LIVE-OUT population of one file — the exit leaf's own contribution.
	export function blockLiveOut(blockLocal CfgIndex, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return self.deltaAt(self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal), class: class)
	end 'blockLiveOut'

	// `liveAfter(slot)` for one file: the suffix sum over `(slot, blockEnd]`. That is the population a
	// store anchored right after the op at `slot` would face (`defLiveAfter`).
	export function liveAfterSlot(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return self.suffixFrom(blockLocal, slot: slot, class: class) - self.deltaAt(slot, class: class)
	end 'liveAfterSlot'

	// The OR of the clobber masks of every op in `[lo, hi]` — every register a value live ACROSS that
	// stretch of `blockLocal` must avoid. Free slots carry 0, so a range stated in slots needs no
	// rounding to the ops actually in it.
	export function clobberOr(blockLocal CfgIndex, lo PressureSlot, hi PressureSlot) returns RegMask
		if hi < lo 'emptyRange'
			return 0
		end 'emptyRange'
		// The range is derived from a value's live interval IN THIS BLOCK, so a range reaching outside the
		// block's run is a caller that mixed two blocks up — and the answer would silently carry another
		// block's clobbers into this value's forbidden mask.
		if lo < self.firstSlotOf(blockLocal) or hi > self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal) 'outsideTheBlock'
			panic("PressureIndex.clobberOr: slots [{lo}, {hi}] are not inside block {blockLocal}'s run [{self.firstSlotOf(blockLocal)}, {self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal)}]")
		end 'outsideTheBlock'

		var mask = 0 as RegMask
		var left = self.leafNodeOf(lo)
		var right = self.leafNodeOf(hi) + 1
		while left < right 'climb'
			if left - (left / 2) * 2 == 1 'leftEdgeIsARightChild'
				mask = mask or (try self.nodeClobber.get(left) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.clobberOr: nodeClobber.get OOB at node {left}"))
				left = left + 1
			end 'leftEdgeIsARightChild'
			if right - (right / 2) * 2 == 1 'rightEdgeIsARightChild'
				right = right - 1
				mask = mask or (try self.nodeClobber.get(right) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.clobberOr: nodeClobber.get OOB at node {right}"))
			end 'rightEdgeIsARightChild'
			left = left / 2
			right = right / 2
		end 'climb'
		return mask
	end 'clobberOr'

	// The clobber of the ONE op at `slot` — 0 for a free slot. It is the leaf `clobberOr` folds, read one
	// slot at a time, and it exists because a REPAIR has to name the op that takes a register rather than
	// the OR over a stretch (`findConfiningClobber`). Reading the seated leaf rather than re-deriving
	// `opClobberMask` from the op is what keeps "what does this op clobber" a single statement — see
	// `opClobberMask`, whose header says why that matters.
	export function clobberAtSlot(slot PressureSlot) returns RegMask
		return try self.nodeClobber.get(self.leafNodeOf(slot)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.clobberAtSlot: nodeClobber.get OOB at slot {slot}")
	end 'clobberAtSlot'

	// The sum of every leaf in `[slot, blockEnd]` — `liveBefore` at the op sitting in `slot`.
	function suffixFrom(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		let top = self.nodeOf(blockLocal)
		var node = self.leafNodeOf(slot)
		var total = self.sumOf(node, class: class)
		while node > top 'climb'
			// A LEFT child's right sibling covers slots after it and is wholly inside the suffix; a RIGHT
			// child's sibling is wholly before it and contributes nothing.
			if node - (node / 2) * 2 == 0 'isLeftChild'
				total = total + self.sumOf(node + 1, class: class)
			end 'isLeftChild'
			node = node / 2
		end 'climb'
		return total
	end 'suffixFrom'

	// The forward POSITION of the op at `slot` — how many op slots precede it in its block. Derived from
	// the tree's own occupancy, so the order has exactly one statement.
	export function positionOfSlot(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot) returns BlockOpPos
		let top = self.nodeOf(blockLocal)
		var node = self.leafNodeOf(slot)
		var before = 0 as BlockOpPos
		while node > top 'climb'
			if node - (node / 2) * 2 == 1 'isRightChild'
				before = before + (try self.nodeOps.get(node - 1) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.positionOfSlot: nodeOps.get OOB at node {node - 1}"))
			end 'isRightChild'
			node = node / 2
		end 'climb'
		return before
	end 'positionOfSlot'

	// The slot of the op at forward position `pos` of `blockLocal`, or the block's EXIT slot when `pos`
	// is one past the last op (which is where a splice at the end of the body lands).
	export function slotOfPosition(blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos) returns PressureSlot
		let top = self.nodeOf(blockLocal)
		let ops = try self.nodeOps.get(top) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.slotOfPosition: nodeOps.get OOB at node {top}")
		if pos >= ops 'pastTheLastOp'
			if pos > ops 'wellPastIt'
				panic("PressureIndex.slotOfPosition: position {pos} of block {blockLocal}, which holds {ops} op(s) — a position may name an op or the slot one past the last, never more")
			end 'wellPastIt'
			return self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal)
		end 'pastTheLastOp'

		var node = top
		var remaining = pos
		while node < self.slotCount 'descend'
			let left = node + node
			let inLeft = try self.nodeOps.get(left) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.slotOfPosition: nodeOps.get OOB at node {left}")
			if remaining < inLeft 'goLeft'
				node = left
			end 'goLeft' else 'goRight'
				remaining = remaining - inLeft
				node = left + 1
			end 'goRight'
		end 'descend'
		return self.slotOfLeafNode(node)
	end 'slotOfPosition'

	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
	// EDITS
	// ----------------------------------------------------------------------

	// Seat a newly spliced op at forward position `pos` of `blockLocal` and return its slot. The slot is
	// a FREE one strictly between the ops now at `pos - 1` and `pos`, so nothing already in the tree
	// moves; when the gap between them is used up the whole layout is re-spaced first.
	export function insertOp(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, scratch PressureRelayoutScratch, opFacts OpVariantFacts, blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos, opIdx OpIndex, buf OperandList, pending DenseColumn) returns PressureSlot
		var slot = self.freeSlotAt(blockLocal, pos: pos)
		if slot == self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal) 'gapUsedUp'
			// The one place the gap labelling can fail — see `makeRoom` for the scope it re-spaces and why
			// that is the block's business and not the function's.
			self.makeRoom(module, topo: topo, scratch: scratch, opFacts: opFacts, blockLocal: blockLocal, pos: pos, pending: pending)
			slot = self.freeSlotAt(blockLocal, pos: pos)
			if slot == self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal) 'stillUsedUp'
				panic("PressureIndex.insertOp: block {blockLocal} still has no free slot before position {pos} after a re-space that gave it {self.runLengthOf(blockLocal)} slots — the layout's gap arithmetic and `freeSlotAt`'s bounds have come apart")
			end 'stillUsedUp'
		end 'gapUsedUp'

		self.seatOp(slot, op: try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.insertOp: spliced op {opIdx} is not in module.ops"), opIdx: opIdx, opFacts: opFacts, buf: buf)
		self.promote(blockLocal, slot: slot)
		return slot
	end 'insertOp'

	export function runLengthOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns PressureSlot
		return try self.blockLen.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.runLengthOf: block {blockLocal} out of range")
	end 'runLengthOf'

	// A free slot strictly between the ops at `pos - 1` and `pos`, or the block's EXIT slot to say there
	// is none. Midpoint, so repeated inserts at one place HALVE the room rather than eat it from one end
	// — the same subdivision the gap labelling exists for.
	//
	// At position 0 the lower bound is the run's FIRST slot, which the layout never seats an op in for
	// exactly this reason; the upper bound at the end of the body is the exit leaf, which it also never
	// seats one in. So both ends of a block have room and neither needs a special case beyond the bound.
	function freeSlotAt(blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos) returns PressureSlot
		let low = self.spliceLowBound(blockLocal, pos: pos)
		let high = self.slotOfPosition(blockLocal, pos: pos)
		if high <= low + 1 'noRoom'
			return self.exitSlotOf(blockLocal)
		end 'noRoom'
		return low + (high - low) / 2
	end 'freeSlotAt'

	// The lower bound of the free stretch a splice at `pos` may take: the slot of the op it will follow,
	// or the run's FIRST slot at position 0 — which the layout never seats an op in, so a splice at the
	// top of a block needs no special case beyond this bound.
	function spliceLowBound(blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos) returns PressureSlot
		return self.firstSlotOf(blockLocal) if pos == 0 else self.slotOfPosition(blockLocal, pos: pos - 1)
	end 'spliceLowBound'

	// Return `slot` to the EMPTY state — no op, no clobber, no population numbers — so nothing it carried
	// survives as a phantom program point. The tree is NOT republished here: `removeOp` does that for its
	// one slot, `respaceRange` does it once for a whole subtree.
	function vacateLeaf(slot PressureSlot)
		try self.holdsOp.set(slot, value: false) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.vacateLeaf: holdsOp.set OOB at slot {slot}")
		try self.nodeClobber.set(self.leafNodeOf(slot), value: 0) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.vacateLeaf: nodeClobber.set OOB at slot {slot}")
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			self.setDelta(slot, class: class, value: 0)
			self.setTransient(slot, class: class, value: 0)
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'vacateLeaf'

	// Vacate `slot` — the op there left the function (a fully rematerialized constant's def).
	export function removeOp(blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot)
		self.vacateLeaf(slot)
		self.promote(blockLocal, slot: slot)
	end 'removeOp'

	// Build the whole index from ONE backward pass per block — the SAME pass `analyzeBlockPressure` runs,
	// stepping with the same transfer function, so every leaf is by construction the number that sweep
	// would have produced there. Run once per function, when it turns out to need a split at all.
	//
	// ⭐ `delta` IS MEASURED, NOT RE-DERIVED. It is the per-class live tally's movement ACROSS
	// `stepRowBackwardOverOperands` — read either side of the step — so the index cannot come to hold a
	// different notion of "what this op does to the live population" than the sweep does. The incremental
	// repair states the same quantity per VALUE (`operandContribution`); `verifyPressureIndex` is what
	// keeps those two honest against each other.
	export function rebuild(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, sweep PressureSweepScratch, seedBuf ValueIdArray, scratch PressureRelayoutScratch)
		let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
		self.layOut(module, topo: topo, opCounts: scratch.opCounts)
		self.defSlot.refill(liveness.valueCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)

		var liveRow = sweep.liveRow
		var buf = sweep.buf
		var ordered = sweep.ordered
		var liveByClass = sweep.liveByClass
		var transient = sweep.opPressure
		// The live tally as it stood BEFORE each backward step — the other half of the measurement that
		// makes a leaf's number the sweep's own. A local, because this runs once per splitting function
		// and a field on the shared scratch would be a row named for one purpose and read for another.
		var before = zeroClassRow()

		for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
			let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: b)
			liveness.seedRowFromLiveOut(b, row: liveRow, scratch: seedBuf)
			seedClassLive(liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass, values: seedBuf)

			// The EXIT leaf carries the block's live-out population, which is what turns a suffix sum from
			// an op into that op's live-BEFORE count with no separate term to add. `liveByClass` is that
			// population exactly — `seedClassLive` has just filled it from the same live-out list — so this
			// build and the incremental repair publish the leaf through one function.
			self.setExitPopulation(b, population: liveByClass)

			ordered.clear()
			collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
			let opCount = ordered.count()
			for ri in 0 upto opCount 'eachOpRev'
				let forwardPos = opCount - 1 - ri
				let opIdx = try ordered.get(forwardPos) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.rebuild: ordered.get OOB")
				let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.rebuild: opIdx not in module.ops")
				targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)

				// The transient is decided on the live-AFTER set, before the backward step — exactly where
				// `analyzeBlockPressure` reads it.
				classRowZero(transient)
				addOpTransientPressure(buf, liveRow: liveRow, classes: liveness.valueClass, pressureOut: transient)

				for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'snapshotBefore'
					let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
					classRowSet(before, class: class, value: classRowGet(liveByClass, class: class))
				end 'snapshotBefore'
				stepRowBackwardOverOperands(liveRow, base: 0, buf: buf, liveByClass: liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass)

				let slot = self.evenSlot(b, pos: forwardPos)
				self.seatOp(slot, op: op, opIdx: opIdx, opFacts: liveness.opFacts, buf: buf)
				for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
					let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
					self.setDelta(slot, class: class, value: (classRowGet(liveByClass, class: class) as PressureDelta) - (classRowGet(before, class: class) as PressureDelta))
					self.setTransient(slot, class: class, value: classRowGet(transient, class: class) as PressureDelta)
				end 'eachClass'
			end 'eachOpRev'

			liveness.clearRowAfterSweep(b, block: block, row: liveRow, scratch: seedBuf)
		end 'eachBlock'

		self.rebuildRange(1, first: 0, len: self.slotCount as PressureSlot)
	end 'rebuild'

	// Make room for a splice at `pos` of `blockLocal`, whose gap is used up. THE ONE PLACE the gap
	// labelling can fail, and the only cost in the index that is not proportional to what a split changed
	// — so what it re-spaces, and how rarely, is the whole question.
	//
	// ⭐ THE SCOPE IS THE SMALLEST SUBTREE THAT CAN ABSORB IT, NOT THE FUNCTION. Climbing from the
	// exhausted gap, the first ancestor whose ops still fit its own slots with a free slot between each
	// neighbouring pair is re-spaced, and nothing outside it moves. A block's run is exactly one node, so
	// every ancestor up to it lies inside the block — and the climb stops there, because a node spanning
	// two blocks is not this block's to re-space.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ WHY THE FALLBACK IS AMORTIZED, which is what makes this a BOUND and not a hope. Only when the
	// block's WHOLE RUN can no longer hold its ops two slots apart is the FUNCTION re-spaced. A run is
	// laid out at `(k + 1) * SlotGap + 1` slots rounded up to a power of two, so a block laid out with `k`
	// ops has room for about `k * SlotGap / 2` before that test fails: with `SlotGap` at 4 the block must
	// roughly DOUBLE its op count to force one. And `relayout` re-sizes every run from its current count,
	// so the doubling starts over. A block can therefore force at most O(log(final ops / initial ops))
	// function-wide re-spaces however many ops are spliced into it.
	//
	// A LOCAL re-space is Θ(the subtree), which for an ordinary collision is a few dozen slots.
	function makeRoom(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, scratch PressureRelayoutScratch, opFacts OpVariantFacts, blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos, pending DenseColumn)
		let top = self.nodeOf(blockLocal)
		// From the exhausted gap's LOWER neighbour, so every node the climb tests contains it: re-spacing a
		// range that holds only the upper neighbour could leave the two still adjacent.
		var node = self.leafNodeOf(self.spliceLowBound(blockLocal, pos: pos)) / 2
		var len = 2 as PressureSlot
		while node >= top 'climb'
			let count = try self.nodeOps.get(node) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.makeRoom: nodeOps.get OOB at node {node}")
			let stride = spreadStride(len, count: count)
			if stride >= MinRespaceStride 'thisSubtreeAbsorbsIt'
				// A node covering `len` leaves from `first` IS leaf `slotCount + first` divided by `len`, so
				// the node index and its width state the range without a second column to keep current.
				self.respaceRange(module, node: node, first: self.slotOfLeafNode(node * (len as ColumnLength)), len: len, count: count, stride: stride, blockLocal: blockLocal, opFacts: opFacts, scratch: scratch, pending: pending)
				return
			end 'thisSubtreeAbsorbsIt'
			node = node / 2
			len = len + len
		end 'climb'

		self.relayout(module, topo: topo, scratch: scratch, opFacts: opFacts, pending: pending)
	end 'makeRoom'

	// Re-space ONE subtree — `node`, covering `[first, first + len)` and holding `count` ops — evenly
	// across its own slots, and republish it. Θ(len), and nothing outside the range moves.
	//
	// It reads the LEAVES, so every op's population numbers travel with it: a COPY of the index, never a
	// re-derivation from the IR.
	function respaceRange(module TargetModule, node ColumnLength, first PressureSlot, len PressureSlot, count BlockOpPos, stride PressureSlot, blockLocal CfgIndex, opFacts OpVariantFacts, scratch PressureRelayoutScratch, pending DenseColumn)
		scratch.captureWindow(self.holdsOp, runOpAt: self.opAt, runDelta: self.leafDelta, runTransient: self.leafTransient, from: first, count: len)

		// VACATE EVERY OLD SEAT BEFORE TAKING ANY NEW ONE. The two sets of slots overlap — a re-space that
		// moves an op only a little may seat it where it already sat — so a single pass would clear a leaf
		// this same re-space had already filled.
		for k in 0 upto len 'eachOldSlot'
			if scratch.holdsOpAt(first + k) 'anOp'
				self.vacateLeaf(first + k)
			end 'anOp'
		end 'eachOldSlot'

		var j = 0 as BlockOpPos
		for k in 0 upto len 'eachMove'
			let oldSlot = first + k
			if not scratch.holdsOpAt(oldSlot) 'notAnOp'
				continue
			end 'notAnOp'
			self.reseat(module, oldSlot: oldSlot, newSlot: first + ((j + 1) as PressureSlot) * stride, opIdx: scratch.opAtSlot(oldSlot), opFacts: opFacts, scratch: scratch)
			j = j + 1
		end 'eachMove'

		if j != count 'countDisagreesWithTheTree'
			panic("PressureIndex.respaceRange: node {node} covering slots [{first}, {first + len}) claims {count} op(s) but its leaves hold {j} — the occupancy count `slotOfPosition` descends on has drifted from the `holdsOp` flags it summarises")
		end 'countDisagreesWithTheTree'

		scratch.applyMoves(pending)
		self.rebuildRange(node, first: first, len: len)
		self.promoteFrom(blockLocal, node: node)
	end 'respaceRange'

	// Re-space every block's run so each op has its full gap again, GROWING the space of a block that has
	// taken on more ops than its run was sized for. Θ(THE FUNCTION'S SLOTS) — the last resort `makeRoom`
	// falls back to, and see there for why a block must roughly double its op count to force one.
	//
	// ⚠ THE PRICE OF THE GAP IS MEMORY, AND IT IS THE INDEX'S ONLY REGRESSION. `SlotGap` multiplies every
	// column, and each block's run is additionally rounded up to a power of two and aligned to its own
	// length (which is what makes a whole-block query one node read). MEASURED on the scale corpus:
	// whole-compile bytes +8.9%, all of it linear.
	function relayout(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, scratch PressureRelayoutScratch, opFacts OpVariantFacts, pending DenseColumn)
		scratch.captureRuns(self.blockFirst, runLen: self.blockLen)
		scratch.captureWindow(self.holdsOp, runOpAt: self.opAt, runDelta: self.leafDelta, runTransient: self.leafTransient, from: 0, count: self.slotCount as PressureSlot)

		scratch.opCounts.refill(topo.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'countEachBlock'
			try scratch.opCounts.set(b, value: scratch.opsIn(b)) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.relayout: opCounts.set OOB for block {b}")
		end 'countEachBlock'
		self.placeRuns(topo, opCounts: scratch.opCounts)

		for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
			var pos = 0 as BlockOpPos
			for k in 0 upto scratch.runLengthOf(b) - 1 'eachOldSlot'
				let oldSlot = scratch.firstOf(b) + k
				if not scratch.holdsOpAt(oldSlot) 'notAnOp'
					continue
				end 'notAnOp'
				self.reseat(module, oldSlot: oldSlot, newSlot: self.evenSlot(b, pos: pos), opIdx: scratch.opAtSlot(oldSlot), opFacts: opFacts, scratch: scratch)
				pos = pos + 1
			end 'eachOldSlot'
			// The exit leaf's population travels too — it is a fact about the BLOCK, not about a slot.
			self.copyLeafNumbers(scratch, oldSlot: scratch.exitOf(b), newSlot: self.exitSlotOf(b))
		end 'eachBlock'

		scratch.applyMoves(pending)
		self.rebuildRange(1, first: 0, len: self.slotCount as PressureSlot)
	end 'relayout'

	// Move one op from `oldSlot` to `newSlot`: seat it, carry its population numbers across, and record
	// the move so `applyMoves` can repair the slots the caller is holding.
	//
	// ⚠ THE SCRATCH'S OWN OPERAND BUFFER, NOT THE CALLER'S. A re-space happens INSIDE `insertOp`, which is
	// mid-way through seating an op of its own and still holding that op's operands — so borrowing the
	// caller's list here leaves the interrupted seat recording the LAST moved op's defs instead of its
	// own, and the fresh id's def slot then points at whatever the layout put in slot 0. (Measured, with
	// the gap tightened to force a re-space on every insertion: `opAtSlot: slot 0 holds no op` on four
	// spill tests.)
	function reseat(module TargetModule, oldSlot PressureSlot, newSlot PressureSlot, opIdx OpIndex, opFacts OpVariantFacts, scratch PressureRelayoutScratch)
		self.seatOp(newSlot, op: try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("PressureIndex.reseat: op {opIdx} not in module.ops"), opIdx: opIdx, opFacts: opFacts, buf: scratch.operands)
		self.copyLeafNumbers(scratch, oldSlot: oldSlot, newSlot: newSlot)
		scratch.recordMove(oldSlot, newSlot: newSlot)
	end 'reseat'

	function copyLeafNumbers(scratch PressureRelayoutScratch, oldSlot PressureSlot, newSlot PressureSlot)
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			self.setDelta(newSlot, class: class, value: scratch.deltaAt(oldSlot, class: class))
			self.setTransient(newSlot, class: class, value: scratch.transientAt(oldSlot, class: class))
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'copyLeafNumbers'
end 'PressureIndex'

// The index's own leaves, taken aside so a re-space can lay slots out fresh and then read the old ones
// back. It lives on `SplitScratch` rather than being built per re-space for the reason every other
// buffer there does: a re-space allocating six function-sized columns would put the cost back where the
// gap labelling exists to keep it away from.
//
// IT IS A WINDOW, NOT NECESSARILY THE WHOLE INDEX. A local re-space (`respaceRange`) copies aside only
// the subtree it is about to re-space, which is what makes it Θ(that subtree); the function-wide
// `relayout` is simply the case where the window is every slot. The per-BLOCK run columns are captured
// separately, because only `relayout` re-sizes runs.
export type PressureRelayoutScratch
	export var opCounts as DenseColumn
	// The re-space's OWN operand list — see the note at its one use.
	export var operands as OperandList
	var first as PressureSlotColumn
	var len as PressureSlotColumn
	// The captured slots are `[windowFirst, windowFirst + holdsOp.count())` of the index, and `moved`
	// holds, per captured slot, where the op that sat there ENDED UP. A slot holding no op maps to
	// itself and is never read.
	var windowFirst as PressureSlot
	var holdsOp as BoolArray
	var opAt as OpIndexArray
	var delta as PressureDeltaColumn
	var transient as PressureDeltaColumn
	var moved as DenseColumn

	export static function create() returns PressureRelayoutScratch
		return Self{opCounts: DenseColumn.create(), operands: OperandList.create(), first: PressureSlotColumn.create(), len: PressureSlotColumn.create(), windowFirst: 0, holdsOp: BoolArray.create(), opAt: OpIndexArray.create(), delta: PressureDeltaColumn.create(), transient: PressureDeltaColumn.create(), moved: DenseColumn.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Copy the index's per-BLOCK run layout aside — `relayout` only, which is the one thing that moves a
	// run. `refill(0)` first, so it never reads a stale tail from a previous re-space.
	export function captureRuns(runFirst PressureSlotColumn, runLen PressureSlotColumn)
		self.first.refill(0, value: 0)
		self.len.refill(0, value: 0)
		self.first.append(runFirst)
		self.len.append(runLen)
	end 'captureRuns'

	// Copy the leaves of `[from, from + count)` aside. Sized by `refill` rather than grown by `append`,
	// so a window smaller than the last one neither reads a stale tail nor reallocates.
	export function captureWindow(runHoldsOp BoolArray, runOpAt OpIndexArray, runDelta PressureDeltaColumn, runTransient PressureDeltaColumn, from PressureSlot, count PressureSlot)
		self.windowFirst = from
		self.holdsOp.refill(count as ElementIndex, value: false)
		self.opAt.refill(count as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		self.delta.refill(((count * RegClassCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		self.transient.refill(((count * RegClassCount) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		self.moved.refill(count as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		for k in 0 upto count 'eachSlot'
			try self.holdsOp.set(k, value: try runHoldsOp.get(from + k) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: holdsOp.get OOB at slot {from + k}")) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: holdsOp.set OOB at {k}")
			try self.opAt.set(k, value: try runOpAt.get(from + k) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: opAt.get OOB at slot {from + k}")) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: opAt.set OOB at {k}")
			try self.moved.set(k, value: from + k) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: moved.set OOB at {k}")
			for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
				let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
				try self.delta.set(classRowIndex(k, class: class), value: try runDelta.get(classRowIndex(((from + k) as CfgIndex), class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: leafDelta.get OOB at slot {from + k}")) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: delta.set OOB at {k}")
				try self.transient.set(classRowIndex(k, class: class), value: try runTransient.get(classRowIndex(((from + k) as CfgIndex), class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: leafTransient.get OOB at slot {from + k}")) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.captureWindow: transient.set OOB at {k}")
			end 'eachClass'
		end 'eachSlot'
	end 'captureWindow'

	// Record that the op at `oldSlot` now sits at `newSlot`.
	export function recordMove(oldSlot PressureSlot, newSlot PressureSlot)
		try self.moved.set(oldSlot - self.windowFirst, value: newSlot) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.recordMove: slot {oldSlot} is outside the captured window at {self.windowFirst}")
	end 'recordMove'

	// Rewrite every slot in `pending` this re-space MOVED — the slots `SplitEdits` has already recorded
	// for the ops it seated earlier in the same batch. A slot outside the window is left alone.
	//
	// ⚠ THIS REPAIR USED TO BE A COMMENT INSTEAD OF A FUNCTION. `appendInsertedSlots` asserted that a
	// re-space "re-derived" the slots already returned in the batch; nothing did, and the second op of any
	// batch whose first op had triggered a re-space was read back from a slot the re-space had emptied.
	// (Measured with `SlotGap` at 2, which brings the gap's tolerance down to one nested insertion:
	// `PressureIndex.opAtSlot: slot 315 holds no op` out of `reindexSplitValues`.)
	export function applyMoves(pending DenseColumn)
		let count = self.holdsOp.count()
		for i in 0 upto pending.count() 'eachPending'
			let slot = (try pending.get(i) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.applyMoves: pending.get OOB at {i}")) as PressureSlot
			if slot < self.windowFirst or slot >= self.windowFirst + count 'outsideTheWindow'
				continue
			end 'outsideTheWindow'
			try pending.set(i, value: try self.moved.get(slot - self.windowFirst) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.applyMoves: moved.get OOB at {slot - self.windowFirst}")) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.applyMoves: pending.set OOB at {i}")
		end 'eachPending'
	end 'applyMoves'

	export function firstOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns PressureSlot
		return try self.first.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.firstOf: block {blockLocal} out of range")
	end 'firstOf'

	export function runLengthOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns PressureSlot
		return try self.len.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.runLengthOf: block {blockLocal} out of range")
	end 'runLengthOf'

	export function exitOf(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns PressureSlot
		return self.firstOf(blockLocal) + self.runLengthOf(blockLocal) - 1
	end 'exitOf'

	// Every accessor below takes an ABSOLUTE slot and resolves it against the window here, so a caller
	// re-spacing a subtree states the same coordinates as one re-spacing the whole index.
	function windowIndexOf(slot PressureSlot) returns PressureSlot
		if slot < self.windowFirst or slot >= self.windowFirst + self.holdsOp.count() 'outsideTheWindow'
			panic("PressureRelayoutScratch: slot {slot} is outside the captured window [{self.windowFirst}, {self.windowFirst + self.holdsOp.count()})")
		end 'outsideTheWindow'
		return slot - self.windowFirst
	end 'windowIndexOf'

	export function holdsOpAt(slot PressureSlot) returns bool
		return try self.holdsOp.get(self.windowIndexOf(slot)) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.holdsOpAt: slot {slot} out of range")
	end 'holdsOpAt'

	export function opAtSlot(slot PressureSlot) returns OpIndex
		return try self.opAt.get(self.windowIndexOf(slot)) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.opAtSlot: slot {slot} out of range")
	end 'opAtSlot'

	export function deltaAt(slot PressureSlot, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return try self.delta.get(classRowIndex(self.windowIndexOf(slot) as CfgIndex, class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.deltaAt: slot {slot} out of range")
	end 'deltaAt'

	export function transientAt(slot PressureSlot, class RegClass) returns PressureDelta
		return try self.transient.get(classRowIndex(self.windowIndexOf(slot) as CfgIndex, class: class)) otherwise panic("PressureRelayoutScratch.transientAt: slot {slot} out of range")
	end 'transientAt'

	// How many op slots block `blockLocal` held in the layout this holds — the count its new run is
	// sized from.
	export function opsIn(blockLocal CfgIndex) returns BlockOpPos
		var ops = 0 as BlockOpPos
		let base = self.firstOf(blockLocal)
		for k in 0 upto self.runLengthOf(blockLocal) - 1 'eachSlot'
			if self.holdsOpAt(base + k) 'anOp'
				ops = ops + 1
			end 'anOp'
		end 'eachSlot'
		return ops
	end 'opsIn'
end 'PressureRelayoutScratch'

// ==========================================================================
// KEEPING THE POSITIONAL INDEX CURRENT — one split's edits, and nothing more
// ==========================================================================
//
// A split changes the live-before population of every op over a stretch that is Θ(the function) wide,
// and the whole point of the index is that repairing it costs NOTHING proportional to that stretch.
// What makes that exact is the separability the type header states: `e(p)` is a sum of per-VALUE
// contributions, and a split moves only the VICTIM's and the fresh ids'. So the repair is
//
//   1. WITHDRAW the victim's contribution from every op that names it, in the PRE-split world;
//   2. let the mutators run, seating each spliced op as `SplitEdits.commit` materializes it;
//   3. ADD the victim's and each fresh id's contribution at the ops that name them NOW;
//   4. restate each dirty block's EXIT leaf, which carries its live-out population.
//
// Steps 1 and 3 are O(uses of one value); step 4 is O(live-out) per dirty block, which is what the
// block sweep it replaces paid merely to SEED itself.

// Value `v`'s own contribution to the per-op number at an op it is an operand of:
//
//     contrib = (v is a USE operand there) - (v is live AFTER that op)
//
// which is `stepRowBackwardOverOperands` read one value at a time — defs removed if live, then uses
// added if absent — and covers the read-modify-write case (both, so removed and re-added) without a
// third arm. `liveAfter` is the caller's, because it is answered from the value's own use thread.
function operandContribution(isUse bool, liveAfter bool) returns PressureDelta
	return (1 if isUse else 0) - (1 if liveAfter else 0)
end 'operandContribution'

// Is `v` in a block's live-IN or live-OUT list? A block's live sets are SPARSE LISTS, so there is no
// bit to test and membership is an O(live) scan — the same scan `sweepBlockPressure` pays merely to
// seed itself with, and asked once per (value, block) the repair touches.
//
// ONE scan over a LIST, rather than one per QUESTION. It was written THREE times: a `liveOutHolds` and a
// `liveInHolds` differing only in which accessor they call, and a third copy down in `verifyLiveSet`,
// which is the one this keeps the name of. Taking the LIST rather than a (sets, block) pair is what
// makes one function serve all three — the block lookup is the caller's, and it is the only thing they
// disagreed about.
function liveListHolds(list ValueIdArray, v ValueId) returns bool
	for u in list 'each'
		if u == v 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'each'
	return false
end 'liveListHolds'

// Add (`adding`) or withdraw value `v`'s contribution at every op that names it as an operand. The one
// direction difference is the SIGN, so both halves of the repair are one function and cannot come to
// disagree about what a value contributes.
//
// It reads the use thread it is handed, so the WITHDRAW call must run before the mutation rewrites that
// thread and the ADD call after `reindexSplitValues` has rebuilt it.
//
// ⚠ AN EDGE USE IS NOT AN OPERAND. Its record names the block's terminator as its sequence anchor, but
// the value is read by the EDGE, not by that op — it reaches the per-op numbers only through the block's
// live-out population (the exit leaf). Contributing at the terminator would count it twice.
//
// ⚠ THE USE TERM AND THE DEF TERM ARE SUMMED, WHICH IS RIGHT ONLY BECAUSE NO OP GIVES ONE VALUE BOTH
// ROLES. `operandContribution` is one term per (value, op), and this emits one for each op use plus one
// for the def — so a value that were BOTH a use and a def operand of a single op would get two, and its
// leaf would be low by exactly `[it is live after that op]`. SSA is what forbids it: a def mints a new
// id, and a two-address op is modelled as a reuse DEF over a distinct input value (`pushReuseBinOperands`),
// never as `v = op(v)`. A lowering that ever emitted one would need this to fold the two terms.
function applyValueToPressure(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, v ValueId, adding bool)
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	let live = liveness.live
	let class = liveness.classOf(v)
	var sign = 1 as PressureDelta
	if not adding 'withdrawing'
		sign = -sign
	end 'withdrawing'
	let useSites = index.useCount(v)

	// `numBlocks` is "no block read yet" — no block index can equal it (the sentinel `ReachCache` and
	// `PeakTree` both use). The live-out answer is a fact about a BLOCK, so it is read once per run of
	// records in one block rather than once per record.
	var scannedBlock = liveness.cfg.topo.numBlocks
	var scannedLiveOut = false
	var usedInDefBlock = false
	let defBlock = index.defBlockOrNone(v)

	if useSites > 0 'used'
		var record = index.firstUseOf(v)
		for k in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
			let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
			if blockLocal != scannedBlock 'aNewBlock'
				scannedBlock = blockLocal
				scannedLiveOut = liveListHolds(live.liveOut(blockLocal), v: v)
			end 'aNewBlock'
			if blockLocal == defBlock 'readInItsOwnDefBlock'
				usedInDefBlock = true
			end 'readInItsOwnDefBlock'

			let next = index.nextUseOf(record)
			match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
				edge then break 'kind'
				op then applyOneUseToPressure(index, pressure: pressure, class: class, sign: sign, record: record, next: next, isLast: k + 1 == useSites, liveOut: scannedLiveOut)
			end 'kind'
			record = next
		end 'eachUse'
	end 'used'

	// The DEF contributes `0 - [v is live after it]`, and by SSA dominance ANY use of `v` in its own def
	// block sits after the def — so "is it read again here" is exactly "does it have a record here".
	// A PHI has no defining op, so it lands on no leaf: it is live from the block's ENTRY, which the
	// suffix sum reaches through the ops that read it (and through the exit leaf when it survives the
	// block), with nothing to subtract.
	if index.defKindOf(v) != ValueDefKind.opDef 'noDefiningOp'
		return
	end 'noDefiningOp'

	let defBlockLocal = index.defBlockOf(v)
	let defLiveAfter = usedInDefBlock or liveListHolds(live.liveOut(defBlockLocal), v: v)
	pressure.addDelta(defBlockLocal, slot: pressure.defSlotOf(v), class: class, by: sign * operandContribution(false, liveAfter: defLiveAfter))
end 'applyValueToPressure'

// ONE op use of `v`. `v` is live AFTER that op iff it is live-out of the block or the NEXT record on its
// thread reads it later in the SAME block — which is an O(1) lookahead precisely because the thread is
// held ascending by sequence.
function applyOneUseToPressure(index UseIndex, pressure PressureIndex, class RegClass, sign PressureDelta, record UseRecordIndex, next UseRecordIndex, isLast bool, liveOut bool)
	let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
	let seq = index.useSeqAt(record)
	let readAgainHere = (not isLast) and index.useBlockAt(next) == blockLocal and index.useSeqAt(next) > seq
	let slot = pressure.slotOfPosition(blockLocal, pos: index.usePosAt(record))
	pressure.addDelta(blockLocal, slot: slot, class: class, by: sign * operandContribution(true, liveAfter: liveOut or readAgainHere))
end 'applyOneUseToPressure'

// Restate the transient at the op sitting in `slot` — the incremental repair of the quantity
// `addOpTransientPressure` decides.
//
// ⭐ IT DOES NOT DECIDE ANYTHING. It MATERIALIZES this op's live-AFTER row and hands it to
// `addOpTransientPressure`, which is the ONE place the FULL-POOL transient is defined — as
// `opConfinedTransient` is the one place the CONFINED one is, both selecting the same def through
// `opTransientDefOf`. The only thing that
// legitimately differs between the full build and a repair is HOW "is `v` live after this point?" is
// answered — off a swept live row there, off the USE INDEX here, because a repair has no live row —
// and that is now the only thing this function does.
//
// ⚠ IT USED TO RE-DERIVE THE DECISION ITSELF, and that is a fact written down twice, in two files,
// where nothing made the copies agree: the same question ("is the def dead? does the op free a
// register?") asked once against a bitset and once against the index. `VerifyIncrementalSplit` exists
// to CATCH that divergence, but it is compiled out by default, so a clause added to one copy and not
// the other ships as a wrong pressure figure and a colorer panic. The class filter on "frees a
// register" had to be applied to both copies on the day it was found, which is the divergence
// arriving on schedule.
//
// The row is materialized for THIS OP'S OPERANDS ONLY — every value the decision can consult (the def,
// the reuse input, the virtual uses) is an operand of this op — and cleared again the same way, so it
// stays O(operands) and the shared scratch row is zero on the way in and on the way out, exactly as
// `sweepBlockPressure` requires and leaves it.
//
// ⚠ IT IS RE-DERIVED UNCONDITIONALLY, and the narrowing that used to stand here would now be WRONG. The
// reuse-copy transient depends on ONE value (the reuse input), so an op whose reuse input the split
// never named could be skipped; the DEAD-DEF transient depends on the op's def AND on every one of its
// virtual uses — which is exactly the set of values that makes `refreshTransientsNaming` visit this op
// at all. Every visit is therefore a genuine trigger and there is nothing left to narrow away.
function refreshTransientAt(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot, sweep PressureSweepScratch)
	var buf = sweep.buf
	let op = try module.ops.get(pressure.opAtSlot(slot)) otherwise panic("refreshTransientAt: the op at slot {slot} is not in module.ops")
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)

	// The value space grows with every split, so the shared row follows it before anything is set in it.
	sweep.widenRow(liveness.wordsPerRow)
	var liveRow = sweep.liveRow

	var virtualDefs = 0 as RegCount
	for o in buf 'materializeLiveAfter'
		if not operandIsVirtual(o) 'physical'
			continue
		end 'physical'

		if operandIsDef(o) 'aDef'
			virtualDefs = virtualDefs + 1
		end 'aDef'
		if liveAfterSlot(liveness, pressure: pressure, v: operandValueId(o), blockLocal: blockLocal, slot: slot) 'liveAfterTheOp'
			bitsetAdd(liveRow, base: 0, bit: operandValueId(o) as BitIndex)
		end 'liveAfterTheOp'
	end 'materializeLiveAfter'

	// `addOpTransientPressure` takes the FIRST virtual def and stops, so with two of them the answer
	// depends on which one the operand model happens to list first. No TargetOp defines more than one
	// value, so this is unreachable — and it is asserted rather than assumed, because the day one does,
	// a transient would be decided from an arbitrary operand, silently. It rides the walk above, which
	// already visits every operand, so it costs nothing to make.
	if virtualDefs > 1 'aSecondVirtualDef'
		panic("refreshTransientAt: the op at slot {slot} of block {blockLocal} carries {virtualDefs} virtual defs — `addOpTransientPressure` reads exactly one, because no TargetOp defines more than one value")
	end 'aSecondVirtualDef'

	classRowZero(sweep.opPressure)
	addOpTransientPressure(buf, liveRow: liveRow, classes: liveness.valueClass, pressureOut: sweep.opPressure)
	for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
		let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
		pressure.putTransient(blockLocal, slot: slot, class: class, value: classRowGet(sweep.opPressure, class: class) as PressureDelta)
	end 'eachClass'

	// Back to zero in O(operands) — the row is SHARED with the full sweep, which requires it zero on
	// entry, and a bit left standing here is a value the next block's sweep would believe live.
	for o in buf 'clearLiveAfter'
		if operandIsVirtual(o) 'virtual'
			bitsetRemove(liveRow, base: 0, bit: operandValueId(o) as BitIndex)
		end 'virtual'
	end 'clearLiveAfter'
end 'refreshTransientAt'

// Is `v` live AFTER the op sitting in `slot`? The general form of the lookahead
// `applyOneUseToPressure` gets for free: live-out of the block, or read again later in it.
function liveAfterSlot(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, v ValueId, blockLocal CfgIndex, slot PressureSlot) returns bool
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	if liveListHolds(liveness.live.liveOut(blockLocal), v: v) 'liveOnEveryExit'
		return true
	end 'liveOnEveryExit'

	let seq = seqAt(blockLocal, pos: pressure.positionOfSlot(blockLocal, slot: slot))
	let useSites = index.useCount(v)
	if useSites == 0 'neverRead'
		return false
	end 'neverRead'

	var record = index.firstUseOf(v)
	for _ in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
		if index.useBlockAt(record) == blockLocal and index.useSeqAt(record) > seq 'readLaterHere'
			return true
		end 'readLaterHere'
		record = index.nextUseOf(record)
	end 'eachUse'
	return false
end 'liveAfterSlot'

// Restate each dirty block's EXIT leaf — its live-out population, which every suffix sum in that block
// runs through. O(live-out) per block, which is what `sweepBlockPressure` pays merely to SEED itself.
function refreshExitLeaves(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, dirty BlockSet, row RegCountColumn)
	for k in 0 upto dirty.count() 'eachDirtyBlock'
		let blockLocal = dirty.at(k)
		// `seedClassLive`, not a tally written out again here: it is the same per-class count over the same
		// sparse list that the index BUILD seeds its own sweep with, and a second copy of it is a second
		// chance to count a file wrong (see `PressureIndex.setExitPopulation`).
		seedClassLive(row, classes: liveness.valueClass, values: liveness.live.liveOut(blockLocal))
		pressure.setExitPopulation(blockLocal, population: row)
	end 'eachDirtyBlock'
end 'refreshExitLeaves'

// Re-derive the transients of every op the split can have moved one at: the ops that name the victim or
// a fresh id NOW, plus the ops it spliced in (which carry none — they are a store, a reload or a
// constant materialization, none of them two-address).
function refreshSplitTransients(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, split SplitValues, sweep PressureSweepScratch)
	refreshTransientsNaming(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: pressure, v: split.victim, sweep: sweep)
	for f in split.freshFrom upto split.freshTo 'eachFresh'
		refreshTransientsNaming(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: pressure, v: f, sweep: sweep)
	end 'eachFresh'
end 'refreshSplitTransients'

// Every op that names `v` as an operand — its def and its op uses — restated.
function refreshTransientsNaming(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, v ValueId, sweep PressureSweepScratch)
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	let useSites = index.useCount(v)
	if useSites > 0 'used'
		var record = index.firstUseOf(v)
		for _ in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
			let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
			match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
				edge then break 'kind'
				op then refreshTransientAt(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: blockLocal, slot: pressure.slotOfPosition(blockLocal, pos: index.usePosAt(record)), sweep: sweep)
			end 'kind'
			record = index.nextUseOf(record)
		end 'eachUse'
	end 'used'

	if index.defKindOf(v) != ValueDefKind.opDef 'noDefiningOp'
		return
	end 'noDefiningOp'
	refreshTransientAt(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: pressure, blockLocal: index.defBlockOf(v), slot: pressure.defSlotOf(v), sweep: sweep)
end 'refreshTransientsNaming'

// ==========================================================================
// THE THREE THINGS THE DIRTY-BLOCK SWEEP USED TO PRODUCE
// ==========================================================================
//
// `sweepBlockPressure` re-ran a whole block, op by op, to restate three facts about a split — and only
// one of them was ever about the block: the other two are about the handful of values the split moved.
//
//  • `forbidden[w]` for the victim and the ids it minted. A value must avoid every register written by
//    an op it is live ACROSS, which is a RANGE OR over the block's clobber masks — one `PressureIndex`
//    query per block of the value's range, not a walk of each.
//  • the block's DEAD-PHI count, which can only move for a block-arg whose liveness moved — i.e. only
//    when the VICTIM is a block-arg, and then only in its own block.
//  • the block's register DEMAND, which is `max(live-out, the block's peak) + its dead phis` and comes
//    off the positional index in O(1).
//
// The whole-function build still sweeps (`computeLivenessOn`); what is gone is the per-SPLIT sweep, and
// with it the `ScannedValues.split` scope the sweep took to narrow itself.

// Rebuild `forbidden[w]` from scratch, reading the clobbers of the ops `w` is live across as RANGE ORs.
//
// The three sources are exactly the three the sweep had:
//
//  1. everything that comes from no block at all (`nonSweptForbidOf`, which OWNS that list);
//  2. the IMPLICIT registers of every op `w` is an OPERAND of — `forbidOperandsFromImplicit`'s half,
//     which is about the op's own operands and applies whether or not `w` outlives it (an `idiv`'s
//     divisor dies at the op and must still stay out of RAX/RDX);
//  3. the CLOBBERS of every op `w` is live ACROSS — `applyForbidden`'s half.
function rebuildForbiddenOf(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, w ValueId, paramCross ParamCrossForbid, dirty BlockSet)
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	// SOURCE 1. Asked of `nonSweptForbidOf` rather than restated here, because `computeLivenessOn` asks
	// the same question of the same function — see its header for why the two may not be two lists. The
	// `establishedAtDef` half is restated for the dirty blocks by `refreshAfterSplit` before this runs.
	var mask = nonSweptForbidOf(w, paramCross: paramCross, establishedAtDef: liveness.establishedAtDef)

	// ⚠ AN EDGE USE IS NOT AN OPERAND, and source 2 is about operands — so an edge record is SKIPPED here,
	// exactly as `applyValueToPressure`, `refreshTransientsNaming` and `collectReindexCandidates` skip
	// one. Its record names the block's terminator as its sequence anchor, but the value is read by the
	// EDGE, and `forbidOperandsFromImplicit` walks that op's OPERAND LIST, which an edge arg is not in.
	//
	// What the terminator DOES reach it with is its CLOBBER, and that arrives through source 3: an
	// edge-passed value is live-out of its block by construction (`LivenessMarkScratch.walkValue` puts it
	// there for exactly this record), so `liveAcrossClobbersIn` runs its range to the block's exit and
	// takes the terminator in. Asking `implicitTouchOf` here as well would add the terminator's implicit
	// USES on top — which the sweep this reproduces never applies, and which no terminator carrying a
	// successor has today (`jmp`, `jcc` and both jump tables read 0). A mask this could only ever WIDEN,
	// with nothing but `VerifyIncrementalSplit` to notice.
	let useSites = index.useCount(w)
	if useSites > 0 'used'
		var record = index.firstUseOf(w)
		for _ in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
			match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
				edge then break 'kind'
				op then mask = mask or implicitTouchOf(module, opIdx: index.useOpAt(record), opFacts: liveness.opFacts)
			end 'kind'
			record = index.nextUseOf(record)
		end 'eachUse'
	end 'used'
	if index.defKindOf(w) == ValueDefKind.opDef 'definedByAnOp'
		mask = mask or implicitTouchOf(module, opIdx: index.requireDefOp(w), opFacts: liveness.opFacts)
	end 'definedByAnOp'

	// The DIRTY REGION is a superset of the blocks `w` is live in — the victim's whole range seeds it and
	// a fresh id lives only inside the block whose reload minted it — so walking it and skipping the
	// blocks `w` is not live in reaches every block that can contribute and no others.
	for k in 0 upto dirty.count() 'eachBlock'
		mask = mask or liveAcrossClobbersIn(liveness, pressure: pressure, w: w, blockLocal: dirty.at(k))
	end 'eachBlock'

	liveness.clearForbidden(w)
	liveness.addForbidden(w, mask: mask)
end 'rebuildForbiddenOf'

// The registers an op reads or writes WITHOUT naming them as an operand — what every explicit virtual
// operand of that op must avoid (`forbidOperandsFromImplicit`, stated per value instead of per op).
function implicitTouchOf(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex, opFacts OpVariantFacts) returns RegMask
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("implicitTouchOf: op {opIdx} is not in module.ops")
	return opFacts.implicitUsesOf(op) or opFacts.implicitDefsOf(op)
end 'implicitTouchOf'

// The stretch of `blockLocal`'s slots that `w` is live ACROSS, or `found: false` when it is live across
// nothing there.
//
// THE INTERVAL, and every one of its four endpoints is the same rule the sweep applied op by op:
//
//  • it OPENS after `w`'s defining op (a value is not live across the op that creates it, which is the
//    `operandDefinesValue` skip), or at the block's first slot when `w` arrives live — as a live-in
//    value or as a block-arg, both of which are live from the entry;
//  • it CLOSES at the block's exit when `w` is live-out, and otherwise one slot before `w`'s LAST use
//    here, because a value is not live across the op that reads it last.
//
// Free slots carry an empty clobber, so stating the ends in SLOTS rather than positions needs no
// rounding: an inserted op between two of them contributes nothing either way (see `nodeClobber`).
//
// It is stated APART from the OR over it because there are two questions about the same interval — what
// does the range cross (`liveAcrossClobbersIn`, which rebuilds a forbid mask) and WHICH op does it cross
// (`findConfiningClobber`, which picks a repair's cut). Derived twice, the mask a value's forbid is
// built from and the op a repair splits at could disagree about where the range runs, and the repair
// would cut where nothing was forbidden.
type LiveAcrossSpan
	export var found as bool
	export var lo as PressureSlot
	export var hi as PressureSlot

	static function create(found bool, lo PressureSlot, hi PressureSlot) returns LiveAcrossSpan
		return Self{found: found, lo: lo, hi: hi}
	end 'create'
end 'LiveAcrossSpan'

function liveAcrossSpanIn(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, w ValueId, blockLocal CfgIndex) returns LiveAcrossSpan
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	let live = liveness.live
	let definedHere = index.defKindOf(w) != ValueDefKind.none and index.defBlockOf(w) == blockLocal
	let liveOut = liveListHolds(live.liveOut(blockLocal), v: w)
	let lastUse = lastUsePositionIn(index, w: w, blockLocal: blockLocal)
	let empty = LiveAcrossSpan.create(false, lo: 0, hi: 0)

	if not (definedHere or liveOut or lastUse.found or liveListHolds(live.liveIn(blockLocal), v: w)) 'notLiveInThisBlockAtAll'
		return empty
	end 'notLiveInThisBlockAtAll'

	// A block-arg's def is the block's ENTRY, so it opens where a live-in value does; only an OP def
	// opens after itself.
	var lo = pressure.firstSlotOf(blockLocal)
	if definedHere and index.defKindOf(w) == ValueDefKind.opDef 'opensAfterItsOwnDef'
		lo = pressure.defSlotOf(w) + 1
	end 'opensAfterItsOwnDef'

	if liveOut 'liveOnEveryExit'
		return LiveAcrossSpan.create(true, lo: lo, hi: pressure.exitSlotOf(blockLocal))
	end 'liveOnEveryExit'
	if not lastUse.found 'readNowhereHereAndNotLiveOut'
		// Defined or live-in but never read here and not carried out: live across nothing.
		return empty
	end 'readNowhereHereAndNotLiveOut'

	let lastUseSlot = pressure.slotOfPosition(blockLocal, pos: lastUse.position)
	if lastUseSlot <= lo 'closesBeforeItOpens'
		return empty
	end 'closesBeforeItOpens'
	return LiveAcrossSpan.create(true, lo: lo, hi: lastUseSlot - 1)
end 'liveAcrossSpanIn'

// The OR of the clobbers of every op of `blockLocal` that `w` is live ACROSS — 0 when `w` is not live
// there at all.
function liveAcrossClobbersIn(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, w ValueId, blockLocal CfgIndex) returns RegMask
	let span = liveAcrossSpanIn(liveness, pressure: pressure, w: w, blockLocal: blockLocal)
	if not span.found 'liveAcrossNothingHere'
		return 0
	end 'liveAcrossNothingHere'
	return pressure.clobberOr(blockLocal, lo: span.lo, hi: span.hi)
end 'liveAcrossClobbersIn'

// The LAST position `w` is read at in `blockLocal`, or `found: false` when it is read there not at all.
// A value's use thread is ascending by sequence, so the last record in the block is the last one before
// it leaves — one walk, no search.
type BlockLastUse
	export var found as bool
	export var position as BlockOpPos

	static function create(found bool, position BlockOpPos) returns BlockLastUse
		return Self{found: found, position: position}
	end 'create'
end 'BlockLastUse'

function lastUsePositionIn(index UseIndex, w ValueId, blockLocal CfgIndex) returns BlockLastUse
	let useSites = index.useCount(w)
	var found = false
	var position = 0 as BlockOpPos
	if useSites == 0 'neverRead'
		return BlockLastUse.create(false, position: 0)
	end 'neverRead'

	var record = index.firstUseOf(w)
	for _ in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
		if index.useBlockAt(record) == blockLocal 'readHere'
			found = true
			position = index.usePosAt(record)
		end 'readHere'
		record = index.nextUseOf(record)
	end 'eachUse'
	return BlockLastUse.create(found, position: position)
end 'lastUsePositionIn'

// The DEAD block-arg count of the ONE block a split can move it in.
//
// A block-arg is dead iff nothing in its block reads it and it is not carried out — the same predicate
// `countDeadPhis` applies to the live-IN view its backward walk leaves, restated against the index and
// the live sets because there is no walk here to leave one. `verifyIncremental` compares the two counts
// after every split, which is what keeps the two statements of one rule together.
//
// ⚠ ONLY THE VICTIM CAN MOVE ONE, so only its block is restated: a fresh id is never a block-arg, and no
// other value's uses or liveness change.
function refreshDeadPhisFor(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, victim ValueId)
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	if not index.isPhiValue(victim) 'notABlockArg'
		return
	end 'notABlockArg'

	let blockLocal = index.defBlockOf(victim)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: liveness.cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'zeroRow'
		try liveness.deadPhis.set(classRowIndex(blockLocal, class: regClassAt(classOrdinal)), value: 0) otherwise panic("refreshDeadPhisFor: deadPhis.set OOB for block {blockLocal}")
	end 'zeroRow'

	for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
		if lastUsePositionIn(index, w: arg.valueId, blockLocal: blockLocal).found or liveListHolds(liveness.live.liveOut(blockLocal), v: arg.valueId) 'live'
			continue
		end 'live'
		let idx = classRowIndex(blockLocal, class: liveness.classOf(arg.valueId))
		try liveness.deadPhis.set(idx, value: (try liveness.deadPhis.get(idx) otherwise panic("refreshDeadPhisFor: deadPhis.get OOB")) + 1) otherwise panic("refreshDeadPhisFor: deadPhis.set OOB")
	end 'eachPhi'
end 'refreshDeadPhisFor'

// Each dirty block's register DEMAND, off the positional index: its peak simultaneous count of that file
// — which is `max(its live-out population, its peak op)`, the block-EXIT boundary being a program point
// the peak-finder does not visit — plus that file's dead-phi reservations.
function refreshBlockDemands(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, dirty BlockSet)
	for k in 0 upto dirty.count() 'eachDirtyBlock'
		let blockLocal = dirty.at(k)
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			var blockMax = pressure.blockLiveOut(blockLocal, class: class)
			let peak = pressure.blockPeak(blockLocal, class: class)
			if peak.found and peak.pressure > blockMax 'theOpsDemandMore'
				blockMax = peak.pressure
			end 'theOpsDemandMore'
			liveness.setBlockPressure(blockLocal, class: class, demand: (blockMax as RegCount) + liveness.deadPhiCountOf(blockLocal, class: class))
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'eachDirtyBlock'
end 'refreshBlockDemands'

// The smallest power of two >= `n` (and 1 for 0) — the run length a block's aligned subtree needs.
function roundUpToPowerOfTwo(n PressureSlot) returns PressureSlot
	var p = 1 as PressureSlot
	while p < n 'grow'
		p = p + p
	end 'grow'
	return p
end 'roundUpToPowerOfTwo'

// The smallest multiple of `runLength` that is >= `n`. A run length is a POWER OF TWO, so the rounding
// is a mask rather than a division — which is also why it needs no zero guard.
function roundUpToRunBoundary(n PressureSlot, runLength PressureSlot) returns PressureSlot
	let mask = runLength - 1
	return (n + mask) and not mask
end 'roundUpToRunBoundary'

// Every buffer the per-split pressure sweep and victim choice need, allocated ONCE per FUNCTION and
// reused by every iteration of the driver's loop. See the file header for why per-iteration
// allocation was the cost.
//
// THE RULE THAT MAKES REUSE SAFE IS ABOUT VALUES, NOT ABOUT ZEROES. Every buffer here is either fully
// re-filled over the extent that iteration reads (`Array.refill`, `EffectivePools.fillAll`), GROWN with
// its added entries explicitly initialised (`Array.growFilled`, `PressureSweepScratch.widenRow`), or
// `clear()`ed before its first push — so no reader ever inherits a value from the previous iteration.
//
// It is NOT, as this comment used to claim, that a lengthening `resize` hands back the previous
// occupant's bytes. It does not: THE CAPACITY-SLOT INVARIANT (stdlib/Internals.maxon) zeroes a slot on
// the way OUT of the live range, so what `resize` exposes always reads zero. Verified — see
// `zeroColumn` in TargetLiveness.maxon. The reason these buffers are refilled is that most of them want
// a NON-zero value in every entry (`NoStoreAnchorPressure`, a `numBlocks` "unset", an out-of-file
// register), which no invariant will hand you.
type SplitScratch
	// The pressure sweep's ONE dense working row, and the operand/op buffers it steps with — shared with
	// the liveness sweep it re-runs on a dirty block (`sweepBlockPressure`). The row is zero on entry to
	// a block and zero again on the way out, so it is never cleared wholesale.
	export var sweep as PressureSweepScratch
	export var seedBuf as ValueIdArray
	// Hall's machinery: the per-value effective-pool sizes (MAINTAINED across splits, never refilled),
	// the running census of the live set by that size, the census's observed-delta scratch, and the exact
	// confirmation's five columns.
	export var effPools as EffectivePools
	export var census as RegPoolCensus
	export var censusIds as ValueIdArray
	export var censusWasLive as BoolArray
	export var liveAtOp as ValueIdArray
	export var hall as HallScratch
	// Per-value def-point pressure, AS THE WALK DERIVES IT — and, since the positional index took over
	// answering `storeFitsPool`, a VERIFY-MODE column and nothing else: it is the independent second
	// derivation `verifyIncremental` checks the index against, and it is maintained only when that check
	// is compiled in. See `VerifyIncrementalSplit`.
	export var defLiveAfter as RegCountColumn
	// `chooseVictim`'s two buffers, plus the reachability memo — which, unlike everything else here,
	// keeps its CONTENTS across iterations (see `ReachCache`).
	export var usedAtPeak as BitWordArray
	export var candidates as ValueIdArray
	export var reach as ReachCache
	// The PEAK's live-before row: the row `materializePeak` fills and the `PressurePeak` it returns
	// POINTS AT — it is not copied out (see the file header for the cost of copying it).
	//
	// ⚠ SO IT HOLDS THE CURRENT ANALYSIS'S PEAK AND NOTHING ELSE, and that is the lifetime every reader
	// already respects: `chooseVictim`, the E5001 diagnostic and `verifyPeak` all read `peak.liveBefore`
	// within the iteration that produced it, and the driver replaces `peak` on the same line that
	// `refreshPeak` refills this. A reader that outlived that would see the NEXT peak's row.
	export var peakLiveBefore as BitWordArray
	// The incremental machinery: the per-block peak tournament, the POSITIONAL index under it, one
	// split's dirty region, its insertion log, and the memo its liveness re-walks use. All of them are
	// sized by `beginSplitting`, which the driver calls only when the function turns out to need a split
	// at all.
	export var tree as PeakTree
	export var pressure as PressureIndex
	export var relayout as PressureRelayoutScratch
	export var dirty as BlockSet
	export var edits as SplitEdits
	export var marks as LivenessMarkScratch
	// One op's per-class effective pressure, and one block's per-class LIVE-OUT population — caller-owned
	// rows the confined sweep and the positional index's repair write into, allocated once per function
	// like every other buffer here.
	//
	// (A third, one block's DEMAND, went with the per-split block sweep: the demand is now read off the
	// index in O(1) and written straight into `LivenessResult.blockPressure`, with no row in between.)
	export var opEffective as RegCountColumn
	export var exitPopulation as RegCountColumn
	// The ops one split's index rebuild has to re-read, as slots, and the merge buffer that sorts them.
	export var reindexCandidates as DenseColumn
	export var reindexSortBuf as DenseColumn
	// ONE block sweep's running argmax, mutated in place at every op that outranks it and copied out
	// once at the end (`PeakRank.snapshot`). A fresh `PeakRank` per winning op was Θ(block) heap objects
	// per block sweep — see the note on `PeakRank`'s mutators.
	export var rank as PeakRank
	// THE MUTATORS' buffers: one flag per use site of the victim (does that use start a fresh reload —
	// `markRunStarts`), and the operand list the use-rewriter fills. Both used to be allocated per
	// SPLIT (`OperandList.create()` inside `spillOneValue` / `rematerializeConstant`), which is the same
	// per-split allocation term the rest of this type exists to remove.
	export var runStarts as BoolArray
	export var splitOperands as OperandList
	// WHERE THE SPLIT IN FLIGHT CUTS — one object per FUNCTION, re-pointed at each cut. See
	// `SplitAnchor`, and `PeakRank` for the discipline it follows.
	export var anchor as SplitAnchor
	// Has this FUNCTION left full-pool mode? Set from the whole-function analysis and flipped at most
	// once, by `refreshPeak`, which is where the whole story lives. Like `ReachCache`'s rows and unlike
	// every buffer above, it CARRIES ACROSS splits — it is a fact about the function, not scratch.
	export var confinedMode as bool

	static function create(numBlocks CfgIndex) returns SplitScratch
		return Self{sweep: PressureSweepScratch.create(), seedBuf: ValueIdArray.create(), effPools: EffectivePools.create(), census: RegPoolCensus.create(), censusIds: ValueIdArray.create(), censusWasLive: BoolArray.create(), liveAtOp: ValueIdArray.create(), hall: HallScratch.create(), defLiveAfter: RegCountColumn.create(), usedAtPeak: BitWordArray.create(), candidates: ValueIdArray.create(), reach: ReachCache.create(numBlocks), peakLiveBefore: BitWordArray.create(), tree: PeakTree.create(), pressure: PressureIndex.create(), relayout: PressureRelayoutScratch.create(), dirty: BlockSet.create(), edits: SplitEdits.create(), marks: LivenessMarkScratch.create(), opEffective: zeroClassRow(), exitPopulation: zeroClassRow(), reindexCandidates: DenseColumn.create(), reindexSortBuf: DenseColumn.create(), rank: emptyRank(), runStarts: BoolArray.create(), splitOperands: OperandList.create(), anchor: SplitAnchor.create(), confinedMode: false}
	end 'create'

	// Size everything the incremental update needs and BUILD the positional pressure index. Deferred
	// until the function turns out to need a SPLIT — the overwhelming majority never do, and an eager
	// allocation on each of these would be paid to serve the few that spill (the same discipline as
	// `ReachCache`).
	export function beginSplitting(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult)
		let numBlocks = liveness.cfg.topo.numBlocks
		self.dirty.begin(numBlocks)
		self.marks.begin(numBlocks)
		self.pressure.rebuild(module, liveness: liveness, sweep: self.sweep, seedBuf: self.seedBuf, scratch: self.relayout)
	end 'beginSplitting'
end 'SplitScratch'

// ==========================================================================
// The driver
// ==========================================================================

// ONE FUNCTION'S SPLITTING STATE, held in a type because there are now TWO callers and they run at
// DIFFERENT TIMES.
//
// Every field below was a local of `splitLiveRanges`, which was right while relief happened exactly
// once, BEFORE colouring. The colorer's own repair (`repairAtExhaustion`) runs AFTER it, and it must run
// under the SAME state rather than a second copy — two of these are two of every invariant they carry:
//
//  ⭐⭐ **`nextSlot` IS A HIGH-WATER MARK ACROSS EVERY SPLIT THE FUNCTION EVER MAKES.** A repair that
//     restarted slot numbering would hand a value a slot the pressure splitter had already given to
//     another; the two stores would alias and one value's spill would silently overwrite the other's.
//     (v1 keeps `spillBaseOffset` across ALL its spill rounds for exactly this reason —
//     `maxon-selfhosted/.../RegisterAllocator.maxon:980-986`. Its shape is a reactive spill loop that
//     shv2 deliberately does not copy; the SLOT trap is the part that is about frames, not about
//     algorithms, and it applies here unchanged.)
//  • `lineage` maps every fresh id back to its source for the E5001 diagnostic; a second one loses every
//    id the first minted.
//  • `scratch` holds the positional pressure index, the per-block peak tournament, the reachability memo
//    and every reusable buffer. Rebuilding it per repair is the whole-function cost the incremental
//    design exists to remove.
//  • `nextValueId` is the id space itself, and `cfg.valueCount` follows it.
//
// `module`, `func` and `counters` stay ARGUMENTS, as they are everywhere else in this file: they are the
// compile's, not this function's, and holding them here would be a second name for each.
export type SplitDriver
	export var cfg as FuncCfg
	export var liveness as LivenessResult
	var scratch as SplitScratch
	var lineage as SplitLineage
	var paramCross as ParamCrossForbid
	var pool as RegMask
	var file as RegisterFile
	var nextValueId as ValueId
	var nextSlot as SpillSlot
	// Has the incremental machinery been built? See `buildSplitIndex`.
	var indexBuilt as bool

	// THE CFG IS INVARIANT UNDER SPLITTING, so it is built ONCE and reused by every liveness
	// recomputation. A split only ever inserts `storeSlotReg`/`loadRegSlot`/`movRegImm` BODY ops,
	// rewrites operands, and repoints branch-edge ARGS — it never adds or removes a block, never changes
	// a `jcc`/`jmp` target, and so cannot change the block set, the successor/predecessor edges, or the
	// loop depths. Rebuilding it per split re-scanned the whole function, re-derived both CSR graphs, and
	// re-ran the natural-loop walk once per loop header — a per-split cost proportional to the function
	// that bought nothing. `valueCount` is the ONE thing that changes (fresh reload/remat ids), and the
	// driver knows it exactly: every minted id is materialized by an op it inserts, so `nextValueId` IS
	// what a rescan would find.
	//
	// The SKELETON is not even built here: `reorderFuncBlocksRpo` just built it (and it is the caller's
	// `topo` argument), and it has been valid ever since — the reorder is the last pass that touches the
	// block list. So all that is added on top is the loop depths and the value count, which the reorder
	// had no use for and therefore never paid for.
	//
	// The ONE liveness build happens here too. Everything after it is an incremental repair of the region
	// a split dirtied (`refreshAfterSplit`) — the whole-function rebuild this used to run per split was
	// the O(function × splits) quadratic.
	export static function create(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, topo FuncTopology, pool RegMask, file RegisterFile, valueClass ValueClassColumn, opFacts OpVariantFacts, costs RegAllocCosts) returns SplitDriver
		var cfg = buildFuncCfg(module, func: func, topo: topo)
		let liveness = timedLiveness(module, cfg: cfg, func: func, valueClass: valueClass, file: file, opFacts: opFacts, costs: costs)
		// The one contribution to `forbidden` that does not come from a block sweep, so the one the
		// dirty-region rebuild has to re-apply by hand when the victim is a parameter. Resolved once.
		let paramCross = ParamCrossForbid.resolve(module, topo: cfg.topo, paramCount: func.paramCount)
		// One set of working buffers for every split — the sweep and the victim choice allocate nothing
		// per split (see `SplitScratch`). Sized off the CFG, which splitting cannot change.
		// `lineage` is the fresh-id -> source-value map the E5001 diagnostic chases a blocking reload id
		// back through (see `SplitLineage`).
		// ⭐⭐ **THE SPILL SLOTS START ABOVE THE PROMOTED-RECORD REGION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHAT KEEPS
		// STACK PROMOTION SAFE.** `promoteStackRecords` (Std tier) reserves `func.stackSize` bytes at the
		// bottom of the slot grid for records it has proven cannot outlive the frame, and each such record's
		// address is a `leaRegSlot` naming a slot in `[0, stackSize/8)`. Seeded at 0, the very first spill
		// this function makes would be handed slot 0 — the same eight bytes a live record occupies — and the
		// spill would overwrite the record's first field with an unrelated value. Not a diagnostic: a wrong
		// ANSWER, in a function nothing about either the record or the spill names.
		//
		// It is `stackSize` and not a field of its own because `stackSize` already MEANS "bytes of slot area
		// reserved so far" (see IrFunction), and `fixFrameSize` writes the final total back through the same
		// counter — so the promoted region is reserved, the spills are placed above it, and the frame is
		// sized for both, from ONE number. Zero for every function the promotion pass did not touch.
		return Self{cfg: cfg, liveness: liveness, scratch: SplitScratch.create(cfg.topo.numBlocks), lineage: SplitLineage.create(), paramCross: paramCross, pool: pool, file: file, nextValueId: cfg.valueCount, nextSlot: (func.stackSize / SpillSlotBytes) as SpillSlot, indexBuilt: false}
	end 'create'

	// Build the incremental machinery — the positional pressure index and the per-block peak tournament
	// `refreshPeak` reads its answers off — and hand back the peak its seeding pass found.
	//
	// TWO whole-function walks, so it is deferred until the function turns out to need a split at all
	// (the overwhelming majority never do) and run at most ONCE. It now has TWO triggers rather than one:
	// the relief loop's first over-pool point, and a colorer repair on a function that never had one.
	function buildSplitIndex(module TargetModule) returns PressurePeak
		if self.indexBuilt 'alreadyBuilt'
			panic("SplitDriver.buildSplitIndex: the incremental machinery is already built — `beginSplitting` re-sizes and re-fills every column and the tournament seeding is a whole-function sweep, so a second call would be a silent Θ(function) cost. Both callers guard on `indexBuilt`.")
		end 'alreadyBuilt'
		self.scratch.beginSplitting(module, liveness: self.liveness)
		self.indexBuilt = true
		return analyzePressure(module, liveness: self.liveness, pool: self.pool, scratch: self.scratch, intoTree: true)
	end 'buildSplitIndex'

	// Split this function's live ranges so the colorer finds every program point within its pool.
	// Mutates the IR (inserts spill/reload/remat ops, rewrites reloaded uses to fresh values) and leaves
	// `self.liveness` current on the split IR, `maxPressure ≤ pool` guaranteed.
	//
	// `throws RegAllocRefusal` because a HOT overflow — a program point over its pool where NO crossing
	// value is cold-spillable — is E5001, not a placeholder panic. It is not RENDERED here: the
	// diagnostic reads `project.opRanges` / `valueOrigins` to map each blocking Target ValueId back to
	// its source span, and this runs where there is no `Project` (see `RegAllocRefusalSite`). What the
	// refusal leaves behind is this driver and the peak, in `refusal`.
	export function relievePressure(module TargetModule, func IrFunction, hasAuthor bool, counters RegAllocCounters, refusal RegAllocRefusalSite) throws RegAllocRefusal
		var work = self.scratch
		var funcLiveness = self.liveness

		// TERMINATION. Each iteration splits one value at the worst OVER-POOL op: the victim goes to
		// memory ACROSS that op (dead there), so the op's pressure drops by exactly one, while NO other
		// op's pressure rises (a store/reload only renames an already-live value over a region it was
		// live in anyway — it never lifts the function's max pressure). So the potential
		// Φ = Σ_ops max(0, effectivePressure(op) − reducedPool(op)) — a non-negative integer — STRICTLY
		// DECREASES every iteration and the loop MUST reach Φ = 0 (no over-pool op → `peak.found` false).
		// This holds under MULTIPLE splits per lineage too: a reload is itself an ordinary value the next
		// iteration may split again (via a fresh id), each such split cutting a strictly smaller run of
		// uses. The bound below is what turns a hypothetical non-terminating splitter bug into a loud
		// panic rather than a hang.
		let iterationBound = splitRunawayBound(funcLiveness)
		var iterations = 0 as EventCount

		// `analyzePressure` finds the worst OVER-POOL point — an op whose effective pressure exceeds
		// its OWN pool (the full pool for a normal op, the reduced `pool ∖ implicitDefs` at a fixed-
		// register point), with the same reuse-transient + dead-phi corrections `maxPressure` carries
		// so the peak-finder and this driver agree. `peak.found == false` means NO point overflows —
		// the colorer will succeed. It runs over the whole function; every re-analysis below re-sweeps only
		// the blocks the split it followed actually dirtied (`refreshPeak`).
		//
		// The FIRST pass does not build the peak tournament, because the overwhelming majority of functions
		// stop right here — no point overflows, nothing is split, and the incremental machinery is never
		// needed. Only a function that turns out to split pays the second pass that seeds it.
		var peak = analyzePressure(module, liveness: funcLiveness, pool: self.pool, scratch: work, intoTree: false)
		// Has the function left FULL-POOL mode? The whole-function analysis above swept BOTH halves, so the
		// answer is readable straight off its peak: anything but a full-pool overflow means no op in the
		// function exceeds its own pool, and by the no-pressure-rise premise none ever will (`refreshPeak`).
		work.confinedMode = not (peak.found and not peak.clobberOnly)
		if peak.found 'willSplit'
			peak = self.buildSplitIndex(module)
		end 'willSplit'

		while peak.found 'relieve'
			if iterations >= iterationBound 'runaway'
				panic("SplitDriver.relievePressure: '{String.from(func.name)}' did not converge after {iterations} splits (peak effective pressure {peak.pressure} still over its pool) — a splitter bug")
			end 'runaway'
			iterations = iterations + 1

			let index = funcLiveness.useIndex
			// THE POINT THIS ITERATION ACTUALLY RELIEVES. Normally the analysis's own peak; but a FULL-POOL
			// peak with no relievable value is not yet an E5001 — the same op may carry a CONFINED overflow
			// the ranking discarded, and that one is always relievable by the bracket the ABI has already
			// chosen (`confinedOverflowAtPeak`). Both peaks name the SAME op, so the anchor, the live row and
			// the sequence number below are the same either way; only the placement and the witness differ.
			//
			// ⚠ **THE CONFINED PEAK REPLACES `peak` RATHER THAN BEING ALIASED BESIDE IT.** A second name for
			// the same peak (`var target = peak`) MOVES the loop-carried value out of the loop, which is
			// E2015; and the alias bought nothing, because the full-pool peak is not read again on the path
			// that takes the confined one. The path that does read it — the unrelievable retry below — keeps
			// it by never reassigning. Discarding the ranked peak here is safe: `PressureAnchor.takePeak`
			// copied the three scalars it needs out of it, so nothing holds its live row.
			var choice = chooseVictim(module, index: index, liveness: funcLiveness, peak: peak, pool: self.pool, scratch: work, placement: placementOf(peak))
			if (not choiceFound(choice)) and (not peak.clobberOnly) 'theAbiHasNotFinishedYet'
				let confined = confinedOverflowAtPeak(funcLiveness, peak: peak, pool: self.pool, scratch: work)
				if confined.found 'dischargeTheForcedWorkFirst'
					let bracketed = chooseVictim(module, index: index, liveness: funcLiveness, peak: confined, pool: self.pool, scratch: work, placement: placementOf(confined))
					if choiceFound(bracketed) 'relievable'
						peak = confined
						choice = bracketed
					end 'relievable' else 'nothingThereEither'
						// The confined retry re-pointed the function's one anchor; put it back on the peak the
						// E5001 below must describe. Falling back to the FULL-POOL peak rather than letting the
						// confined one reach `victimAtPeak` is deliberate: an unrelievable confined peak panics as
						// a splitter bug, and this one was synthesized here rather than found by the analysis, so
						// it may not be held to that invariant. The honest report is the overflow that was ranked.
						work.anchor.takePeak(peak, cutPlacement: placementOf(peak))
					end 'nothingThereEither'
				end 'dischargeTheForcedWorkFirst'
			end 'theAbiHasNotFinishedYet'

			// ⭐⭐ **E5001 IS AN INSTRUCTION TO AN AUTHOR, AND A FUNCTION THE COMPILER EMITTED HAS NONE.**
			// A full-pool overflow is REFUSED rather than relieved because the only relief that works inside a
			// loop is a reload before every use, and paying that per iteration behind the author's back is what
			// this design will not do silently (`isColdSpillable`). That argument ends where the author does.
			// A function with no AUTHOR was never parsed — the runtime's own scaffolding and
			// the synthesized builtin bodies — so there is no line to name, no value the author can see and
			// nothing to rewrite. The diagnostic cannot even be BUILT for one: every route `defRangeOf` chases
			// ends in a table only the parser fills, so it panics on the first blocking value (RULE 3). Here the
			// bracket is therefore the only home left, for the reason `repairAtExhaustion`'s cure (b) states
			// rather than the ABI's: the alternative is a compiler failure on code that fits the machine
			// everywhere else, and this code is the compiler's own.
			//
			// MEASURED (`sleep(10)` in `main`, x64-windows, `d62e05459e`): `__gt_timer_check`'s pop-min walk
			// holds 18 GPRs at a point inside its loop against a pool of 14, and every one of the 18 is defined
			// or read inside that loop, so the cold gate refused all of them — and no program using a green
			// thread could be compiled at all.
			//
			// STRICTLY ADDITIVE. It runs only where `chooseVictim` has ALREADY answered `none`, which is
			// precisely the state that used to reach E5001, so no program that compiles today can take a
			// different decision because of it. Termination is unchanged: the forced bracket makes the victim
			// dead across the peak exactly as the cold split does (`splitScopeAt` picks which uses it rewrites),
			// so the potential Φ still falls by at least one every iteration.
			if (not choiceFound(choice)) and not hasAuthor 'noAuthorToRefuse'
				choice = chooseVictim(module, index: index, liveness: funcLiveness, peak: peak, pool: self.pool, scratch: work, placement: SpillPlacement.forced)
			end 'noAuthorToRefuse'

			// A `none` choice does not come back: at a FULL-pool peak it is the E5001 this design exists to
			// raise, and at a CONFINED one it is a splitter bug (`noVictimAtPeak`).
			let victim = try victimAtPeak(choice, refusal: refusal, func: func, hasAuthor: hasAuthor, liveness: funcLiveness, peak: peak, pool: self.pool, lineage: self.lineage)

			// `chooseVictim` re-pointed the function's ONE anchor at this peak, and the mutation below cuts
			// there. Asserted rather than assumed: the two are a few lines apart today, and a reorder that
			// broke it would split at the PREVIOUS peak — a wrong cut in the right-looking place, not a crash.
			if work.anchor.seq != peak.peakSeq 'anchorIsNotThisPeaks'
				panic("SplitDriver.relievePressure: the split anchor is at sequence {work.anchor.seq} but this iteration's peak is at {peak.peakSeq} — `chooseVictim` re-points the function's one anchor and the mutation reads it back, so nothing may run between them that re-points it")
			end 'anchorIsNotThisPeaks'

			// Only a SPILL of a value memory does not already hold consumes a slot: a value relieved again
			// at a second peak reloads out of the slot it is already resident in (see `slotForSpillOf`), and
			// so does a RELOAD id relieved in its turn. Read BEFORE `beginSplit`, which is what bumps the
			// supply, and before the mutation, which is where `isSlotResident` would start answering about
			// the store this iteration is about to insert.
			let slot = slotForSpillOf(index, valueId: victim, nextSlot: self.nextSlot)
			let takesSlot = match choice 'countSlot'
				none to remat gives false
				spill gives true
			end 'countSlot'
			self.beginSplit(index, victim: victim, takesSlot: takesSlot)

			let freshFrom = self.nextValueId
			match choice 'act'
				none then panic("SplitDriver.relievePressure: `victimAtPeak` returned for a `none` choice — it must throw")
				remat(v, scope) then self.nextValueId = rematerializeConstant(module, index: index, topo: self.cfg.topo, valueId: v, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, scope: scope, nextValueId: self.nextValueId, counters: counters, lineage: self.lineage, scratch: work, opFacts: funcLiveness.opFacts)
				spill(v, scope) then self.nextValueId = spillOneValue(module, index: index, cfg: self.cfg, liveness: funcLiveness, valueId: v, anchor: work.anchor, scope: scope, slot: slot, pool: self.pool, nextValueId: self.nextValueId, counters: counters, lineage: self.lineage, scratch: work)
			end 'act'

			self.commitSplit(module, victim: victim, freshFrom: freshFrom)
			peak = refreshPeak(module, liveness: funcLiveness, pool: self.pool, scratch: work)

			if VerifyIncrementalSplit 'checkAgainstAFullRebuild'
				verifyIncremental(module, cfg: self.cfg, func: func, liveness: funcLiveness, peak: peak, scratch: work, pool: self.pool, iterations: iterations)
			end 'checkAgainstAFullRebuild'
		end 'relieve'

		// The per-block demands are current (every dirtied block was re-swept); the function's max is the
		// max over them, taken ONCE here rather than re-folded on every split.
		funcLiveness.refreshMaxPressure()

		// Post-condition (design Phase 4.6): after splitting, NO program point overflows its pool. The
		// loop exits precisely on `not peak.found`, which is that guarantee for BOTH the full pool (normal
		// ops) and every reduced pool (fixed-register points). `liveness.maxPressure` is an INDEPENDENT
		// cross-check of the full-pool half — it comes from TargetLiveness's backward sweep, a different
		// computation than `analyzePressure`, and it additionally counts the block-exit boundary the
		// peak-finder does not visit — so a full-pool overflow the peak-finder somehow missed still fails
		// here rather than as a colorer panic. (The reduced-pool half has no separate maxPressure analogue;
		// the loop's `analyzePressure`-based exit is its guarantee.)
		//
		// ⚠ IT IS NOT "THE COLORER CANNOT FAIL", AND IT NEVER WAS. Pointwise feasibility does not imply a
		// GREEDY colouring exists — see `HallCondition.hallVerdictAt`. A value the colorer cannot place on
		// a point this guarantees is a CONFINEMENT, and `repairAtExhaustion` relieves it.
		//
		// PER CLASS, because the files are independent and a mixed figure against a mixed pool is the
		// comparison that made this guard stop firing at all: with 30 registers nominally available, 15
		// live ints never trip `15 > 30`, and the colorer panics instead. Each file is checked against
		// ITS OWN pool.
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			let classPoolSize = classPoolSizeOf(self.pool, class: class, file: self.file)
			if funcLiveness.maxPressureOf(class) > classPoolSize 'residual'
				panic("SplitDriver.relievePressure: '{String.from(func.name)}' finished with {class.name} maxPressure {funcLiveness.maxPressureOf(class)} still > the {class.name} pool {classPoolSize} — the splitter's post-condition (every point within its pool, per register file) was violated")
			end 'residual'
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'relievePressure'

	// The slot a spill of `victim` takes out of the function's supply — none at all when memory already
	// holds it. Read from the PRE-rebuild index, so it must be called before `commitSplit`.
	function consumeSlotFor(index UseIndex, victim ValueId)
		if index.isSlotResident(victim) 'memoryAlreadyHoldsIt'
			return
		end 'memoryAlreadyHoldsIt'
		self.nextSlot = self.nextSlot + 1
	end 'consumeSlotFor'

	// ONE split's PROLOGUE — everything the incremental update must take from the PRE-SPLIT world,
	// before a single op moves. It mirrors `commitSplit`, and it is one function for the same reason:
	// three callers make the same mutation and owe the same preparation.
	//
	// ⛔ IT IS ALSO AN ORDER, NOT JUST A LIST, AND THE ORDER WAS THE DUPLICATION WORTH REMOVING. Written
	// out at each call site the two copies drifted into two different step orders, which was sound only
	// because `UseIndex` is not rebuilt until `refreshAfterSplit` — so `isSlotResident` answers the same
	// before and after the mutation. The day that stops being true, the symptom is TWO VALUES SHARING A
	// SPILL SLOT: one store silently overwriting the other, no crash, wrong answer.
	//
	// `takesSlot` is false for a REMAT, which writes no memory and consumes nothing. The caller decides
	// it from the relief CHOICE, because that is where the two kinds are already told apart.
	//
	// ⚠ `slotForSpillOf` MUST BE READ BEFORE THIS RUNS: this bumps `nextSlot`, and the slot the split
	// writes into is the value BEFORE the bump.
	function beginSplit(index UseIndex, victim ValueId, takesSlot bool)
		self.scratch.dirty.clear()
		self.scratch.edits.clear()
		if takesSlot 'aSpillNeedsAHome'
			self.consumeSlotFor(index, victim: victim)
		end 'aSpillNeedsAHome'
		unmarkVictim(self.cfg.topo, index: index, liveness: self.liveness, scratch: self.scratch, victim: victim)
	end 'beginSplit'

	// ONE split's mutation, committed: the id space the mutators grew, and the incremental repair of
	// every derived structure. Both callers make the same mutation and owe the same repair, so it is
	// stated once — written twice, a second caller would be one `growValueSpace` or one
	// `refreshAfterSplit` short and the failure would be a wrong colour at a call, not a crash.
	//
	// The mutators minted fresh value ids beginning at `freshFrom` and left `self.nextValueId` past the
	// last; every such id is DEFINED by an op they inserted, so it is exactly what a rescan of the
	// function would report as the new value count. Publishing it on the (reused) CFG is what re-sizes
	// every value-indexed column.
	function commitSplit(module TargetModule, victim ValueId, freshFrom ValueId)
		self.cfg.valueCount = self.nextValueId
		let split = SplitValues.create(victim, freshFrom: freshFrom, freshTo: self.nextValueId)
		refreshAfterSplit(module, cfg: self.cfg, liveness: self.liveness, scratch: self.scratch, split: split, pool: self.pool, paramCross: self.paramCross)
	end 'commitSplit'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO CURES FOR A VALUE THE COLORER COULD NOT PLACE, AND WHY THERE ARE EXACTLY TWO.**
	//
	// The point is NOT over-pool — `relievePressure` has already driven its Φ to 0 and Hall's exact
	// verdict there is FEASIBLE (`reportExhaustion` runs it). What failed is the COLOURING ORDER: an
	// earlier def took a register out of `v`'s admissible set while the caller-saved half was momentarily
	// full, and a colour once given is held for the value's whole range. Greedy is not exact for this
	// constraint family and no static order can be — `HallCondition.hallVerdictAt` is where that is
	// argued, and it is argued in exactly one place on purpose.
	//
	// Exhaustion means `classPool ⊆ held ∪ forbidden(v)`, and reading that ONE fact in its two directions
	// gives the two cures, which between them are TOTAL:
	//
	//  (a) `free = classPool ∖ held` is a subset of `forbidden(v)` — a register is FREE and `v` is merely
	//      not allowed it. WIDEN `A(v)`: cut `v` across a clobber in its own live range, which removes
	//      that clobber (and every clobber after it) from its forbidden mask. `repairAtExhaustion`.
	//  (b) `A(v) = classPool ∖ forbidden(v)` is a subset of `held` — every register `v` IS allowed is
	//      taken. FREE ONE: cut an OCCUPANT of one of them across `v`'s own def point, so it is dead
	//      there and `v` can take the register it was allowed all along.
	//      `repairByEvictingOccupant`.
	//
	// **(b) always has a candidate**, which is what makes the pair total: `A(v)` cannot be empty (a value
	// allowed no register cannot be matched, so Hall would have said OVERFLOW), and every register in it
	// is held by a live value. So "nothing can be done" is not a state this can reach — see
	// `repairStuckValues`, whose panic now stands only for a contradiction.
	//
	// ⭐ **TERMINATION — ONE potential for both cures.** Φ = Σ_v |{ ops `v` is live ACROSS that are not
	// themselves the `storeSlotReg`/`loadRegSlot` a split SPLICES }|. Two properties make it work:
	//
	//  • NO value's count can RISE. The only ops a split adds are spill ops, which this does not count;
	//    the victim's range only shrinks; and each reload's range is a subset of the victim's old one,
	//    with the runs pairwise disjoint.
	//  • The CUT op goes from crossed to un-crossed, so the count FALLS by at least one. The victim's new
	//    range ends before the cut and every reload is born at a use strictly after it — which is why
	//    both cures REFUSE a victim the cut op READS: the reload would take over that use and be live
	//    across the cut in the victim's place, and Φ would not move.
	//
	// Cure (a)'s cut is a CLOBBER op, and no spill op clobbers anything, so its cut is always counted.
	// ⚠ Cure (b)'s cut is the stuck value's own DEF op, which IS a spill op in one shape — a stuck value
	// that is itself a RELOAD. Φ is then non-increasing rather than strictly decreasing, and the round
	// bound in `colorRepairingConfinements` is what makes that loud instead of infinite. That is the one
	// gap in the argument and it is written here rather than left for a reader to find.
	//
	// A split also never raises peak pressure, so `relievePressure`'s own Φ = 0 survives every repair —
	// asserted after each one.
	export function repairAtExhaustion(module TargetModule, v ValueId, free RegMask, counters RegAllocCounters) returns bool
		var work = self.scratch
		var funcLiveness = self.liveness
		let index = funcLiveness.useIndex
		self.buildSplitIndexOnce(module)

		// `v`'s live REGION, as blocks. `walkValue` in the ADDING direction over a value that is ALREADY
		// marked is a no-op on the live sets (`LiveSets.addLiveIn`/`addLiveOut` are idempotent) and fills
		// `touched` with exactly the blocks the value occupies — so the search below is bounded by the
		// live range rather than by the function, and it reuses THE liveness walk instead of a second
		// copy of it. It must run BEFORE `beginSplit`, whose walk clears the very live sets the span
		// endpoints are read from.
		work.dirty.clear()
		collectVictimRegion(self.cfg.topo, index: index, liveness: funcLiveness, scratch: work, victim: v, adding: true)

		let classPool = poolOfClass(self.pool, class: funcLiveness.classOf(v), file: funcLiveness.registerFile)
		let cut = findConfiningClobber(module, liveness: funcLiveness, scratch: work, v: v, free: free, classPool: classPool)
		if not cut.found 'nothingToSplitAround'
			// `v` crosses no clobber that takes a register of its own file, so its confinement is entirely
			// non-swept (`ParamCrossForbid` / `establishedAtDef`) or comes from an op that names it as an
			// OPERAND. Cure (a) has nothing to cut; cure (b) still does.
			return false
		end 'nothingToSplitAround'

		work.anchor.takeForcedCutAt(cut.blockLocal, pos: cut.pos)
		self.applyRepairSplit(module, victim: v, counters: counters)
		// Counted HERE and not in the shared tail: WHICH cure ran is the one thing the two callers know
		// and `applyRepairSplit` does not, and keeping the two counts apart is the whole point of having
		// two (see `RegAllocStats.repairedByEviction`).
		counters.repairedByWidening = counters.repairedByWidening + 1
		return true
	end 'repairAtExhaustion'

	// ⭐⭐ **CURE (b): FREE A REGISTER `v` IS ALREADY ALLOWED, by cutting the OCCUPANT of it across `v`'s
	// own def point.** See `repairAtExhaustion`'s header for why this always has a candidate and for the
	// shared termination argument.
	//
	// The anchor is the FAILING POINT — `v`'s def — because that is the one place the register has to be
	// free. `w` is live across it (it held a register there), so this is exactly the shape `spillOneValue`
	// already handles, and afterwards `w` is dead there and `v` takes the register.
	//
	// ⚠ **`SpillPlacement.forced`, AND THE JUSTIFICATION IS NARROWER THAN A CLOBBER'S — DO NOT CLAIM THE
	// ABI FORCES IT, BECAUSE IT DOES NOT.** At a clobber the callee destroys the register and memory is
	// the only home there is. Here the register is merely TAKEN, and what makes memory the only home is
	// that the alternative is a compiler panic on a program that fits the machine. That is a good enough
	// reason and it is a different reason, so it is written down as one — including that it lets the
	// bracket land inside a loop, which is a cost this cure pays and a clobber's does not.
	//
	// ⚠ **WHICH occupant is ARBITRARY, and must stay arbitrary.** `StuckValues` records them in ascending
	// register order and the caller offers them in that order, so the choice is reproducible run to run;
	// beyond that it is not a cost model, and it must never grow into an eviction tournament — that is the
	// search this allocator exists to refuse (`register-pressure.md`). Ranking these by next-use would be
	// exactly that search, arriving through a back door.
	//
	// Returns false when `w` is not usable at that point: the def op READS it (evicting it there frees
	// nothing — the reload would hold the register at the very op), or `w` is not live across the point at
	// all because an earlier repair in the same round already moved it. The caller then offers the next
	// occupant.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **NO SPEC REACHES THIS, AND `RegAllocStats.repairedByEviction` READS ZERO. THAT IS
	// NOT-CONSTRUCTIBLE-TODAY, NOT UNREACHABLE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE OF THIS NOTE.** Cure (a)
	// runs first and relieves everything the corpus produces; this path is proven live only by DISABLING
	// cure (a), which leaves the whole suite green on this cure alone. What could not be built is a
	// program that reaches it with cure (a) still enabled.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE REASON IS THAT THE MECHANISM CAUSING A5g WORKS AGAINST REPRODUCING ITS RESIDUE.** The one
	// forbid family that confines a value without any clobber in its live range is `ParamCrossForbid` —
	// a parameter capture forbidden its SIBLINGS' incoming registers. To reach here it would have to be
	// stuck with all eleven registers it IS allowed held and the three sibling registers left FREE. But
	// those three are CALLER-saved, and `preferredVolatilityMask` steers every unconstrained value into
	// the caller-saved half FIRST — so the greedy fills exactly the registers the construction needs
	// empty. `TargetLiveness.maxon:3464-3477` records the same wall from the other side: a detector
	// found seven programs reaching the sibling shape, every one compiler-SYNTHESIZED, because
	// `E3012 unused variable` forbids the source-level route.
	//
	// ⇒ A future rung that changes the volatility preference, the parameter forbid, or the unused-
	// parameter rule should re-try the construction. Until one does, the zero counter is the standing
	// record that this cure is UNEXERCISED by the suite.
	export function repairByEvictingOccupant(module TargetModule, v ValueId, w ValueId, counters RegAllocCounters) returns bool
		var work = self.scratch
		var funcLiveness = self.liveness
		self.buildSplitIndexOnce(module)

		if w == v 'itsOwnOccupant'
			panic("SplitDriver.repairByEvictingOccupant: value {v} is recorded as the occupant of a register it is ALLOWED, but the register it was given provisionally is drawn from the FREE set and `A(v)` is disjoint from that — so a stuck value can never hold one of its own allowed registers")
		end 'itsOwnOccupant'

		let point = self.failingPointOf(module, v: v)
		if point.readsOccupant(module, w: w, scratch: work) 'theDefItselfReadsIt'
			return false
		end 'theDefItselfReadsIt'

		// Is `w` still live ACROSS the point? It was when the colouring pass recorded it, but an earlier
		// repair in this same round may since have cut it. Asked through the SAME span the forbid masks are
		// rebuilt from, so "live across" has one definition here and there.
		let span = liveAcrossSpanIn(funcLiveness, pressure: work.pressure, w: w, blockLocal: point.blockLocal)
		let pointSlot = work.pressure.slotOfPosition(point.blockLocal, pos: point.pos)
		if not (span.found and span.lo <= pointSlot and pointSlot <= span.hi) 'noLongerLiveAcrossThePoint'
			return false
		end 'noLongerLiveAcrossThePoint'

		work.anchor.takeForcedCutAt(point.blockLocal, pos: point.pos)
		self.applyRepairSplit(module, victim: w, counters: counters)
		counters.repairedByEviction = counters.repairedByEviction + 1
		return true
	end 'repairByEvictingOccupant'

	// The incremental machinery, built on FIRST use. A function the relief loop never split has no
	// positional index and no tournament, and every repair needs both.
	function buildSplitIndexOnce(module TargetModule)
		if self.indexBuilt 'alreadyBuilt'
			return
		end 'alreadyBuilt'

		// The peak this seeding sweep finds must be EMPTY: `relievePressure` has returned, so its Φ is 0,
		// and this is that post-condition restated from an independent walk.
		let residual = self.buildSplitIndex(module)
		if residual.found 'overPoolPointSurvivedRelief'
			panic("SplitDriver.buildSplitIndexOnce: the relief loop returned but a re-analysis finds an over-pool point (pressure {residual.pressure} against witness {residual.witness}) — a stuck value is a CONFINEMENT, which presupposes that every point already fits its pool, so the two analyses disagree about the same IR")
		end 'overPoolPointSurvivedRelief'
	end 'buildSplitIndexOnce'

	// ONE repair's mutation, with `scratch.anchor` already pointing at the cut. Both cures make the same
	// mutation and owe the same preparation and repair, so it is written once — the difference between
	// them is entirely WHERE they cut and WHOM they cut.
	function applyRepairSplit(module TargetModule, victim ValueId, counters RegAllocCounters)
		var work = self.scratch
		var funcLiveness = self.liveness
		let index = funcLiveness.useIndex

		// `hasStoreAnchor` never refuses a value the function DEFINES — its header states it — so this is
		// an assertion, not a branch. Both cures reach it: the stuck value was having its DEF coloured, and
		// an occupant held a register, which only a defined value can.
		if not hasStoreAnchor(index, valueId: victim) 'noStoreAnchor'
			panic("SplitDriver.applyRepairSplit: value {victim} has no store anchor, yet the colorer saw it defined or holding a register. Every value a function defines has an op def or a phi block entry (`buildUseIndex`), so `UseIndex.defKindOf` and the colorer disagree about whether this value exists.")
		end 'noStoreAnchor'

		// The same scope question `chooseVictim` asks of its own tier-4 victim: the eviction-point split
		// when it leaves the victim dead across the cut, the full split when a before-cut use is still
		// reachable from it around a back edge (see `SplitScope`).
		work.reach.retargetTo(work.anchor.blockLocal)
		let scope = splitScopeAt(index, topo: self.cfg.topo, valueId: victim, anchor: work.anchor, reach: work.reach)
		let slot = slotForSpillOf(index, valueId: victim, nextSlot: self.nextSlot)

		let freshFrom = self.nextValueId
		self.beginSplit(index, victim: victim, takesSlot: true)
		self.nextValueId = spillOneValue(module, index: index, cfg: self.cfg, liveness: funcLiveness, valueId: victim, anchor: work.anchor, scope: scope, slot: slot, pool: self.pool, nextValueId: self.nextValueId, counters: counters, lineage: self.lineage, scratch: work)
		self.commitSplit(module, victim: victim, freshFrom: freshFrom)

		// A REPAIR MUST NOT CREATE AN OVER-POOL POINT, and by Φ it cannot: a store/reload bracket only
		// renames an already-live value over a region it was live in anyway. Asserting it is what keeps
		// this cure from quietly handing `relievePressure`'s post-condition back broken — the colorer
		// would then fail for the OTHER reason, and the panic would name the wrong owner.
		let after = refreshPeak(module, liveness: funcLiveness, pool: self.pool, scratch: work)
		if after.found 'repairRaisedPressure'
			panic("SplitDriver.applyRepairSplit: splitting value {victim} at block {work.anchor.blockLocal} left an over-pool point (pressure {after.pressure} against witness {after.witness}, clobberOnly {after.clobberOnly}) — a split cannot raise any op's pressure, so either the store/reload placement or the incremental pressure repair is wrong")
		end 'repairRaisedPressure'
	end 'applyRepairSplit'

	// WHERE the colorer gave up on `v` — the program point cure (b) has to free a register AT. A value
	// with an OP def fails at that op; a BLOCK-ARG fails at its block's ENTRY, where `seedInUse` has just
	// seeded the live-ins and the phis are being coloured, and where no op reads anything.
	//
	// It mints a small object, on a path that runs only for a value the colorer could not place at all —
	// the same licence `valuesHoldingRegisters` takes on the diagnostic path, and for the same reason.
	function failingPointOf(module TargetModule, v ValueId) returns FailingPoint
		let index = self.liveness.useIndex
		let defBlock = index.defBlockOf(v)
		match index.defKindOf(v) 'kind'
			opDef then return FailingPoint.atOp(defBlock, pos: positionInBlock(blockAt(module, topo: self.cfg.topo, local: defBlock), opIdx: index.requireDefOp(v)), atOpIdx: index.requireDefOp(v))
			phiDef then return FailingPoint.atBlockEntry(defBlock)
			none then panic("SplitDriver.failingPointOf: value {v} has no def site, yet the colorer ran out of registers while placing it — every value it colours is defined by an op or is a block-arg")
		end 'kind'
	end 'failingPointOf'

	// WHY a whole ROUND could relieve nothing — for `repairStuckValues`'s panic, which is now the report
	// of a contradiction rather than of a shortfall. Both cures are named, because both must have failed.
	export function describeUnrepairable(v ValueId, free RegMask, occupants ColumnLength) returns String
		let established = try self.liveness.establishedAtDef.get(v) otherwise panic("SplitDriver.describeUnrepairable: establishedAtDef.get OOB for value {v}")
		return "value {v}: (a) it is live across NO clobber that takes a register of its own file, so there is nothing to cut to widen its allowed set — its forbid is {self.liveness.forbiddenOf(v)}, of which {self.paramCross.maskFor(v)} comes from a SIBLING PARAMETER's incoming register (`ParamCrossForbid`) and {established} from registers ESTABLISHED at its def (`establishedAtDef`), and whatever remains came from an op that names {v} as an OPERAND (`forbidOperandsFromImplicit`) — none of the three is a clobber inside a live range. And (b) all {occupants} occupant(s) of the registers it IS allowed were refused, which happens only when the failing point itself NAMES every one of them — the op reads it, or the op defines it, or its last use is at or before the point. All three say the same thing: that occupant is live AT the point, so evicting it frees nothing there. Free registers at the point were {free}."
	end 'describeUnrepairable'

	// The frame. `func.stackSize` is fixed HERE — after the relief loop AND after every repair — and
	// that placement is load-bearing.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ It used to be the last line of the splitter, which was right while the splitter made the last
	// split. A repair consumes a slot AFTER that point, so a frame sized there would have fewer slots
	// than the code writes: the store would land past the end of the frame, over whatever the callee
	// saved — SILENT STACK CORRUPTION, not a failure. (v1 avoids it by accumulating in `insertSpillCode`
	// and computing the frame in a pass that runs after register allocation has fully finished.)
	export function fixFrameSize(func IrFunction)
		func.stackSize = (self.nextSlot as StackSize) * SpillSlotBytes
	end 'fixFrameSize'
end 'SplitDriver'

// THE RUNAWAY BOUND every split loop in the allocator counts against — the splitter's own Φ→0 loop and
// the colorer's repair rounds alike.
//
// It bounds the potential from above: every potential either loop uses is a sum over ops of something
// no larger than the values live at that op, so `ops × (values + 1)` is above all of them, and each
// iteration takes at least one off. It is therefore UNREACHABLE by correct convergence — its whole job
// is to turn a non-terminating bug into a loud panic instead of a hang, so it wants to be generous
// rather than tight.
//
// ⚠ `RegisterFileSize` is added as a FLOOR and is not derived from anything. On a function small enough
// for the product to be tiny, a legitimate first relief could otherwise sit at the bound; the term costs
// nothing and keeps the panic away from the smallest programs. It is written down here as a choice
// rather than left in two call sites as an unexplained constant.
//
// (`valueCount` grows as fresh reload ids are minted; the bound is computed ONCE, from the initial
// figures, because the split count is bounded by the INITIAL potential.)
export function splitRunawayBound(liveness LivenessResult) returns EventCount
	return (liveness.useIndex.opCount as EventCount) * ((liveness.valueCount + 1) as EventCount) + RegisterFileSize
end 'splitRunawayBound'

// WHERE a colouring failed, in the coordinates a split anchor needs — plus whether an OP sits there at
// all, which is the one question cure (b) must ask of it. A BLOCK-ARG fails at its block's entry, and a
// block entry reads nothing: the phi's incoming values are read on the PREDECESSORS' edges, by different
// values, so no occupant can be excluded there.
type FailingPoint
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var pos as BlockOpPos
	var atAnOp as bool
	var opIdx as OpIndex

	static function atOp(atBlock CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos, atOpIdx OpIndex) returns FailingPoint
		return Self{blockLocal: atBlock, pos: pos, atAnOp: true, opIdx: atOpIdx}
	end 'atOp'

	static function atBlockEntry(atBlock CfgIndex) returns FailingPoint
		return Self{blockLocal: atBlock, pos: 0, atAnOp: false, opIdx: 0}
	end 'atBlockEntry'

	// Does the op at the failing point READ `w`? Evicting such an occupant frees NOTHING there: the value
	// is live AT the op because the op reads it, so the reload that replaces it holds the register at
	// exactly the point the register was wanted. It is the same exclusion `usesAtPeakOp` makes on
	// `chooseVictim`'s candidate pool, and `findConfiningClobber` makes on its cut.
	export function readsOccupant(module TargetModule, w ValueId, scratch SplitScratch) returns bool
		if not self.atAnOp 'aBlockEntryReadsNothing'
			return false
		end 'aBlockEntryReadsNothing'

		let op = try module.ops.get(self.opIdx) otherwise panic("FailingPoint.readsOccupant: op {self.opIdx} is not in module.ops")
		return opUsesValue(op, valueId: w, buf: scratch.splitOperands)
	end 'readsOccupant'
end 'FailingPoint'

// The op a REPAIR splits `v` across: one `v` is live ACROSS whose CLOBBER takes a register of `v`'s own
// file away from it. Split there and `v`'s range ends before it, so that clobber — and every clobber
// after it — leaves `v`'s forbidden mask.
//
// TWO TIERS, and the first is what makes the repair PROGRESS rather than merely legal:
//
//  1. a clobber covering a register that was FREE at the failing point. Greedy exhausted, so
//     `classPool ⊆ held ∪ forbidden(v)`, so `free = classPool ∖ held` is a SUBSET of `forbidden(v)` —
//     and it is non-empty whenever Hall's verdict there was FEASIBLE (`reportExhaustion` asserts both).
//     Cutting at the FIRST clobber covering one of those registers puts a provably free register back
//     into `v`'s allowed set, because no earlier clobber covers it.
//  2. failing that, the first clobber taking ANY register of `v`'s class pool. `free` is read off a
//     colouring that an earlier repair in the same round may since have invalidated, so it is a
//     PREFERENCE, not a premise. What termination needs is only that `v` cross strictly fewer clobbers
//     afterwards, and both tiers give exactly that.
//
// An op that READS `v` is skipped: cutting there rewrites that very use to a reload placed immediately
// before it, so the RELOAD would be live across the clobber in `v`'s place and Φ would not fall. It is
// the same exclusion `usesAtPeakOp` makes on `chooseVictim`'s candidate pool, for the same reason.
//
// ⚠ IT ASKS `opClobberMask` (through the seated leaf), NOT `opStrandsClass`. That predicate answers the
// STRONGER question "does this op leave the file NO register at all?", which is what a RUN BREAK turns
// on (`markRunStarts`) and is false of every call in the GPR file — a call leaves five callee-saved
// registers, and those five are exactly what a confined value is fighting over. Reusing it here would
// find no cut on the very programs this repair exists for.
type ConfiningClobber
	export var found as bool
	export var blockLocal as CfgIndex
	export var pos as BlockOpPos

	static function create(found bool, blockLocal CfgIndex, pos BlockOpPos) returns ConfiningClobber
		return Self{found: found, blockLocal: blockLocal, pos: pos}
	end 'create'
end 'ConfiningClobber'

function findConfiningClobber(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, scratch SplitScratch, v ValueId, free RegMask, classPool RegMask) returns ConfiningClobber
	var dirty = scratch.dirty
	var pressure = scratch.pressure
	var buf = scratch.splitOperands
	var fallback = ConfiningClobber.create(false, blockLocal: 0, pos: 0)

	// ASCENDING block order, so the first hit in each tier is the EARLIEST cut: the clobbers a cut
	// removes are the ones at and after it, so cutting early removes the most.
	dirty.sortAscending()
	for k in 0 upto dirty.count() 'eachBlock'
		let blockLocal = dirty.at(k)
		let span = liveAcrossSpanIn(liveness, pressure: pressure, w: v, blockLocal: blockLocal)
		if not span.found 'liveAcrossNothingHere'
			continue
		end 'liveAcrossNothingHere'

		for slot in span.lo upto span.hi + 1 'eachSlot'
			if not pressure.slotHoldsOp(slot) 'freeSlot'
				continue
			end 'freeSlot'
			let clobber = pressure.clobberAtSlot(slot)
			if (clobber and classPool) == 0 'takesNothingFromThisFile'
				continue
			end 'takesNothingFromThisFile'
			let opIdx = pressure.opAtSlot(slot)
			let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("findConfiningClobber: op {opIdx} seated at slot {slot} is not in module.ops")
			if opUsesValue(op, valueId: v, buf: buf) 'readsTheValueItself'
				continue
			end 'readsTheValueItself'

			let hit = ConfiningClobber.create(true, blockLocal: blockLocal, pos: pressure.positionOfSlot(blockLocal, slot: slot))
			if (clobber and free) != 0 'coversAFreeRegister'
				return hit
			end 'coversAFreeRegister'
			if not fallback.found 'firstConfiningOpAnywhere'
				fallback = hit
			end 'firstConfiningOpAnywhere'
		end 'eachSlot'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return fallback
end 'findConfiningClobber'

// The value the relief will rewrite. A `none` choice does not return: at a FULL-pool peak it is the
// E5001 the design exists to raise, and at a CONFINED one a splitter bug (`noVictimAtPeak`).
//
// It is read out BEFORE the mutation because the incremental update needs the PRE-split index's answer
// for where the victim is live and where it is used.
function victimAtPeak(choice SpillChoice, refusal RegAllocRefusalSite, func IrFunction, hasAuthor bool, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, pool RegMask, lineage SplitLineage) returns ValueId throws RegAllocRefusal
	match choice 'act'
		none then try noVictimAtPeak(refusal, func: func, hasAuthor: hasAuthor, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, pool: pool, lineage: lineage)
		remat(v, _) then return v
		spill(v, _) then return v
	end 'act'
	panic("victimAtPeak: `noVictimAtPeak` returned without throwing — a `none` choice has no victim")
end 'victimAtPeak'

// ==========================================================================
// The incremental update — one split's dirty region, and nothing more
// ==========================================================================

// VERIFY MODE. Flip to `true`, rebuild, and every incremental update below is checked against a FULL
// rebuild of liveness and pressure, exhaustively — the live sets, the forbidden masks, the dead-phi
// counts, the per-block demands, the def-point pressures, the whole use index, and the chosen peak. Any
// divergence PANICS at the split that caused it.
//
// It exists because a wrong invalidation set does not fail a test. It drops ONE clobber bit from ONE
// value's forbidden mask; that value then takes a caller-saved register across a call; and the program
// miscompiles silently, in a way no spec test is obliged to notice. The only defence is to check the
// incremental answer against the thing it replaces, on real programs — the shv2 spec suite and the
// scale corpus (`scale-test --rungs=4` compiles rungs 0..3).
//
// It is off in a shipping compiler because it makes every split O(function) again, which is precisely
// the cost this design removes.
let VerifyIncrementalSplit = false

// Seed the dirty region with everything the PRE-SPLIT index says the victim touches, and UNMARK its
// liveness. Both are read from the index and live sets the victim was CHOSEN in, so both must run
// before the mutation rewrites its uses.
//
// The victim's live range is removed rather than diffed: `LivenessMarkScratch.walkValue` re-derives it
// by the same per-value walk that laid it down, so it removes exactly the marks that are there.
function unmarkVictim(topo FuncTopology, index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, scratch SplitScratch, victim ValueId)
	// The victim's own contribution to the per-op pressure numbers, WITHDRAWN while the world it was
	// measured in still stands: it is read off the use thread the mutation is about to rewrite and the
	// live sets the walk below is about to edit, so it cannot be taken afterwards.
	applyValueToPressure(liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, v: victim, adding: false)
	collectVictimRegion(topo, index: index, liveness: liveness, scratch: scratch, victim: victim, adding: false)
end 'unmarkVictim'

// Every block `victim` touches, into `scratch.dirty`: its DEF block, every block holding a USE of it,
// and its whole LIVE RANGE. `adding` is the direction the range walk marks in.
//
// TWO CALLERS, and the second is why this is a function rather than three lines inside `unmarkVictim`.
// `unmarkVictim` calls it with `adding: false`, which removes the marks the marking walk laid down —
// exactly, because it is the same walk. `SplitDriver.repairAtExhaustion` calls it with `adding: true`
// on a value that is STILL LIVE, where `LiveSets.addLiveIn`/`addLiveOut`'s idempotence makes the marking
// a no-op and `touched` is the whole point: it is the region the repair searches for a clobber, and it
// must be collected while the live sets still hold the value the span endpoints are read from. Spelled
// twice, one of them would miss the def block, and the repair would search a region the split then
// dirtied more of.
function collectVictimRegion(topo FuncTopology, index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, scratch SplitScratch, victim ValueId, adding bool)
	var dirty = scratch.dirty

	// Every block holding a USE of it, plus its DEF block: those hold the ops that name it, so the index
	// rebuild must re-read them — and they are where its `touchesLoop` / `edgePassed` / `spillStored`
	// facts come from.
	let useSites = index.useCount(victim)
	if useSites > 0 'used'
		var record = index.firstUseOf(victim)
		for _ in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
			dirty.add(index.useBlockAt(record))
			record = index.nextUseOf(record)
		end 'eachUse'
	end 'used'

	match index.defKindOf(victim) 'kind'
		opDef to phiDef then dirty.add(index.defBlockOf(victim))
		none then panic("collectVictimRegion: value {victim} has no def site — every victim tier requires one (`hasStoreAnchor`, `isRematerializable`, or a `storeFitsPool` that only a defined value can pass), so `chooseVictim` cannot have offered it, and neither can the colorer, which gets stuck at a DEF")
	end 'kind'

	// Its whole live range: the blocks whose live sets must lose it, AND the blocks its `forbidden` mask
	// was accumulated over — which the dirty-region sweep rebuilds it from.
	liveness.livenessMarkSteps = liveness.livenessMarkSteps + scratch.marks.walkValue(topo, index: index, v: victim, live: liveness.live, adding: adding, touched: dirty)
end 'collectVictimRegion'

// Bring every derived structure back into agreement with the IR the split just changed — WITHOUT
// re-reading the function.
//
// THE DIRTY REGION, and why it is a SUPERSET of what actually moved:
//
//  • the victim's WHOLE PRE-SPLIT LIVE RANGE, plus its def block and every block holding a use of it
//    (`unmarkVictim`, before the mutation). The victim is the only value whose liveness can SHRINK, and
//    it can only shrink inside that range; its uses can only move within the blocks that already held
//    them (a split rewrites uses, it never relocates one); the one op it GAINS is its spill store, at
//    its def.
//  • every block an op was spliced into or removed from (`SplitEdits.apply`). Those are the only blocks
//    whose op layout, sequence numbers or pressure profile can have changed — and the only blocks a
//    fresh id is defined or used in, because a reload is inserted in the block of the uses it serves.
//  • the victim's NEW live range and each fresh id's live range, added by the marking walks below. Both
//    are subsets of the two above; they are collected anyway, because a superset is CORRECT and a
//    subset is a silent miscompile.
//
// NOTHING ELSE MOVED. See the file header for the four facts that make that exact rather than hopeful,
// and `sweepBlockPressure` for why re-sweeping a dirty block is IDEMPOTENT on every value but the
// victim — whose mask is the one thing that can shrink, and is therefore the one thing zeroed.
function refreshAfterSplit(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, liveness LivenessResult, scratch SplitScratch, split SplitValues, pool RegMask, paramCross ParamCrossForbid)
	let topo = cfg.topo
	var index = liveness.useIndex
	var dirty = scratch.dirty

	// FIRST, because the splice below SEATS each inserted op in the positional index and a reload's def
	// is one of the ids this split minted — so the index's per-value column has to reach it already.
	scratch.pressure.growValueSpace(cfg.valueCount)

	// The split's op insertions and its one possible removal, spliced into their blocks — plus the
	// sequence repair, the op count, and the blocks that took an edit.
	scratch.edits.commit(module, topo: topo, index: index, dirty: dirty, pressure: scratch.pressure, relayout: scratch.relayout, opFacts: liveness.opFacts, buf: scratch.sweep.buf)

	// The value space grew by the ids the split minted; every column keyed by it follows.
	//
	// THE FRESH IDS INHERIT THE VICTIM'S CLASS. Every id this split minted is a reload or a
	// rematerialization OF THE VICTIM, so it carries the victim's value and therefore lives in the
	// victim's register file. It cannot be classified from the op that defines it: `loadRegSlot` and
	// `movRegImm` are class-POLYMORPHIC (`mov r64, [slot]` for an int, `movsd xmm, [slot]` for a
	// float), so their own variant answers GPR for both and would silently mis-file a float's reload.
	// `growValueSpace` therefore DEMANDS the class rather than defaulting it.
	let victimClass = liveness.classOf(split.victim)
	index.growTo(cfg.valueCount)
	liveness.growValueSpace(cfg.valueCount, class: victimClass)
	scratch.effPools.growTo(cfg.valueCount, pool: pool, class: victimClass, file: liveness.registerFile)
	scratch.sweep.widenRow(liveness.wordsPerRow)
	if VerifyIncrementalSplit 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'
		// The walk still writes this column on the CONFINED path, so it has to reach the ids the split
		// just minted — even though nothing but the verifier ever reads it.
		scratch.defLiveAfter.growFilled(cfg.valueCount as ElementIndex, value: NoStoreAnchorPressure)
	end 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'

	// Re-derive the index facts of the values the split touched, from the dirty region alone. It must be
	// walked in ASCENDING block order — a use thread is held ascending by sequence.
	dirty.sortAscending()
	reindexSplitValues(module, cfg: cfg, index: index, dirty: dirty, split: split, pressure: scratch.pressure, edits: scratch.edits, candidates: scratch.reindexCandidates, sortBuf: scratch.reindexSortBuf, scratch: scratch.sweep, opFacts: liveness.opFacts)

	// Re-mark their liveness — the victim's OLD marks came off before the mutation. Every other value's
	// mask is left exactly as it is: no other value's live range moved, so no other value can have gained
	// or lost a clobber (the ops a split inserts clobber nothing).
	let regionBeforeRemarking = dirty.count()
	liveness.livenessMarkSteps = liveness.livenessMarkSteps + scratch.marks.walkValue(topo, index: index, v: split.victim, live: liveness.live, adding: true, touched: dirty)
	for f in split.freshFrom upto split.freshTo 'eachFresh'
		liveness.livenessMarkSteps = liveness.livenessMarkSteps + scratch.marks.walkValue(topo, index: index, v: f, live: liveness.live, adding: true, touched: dirty)
	end 'eachFresh'

	// THE INVARIANT THE ORDER ABOVE RESTS ON, ASSERTED. The re-marking walks may only revisit blocks the
	// index rebuild has ALREADY seen — the victim's new range is a subset of its old one plus its def
	// block, and a fresh id lives only inside the block whose reload minted it — so a block appearing
	// here for the FIRST time is one `reindexSplitValues` never re-read.
	//
	// It would fail silently and far away. The block's live sets, masks, dead-phi count and demand would
	// all be re-swept and all agree with each other; only the USE RECORD of the fresh id in it would be
	// missing, so nothing would mark that id live there, so the block's pressure would undercount it,
	// so the colorer would hand its register to another value while the reload's value is still live.
	// Every derived structure would be consistent, and the program would be wrong.
	if dirty.count() != regionBeforeRemarking 'regionGrewAfterTheRebuild'
		panic("refreshAfterSplit: re-marking the liveness of value {split.victim} and the {split.freshTo - split.freshFrom} id(s) it minted reached {dirty.count() - regionBeforeRemarking} block(s) the index rebuild never read. A split may only move a value's liveness INSIDE the region it already touched — if a mutator now places a reload outside the block of the uses it serves, `reindexSplitValues` must run after these walks, not before.")
	end 'regionGrewAfterTheRebuild'

	// THE POSITIONAL INDEX, brought back into agreement — the other half of the withdrawal `unmarkVictim`
	// made before the mutation. Everything it needs is now current: the ops are spliced and seated, the
	// use threads are rebuilt, and the live sets are re-marked.
	refreshExitLeaves(liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, dirty: dirty, row: scratch.exitPopulation)
	applyValueToPressure(liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, v: split.victim, adding: true)
	for f in split.freshFrom upto split.freshTo 'eachFresh'
		applyValueToPressure(liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, v: f, adding: true)
	end 'eachFresh'
	refreshSplitTransients(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, split: split, sweep: scratch.sweep)

	// WHAT WAS ESTABLISHED-AND-PENDING WHERE EACH DEF NOW SITS, restated for the dirty blocks — the one
	// register fact that is about a def's POSITION rather than about a live range, so a split that seats
	// a fresh id between a call's argument pre-moves is exactly what moves it (see
	// `LivenessResult.establishedAtDef`). It runs BEFORE the masks are rebuilt, because
	// `rebuildForbiddenOf` reads this column as one of its sources.
	//
	// It is per DIRTY BLOCK rather than per fresh id because it is derived by ONE forward walk of a
	// block — the same walk the whole-function build uses, not a second reading of the same fact — and a
	// split's fresh ids all live in blocks it dirtied.
	for k in 0 upto dirty.count() 'eachDirtyBlock'
		sweepEstablishedRegisters(module, topo: topo, blockLocal: dirty.at(k), establishedAtDef: liveness.establishedAtDef, scratch: scratch.sweep, opFacts: liveness.opFacts)
	end 'eachDirtyBlock'

	// The three things the dirty-block sweep used to produce, each restated against what actually moved.
	rebuildForbiddenOf(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, w: split.victim, paramCross: paramCross, dirty: dirty)
	for f in split.freshFrom upto split.freshTo 'eachFresh'
		rebuildForbiddenOf(module, liveness: liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, w: f, paramCross: paramCross, dirty: dirty)
	end 'eachFresh'
	refreshDeadPhisFor(module, liveness: liveness, victim: split.victim)
	refreshBlockDemands(liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, dirty: dirty)

	// Their effective pools follow their new masks — and no other value's mask moved, so no other
	// value's pool did.
	scratch.effPools.refresh(liveness, valueId: split.victim, pool: pool)
	for f in split.freshFrom upto split.freshTo 'eachFresh'
		scratch.effPools.refresh(liveness, valueId: f, pool: pool)
	end 'eachFresh'
end 'refreshAfterSplit'

// Re-run the pressure sweep on the DIRTY BLOCKS ONLY, re-publish their leaves, and read the function's
// peak off the tournament's root — the incremental half of `analyzePressure`.
//
// ==========================================================================
// THE CONFINED HALF IS DEFERRED UNTIL IT CAN WIN
// ==========================================================================
//
// A FULL-POOL overflow outranks EVERY confined one unconditionally (`peakOutranks`), so a confined rank
// is only ever the function's peak when NO op anywhere overflows its full pool. The exact Hall
// confirmation that produces one is a maximum bipartite matching — MEASURED at ~80,000 CPU ticks a call
// against ~2,900 for the entire rest of an op's step — and the screen that gates it fires at roughly
// half the ops of a call-heavy block. Computed eagerly it was 86% of `analyzeBlockPressure` and 75% of
// the whole per-split cost on a block carrying Θ(block) splits, and the tournament DISCARDED every one
// of those calls in the same iteration that paid for them.
//
// So the function runs in one of two MODES, and `SplitScratch.confinedMode` is the whole state. It sits
// on the scratch because it is a fact about the FUNCTION rather than about one split — the same place
// and for the same reason `ReachCache`'s rows outlive the split that filled them:
//
//  • FULL-POOL MODE. Dirty blocks are swept for the full-pool half alone. If the tournament then names
//    a full-pool peak, that peak outranks every confined rank in the function and no Hall matching runs
//    at all. If it does NOT, the function has just left full-pool mode: every leaf may be holding a
//    full-pool-only rank, so ALL blocks are re-swept once with the confined half on, and the mode flips.
//  • CONFINED MODE. Dirty blocks are swept with the confined half on, exactly as they always were.
//
// ⚠ THE FULL-POOL TEST IS `found AND NOT clobberOnly`, NOT `found`. A CONFINED best can be a rank a
// CLEAN block still holds from before the mode began, and a dirty block's un-computed confined rank
// might outrank it — so anything short of a full-pool win must re-derive. (A clean block's confined rank
// is otherwise still VALID: the only values whose effective pools a split moves are the victim and the
// ids it minted, and both are live only inside the dirty region.)
//
// ⚠ THE MODE FLIP HAPPENS AT MOST ONCE PER FUNCTION, which is what bounds the one whole-function
// re-sweep it costs: a split never RAISES any op's pressure (the termination argument's premise), so
// once no op overflows its full pool none ever does again. CORRECTNESS DOES NOT REST ON THAT — a
// function that somehow re-entered full-pool mode would sweep both halves from then on, which is merely
// the old cost — only the bound does.
function refreshPeak(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask, scratch SplitScratch) returns PressurePeak
	let dirty = scratch.dirty
	// Snapshotted: the mode flip at the bottom must not change what the tests above it decided.
	let confinedModeOnEntry = scratch.confinedMode
	for k in 0 upto dirty.count() 'eachDirtyBlock'
		let blockLocal = dirty.at(k)
		resweepBlock(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool, scratch: scratch, blockLocal: blockLocal, wantConfined: confinedModeOnEntry)
	end 'eachDirtyBlock'

	// ONE query of the root, answering both the mode test and — when the ranks turn out to be current —
	// the analysis. `best()` mints a rank, and asking the tournament twice for the same champion was two
	// of them per split on the full-pool path.
	let champion = scratch.tree.best()
	if confinedModeOnEntry or (champion.found and not champion.clobberOnly) 'ranksAreCurrent'
		// Either both halves were swept, or a FULL-POOL peak came back — and one of those outranks every
		// confined rank in the function, computed or not.
		return materializePeak(module, liveness: liveness, rank: champion, scratch: scratch)
	end 'ranksAreCurrent'

	// THE MODE FLIP. Every leaf swept since the mode began holds a full-pool-only rank, so the confined
	// half of the whole function is re-derived once, here.
	for blockLocal in 0 upto liveness.cfg.topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		resweepBlock(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool, scratch: scratch, blockLocal: blockLocal, wantConfined: true)
	end 'eachBlock'
	scratch.confinedMode = true
	return materializePeak(module, liveness: liveness, rank: scratch.tree.best(), scratch: scratch)
end 'refreshPeak'

// One block's leaf, re-derived and published to the root. The FULL-POOL half comes off the positional
// index (O(log ops)); the CONFINED half is the per-op walk, which only runs when a confined rank can
// win at all.
function resweepBlock(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask, scratch SplitScratch, blockLocal CfgIndex, wantConfined bool)
	scratch.tree.setLeaf(blockLocal, rank: analyzeBlockPressure(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool, scratch: scratch, blockLocal: blockLocal, wantFullPool: false, wantConfined: wantConfined))
	scratch.tree.promote(blockLocal)
end 'resweepBlock'

// ⭐⭐ THE CONFINED OVERFLOW HIDING UNDERNEATH A FULL-POOL PEAK — the ABI's forced work, found where
// the RANKING discarded it.
//
// `peakOutranks` puts every full-pool overflow above every confined one, so while one op anywhere
// overflows its own pool no confined rank can ever be the function's peak. That ordering is right for
// the normal path (it is what makes `storeFitsPool` sound by the time a confined peak is reached) but
// it hides a fact when the full-pool peak turns out to have NO cold-spillable value: **the very values
// that overflow the pool may also be confined, at that same op, to a subset far too small for them** —
// and THAT overflow is relievable, at any loop depth, by the store/reload bracket Rule 2 calls forced.
//
// MEASURED, and it is the whole reason this exists (`generic-hash-table-regalloc`): a rehash loop in a
// generic type holds 16 GPR values at one op against a pool of 14, and **14 of the 16 are live across a
// method call inside the same loop**, hence confined to the 5 callee-saved registers. The program
// cannot compile without ~9 forced brackets — the ABI decided that, not the allocator — and once they
// are emitted the full-pool peak is gone. Refusing at the full-pool peak measured the loop's working
// set BEFORE the ABI had finished shrinking it, and reported E5001 against a program the bootstrap
// compiles and runs. A false E5001 is the worst bug this compiler can have.
//
// ⚠ THE WITNESS GATE IS WHAT KEEPS THIS FROM MOVING THE E5001 CLIFF. Hall at a point that is over the
// full pool ALWAYS fails; what distinguishes the two cases is its WITNESS. A witness equal to the whole
// file's pool is the pigeonhole restated — the loop genuinely wants more registers than exist, no
// clobber is involved, and E5001 stands. Only a PROPER subset is a confinement, and `confinementOnly`
// is where that line is drawn. Every committed E5001 case is a loop with no call in it, so no value is
// confined to anything, and this returns `found: false` for all of them.
//
// It reuses the peak's OWN already-materialized live-before row and location, so it re-derives nothing
// and costs one bipartite matching per register file. It is reached only where E5001 was about to be
// raised, so the normal path pays nothing at all for it.
//
// ⚠ The `PeakRank` this fills carries a `forwardPos` of 0 and that field is deliberately never read:
// `forwardPos` exists so `materializePeak` can re-walk a block to its peak op, and this peak needs no
// such walk — its row, its sequence and its op index are the FULL-POOL peak's, which are already
// materialized. The rank never leaves this function; only the `PressurePeak` built from it does.
function confinedOverflowAtPeak(liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, pool RegMask, scratch SplitScratch) returns PressurePeak
	var live = scratch.candidates
	bitsetCollectRow(peak.liveBefore, base: 0, wordsPerRow: liveness.wordsPerRow, out: live)

	var best = emptyRank()
	confinedPeakAt(liveness, live: live, pool: pool, best: best, blockLocal: peak.blockLocal, forwardPos: 0, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, atFixedRegisterOp: peak.atFixedRegisterOp, hall: scratch.hall)

	return PressurePeak.create(best.found, pressure: best.pressure, blockLocal: peak.blockLocal, liveBefore: peak.liveBefore, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq, peakOpIdx: peak.peakOpIdx, clobberOnly: true, witness: best.witness, witnessClass: best.witnessClass, atFixedRegisterOp: peak.atFixedRegisterOp)
end 'confinedOverflowAtPeak'

// Did `chooseVictim` find one? A `none` choice is the only shape with no victim, and both of the
// others carry one — so this is the question the driver asks before deciding whether the peak it just
// examined is really the one to refuse.
function choiceFound(choice SpillChoice) returns bool
	return match choice 'k'
		none gives false
		remat to spill gives true
	end 'k'
end 'choiceFound'

// Nothing crossing the peak can be relieved. What that MEANS depends on which overflow it is —
// and keeping the two apart is the whole point of the amended Rule 2.
//
//  • FULL-POOL overflow → E5001, and this is the diagnostic the design exists to raise. Every
//    value crossing the point is one the loop CARRIES — READ inside it, or DEFINED inside it (a
//    loop-header phi for a `var` the loop assigns) — so the only placement a split could give it
//    puts a store or a reload in the loop body, which is what Rule 2's COLD gate refuses. It is
//    the loop's genuine working set AS LOWERED, and it is larger than the machine. No spiller can
//    fix that; only the author can, by restructuring. `maxlive` is EXACT here (SSA interference is
//    chordal, so χ = ω), so the deficit is a theorem about the program, not the outcome of a search.
//
//    ⚠⚠ **THIS SAID "every value crossing the point is USED inside the loop", WHICH IS NARROWER
//    THAN THE TRUTH — narrow enough that reading it produced a wrong diagnosis of a live E5001.**
//    A loop-carried `var` the loop only ASSIGNS is read ZERO times inside it and is still here,
//    because it is a block arg DEFINED in the loop: its store would anchor at its own header block
//    and its reloads would sit at its in-loop edge uses, so no split shortens its range at all.
//    `isColdSpillable` refuses it on the DEPTH of that def, which is the fact that matters, and the
//    ranked list prints it at "read 0 times in the loop" (`RegionReadCount` — it counts op operands,
//    never edge args). MEASURED on the spec harness's own `MaxonArgs.parse` (BATCH43): 22 of the 33
//    blocking values were loop-header phis of exactly this shape — 21 `var` flags plus the counter —
//    against a pool of 14. Relieving THOSE needs a transformation this splitter does not own
//    (demoting a whole phi congruence class into one frame slot), not a live-range split; the
//    refusal is therefore correct as the design stands, and the author's remedy is the one the
//    message names.
//
//  • CONFINED (clobber-only) overflow → a SPLITTER BUG, and it must NEVER reach the user as E5001.
//    The values confined to the witness FIT the pool; they are excluded only from the registers the
//    ops they are live across clobber. A forced bracket is therefore ALWAYS available for at least
//    one of them, and the panic below states the exact invariant that makes that so. Raising E5001
//    instead would be the false positive this design treats as its worst possible bug: it would tell
//    the author to delete a value from code that fits the machine, and the rewrite it suggests would
//    emit strictly worse code.
//
// THE INVARIANT THE CONFINED PANIC ASSERTS, in full, because "it cannot happen" was once asserted
// here on a claim that was merely NECESSARY (a store anchor) and not SUFFICIENT — and the panic was
// reachable from ordinary Maxon (`register-spill.loop-invariant-read-across-a-call-in-a-loop`):
//
//  1. EVERY value the function defines has a store anchor — an op def or a phi's block entry
//     (`buildUseIndex` gives every Target value one or the other), so `hasStoreAnchor` never refuses
//     a live value.
//  2. EVERY value's store FITS the pool. A FULL-pool overflow outranks every confined one
//     (`peakOutranks`), so by the time a confined peak is the peak, NO program point exceeds the full
//     pool — including every def point, which is what `storeFitsPool` measures. And a value already
//     in a slot needs no new store at all.
//  3. EVERY value can be made DEAD across the peak. Rewriting its after-peak uses to reloads does it
//     whenever no before-peak use is still reachable from the peak; when one IS (a loop-invariant read
//     before AND after a call inside the loop, alive across it around the back edge), rewriting EVERY
//     use does it, because the original's only remaining reader is then its own store
//     (`SplitScope.everyUse`). There is no third case: a value with no use at all is not live here.
//  4. So the ONLY confined values `chooseVictim` may refuse are the ones the peak op itself READS —
//     and it cannot refuse them all, PROVIDED THE WITNESS IS NON-EMPTY. The tight set OUTNUMBERS the
//     witness, and a witness a clobber produces normally has at least the five callee-saved registers
//     in it, so at least six values are confined there; an op names at most two virtual registers, so
//     at least four candidates remain.
//
//     ⚠ THAT COUNTING ARGUMENT DOES NOT HOLD FOR AN EMPTY WITNESS, and this file used to state it as
//     though it did. A value confined to ∅ puts the bound at "more than zero", so a tight set of
//     exactly TWO satisfies it, and ONE op can read both. That was not hypothetical: it was
//     `register-spill.two-floats-multiplied-between-calls`, where a stranded reload and the stranded
//     original met at the `mulsd` that reads them both and this panic fired on a program that fits
//     the machine many times over. At the time a float live across a call WAS confined to ∅, because
//     Wave 1 took every XMM as caller-saved.
//
//     What closed it is upstream, not here: a reload is no longer BORN with an empty allowed set
//     (`markRunStarts`), so the values confined to ∅ at any peak are originals only, and the call
//     that confines them outranks the op that reads them. ⇒ REACHING HERE WITH AN EMPTY WITNESS
//     POINTS AT THE RUN-BREAK, not at `hasStoreAnchor` / `storeFitsPool` / `killsValueAtPeak`.
//
//     ⚠⚠ **WAVE 2 REMOVED THE ONLY WAY A *CALL* CAN PRODUCE ∅, AND THE GUARD STAYS ANYWAY.** xmm6–15
//     are callee-saved now, so a float live across a call is confined to TEN registers and a GPR to
//     FIVE — the counting argument holds for both files, and no call op's witness is empty. But ∅ is
//     a property of the WITNESS, which is `classPool ∖ op.implicitDefs` for ANY fixed-register op,
//     not only a call: an op whose `implicitDefs` covered a whole class would produce it again, and
//     that mask is per-op inline data (`TargetOpMeta`) each new op declares for itself. The guard is
//     therefore not DEAD, it is UNREACHED-BY-TODAY'S-OPS — a statement about the current op set, not
//     about this code. It costs one compare on the panic path and is the only thing standing between
//     a future op's clobber mask and a false E5001 against a program that fits the machine.
function noVictimAtPeak(refusal RegAllocRefusalSite, func IrFunction, hasAuthor bool, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, pool RegMask, lineage SplitLineage) throws RegAllocRefusal
	if peak.clobberOnly 'splitterBug'
		if peak.witness == 0 'confinedToNothing'
			panic("noVictimAtPeak: '{String.from(func.name)}' has a CONFINED overflow at a peak whose witness is EMPTY — {peak.pressure} {peak.witnessClass.name} value(s) allowed NO register at all, against a full pool of {classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: peak.witnessClass, file: liveness.registerFile)} — and `chooseVictim` refused every one of them. With an empty witness the usual counting argument gives nothing (the tight set need only outnumber ZERO), so a tight set of two values that ONE op reads leaves nothing to choose. Every value confined to ∅ should be an ORIGINAL, relieved at the op that confines it, because a split must never MINT one: look first at the run-break (`markRunStarts` / `opStrandsClass`), which exists to stop a reload being born with nowhere to live. It must never surface as E5001: this program fits the machine.")
		end 'confinedToNothing'

		panic("noVictimAtPeak: '{String.from(func.name)}' has a CONFINED overflow ({peak.pressure} values confined to the {regMaskPopcount(peak.witness)} registers of witness {peak.witness}, which the full {peak.witnessClass.name} pool of {classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: peak.witnessClass, file: liveness.registerFile)} would fit) yet `chooseVictim` refused every one of them. The invariant that must hold: a confined value the peak op does not itself read is ALWAYS relievable — it has a store anchor (an op def or a phi block entry), its store fits the pool (full-pool peaks are relieved FIRST, so no def point is over-pool by now), and reloading its after-peak uses — or, when a before-peak use is still reachable from the peak around a back edge, EVERY use — makes it dead across the peak. The tight set outnumbers the witness and the peak op names at most two virtual registers, so candidates always remain. Refusing them all is a SPLITTER BUG in `hasStoreAnchor` / `storeFitsPool` / `killsValueAtPeak` / `analyzePressure` — not user register pressure. It must never surface as E5001: this program fits the machine.")
	end 'splitterBug'

	// ⛔ **E5001 IS NEVER RAISED FOR A FUNCTION THE COMPILER EMITTED**, and this is where that contract is
	// enforced rather than hoped for. `relievePressure` has already retried such a function's full-pool peak
	// under the FORCED bracket (`noAuthorToRefuse`), which is total over the values a function defines — every
	// one of them has a store anchor, and one whose store does not fit the pool yet is relieved by a later
	// iteration. So arriving here with no victim is a splitter bug, and reporting it as user register pressure
	// is not an option that exists: the message names a source line the function does not have, and
	// `defRangeOf` panics building it (RULE 3) rather than emitting one.
	if not hasAuthor 'emittedByTheCompiler'
		panic("noVictimAtPeak: '{String.from(func.name)}' is a function the COMPILER emitted — it has no source and so no author — yet `chooseVictim` refused every one of the {peak.pressure} {peak.witnessClass.name} value(s) at its FULL-POOL peak, under the forced bracket `relievePressure` retries such a function with. That bracket is total over the values a function defines: each has a store anchor (an op def or a phi block entry), and reloading every use makes it dead across the peak whatever `killsValueAtPeak` says. Under a forced placement the only gate left is `hasStoreAnchor` — tier 4 takes anything memory already holds and tier 5 takes the rest — so refusing them all is a SPLITTER BUG in `hasStoreAnchor` or in `analyzePressure`'s live row. It must never surface as E5001: there is nobody to address that error to, and no source span to anchor it at.")
	end 'emittedByTheCompiler'

	// ⭐ **THE REFUSAL IS RECORDED AND THROWN, NEVER RENDERED HERE.** E5001 names a source span per
	// blocking value, which only `project.opRanges` / `valueOrigins` can supply — and this runs on a pool
	// worker, which has no `Project` and may not reach one. The site carries the two things no later
	// frame can rebuild (this driver's liveness and lineage, and the peak the overflow was measured at)
	// to whoever holds a `Project`; see `RegAllocRefusalSite`.
	refusal.refused = RefusedAllocation.at(liveness, lineage: lineage, peak: peak)
	throw RegAllocRefusal.hotOverflow
end 'noVictimAtPeak'

// `computeLivenessOn`, attributed to the LIVENESS phase rather than to SPLITTING.
//
// The splitter owns every liveness computation (it runs one up front and one after each split, and
// hands the final result to the colorer), so without this the whole liveness pass was billed to
// `splitting` — and `liveness` read 0.00ms on every program, which is exactly the "74% of
// self-compile with ZERO sub-phase attribution" failure the design set out to avoid. On a large
// function liveness, not splitting, is the dominant cost, and the numbers said the opposite.
//
// It takes `costs`, not `counters`, because the probe records all five quantities and only that value
// holds all five. It is the FUNCTION's own cost column, not the compile's: the core has no `Project`
// (see `RegAllocCosts`), and the driver folds one function's column home when its result lands.
//
// The driver now runs exactly ONE of these per function, so what this phase reports is a single
// whole-function build. The per-split REPAIR of that build (`refreshAfterSplit`) is billed to
// `splitting`, which is where it belongs: it is proportional to what the split changed, not to the
// function, and it is the splitter's own bookkeeping.
function timedLiveness(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, func IrFunction, valueClass ValueClassColumn, file RegisterFile, opFacts OpVariantFacts, costs RegAllocCosts) returns LivenessResult
	let probe = PhaseProbe.beginCounters()
	let liveness = computeLivenessOn(module, cfg: cfg, func: func, valueClass: valueClass, file: file, opFacts: opFacts)
	costs.record(RegAllocPhase.liveness, probe: probe)
	return liveness
end 'timedLiveness'

// ==========================================================================
// The pressure sweep (peak + def-point pressures)
// ==========================================================================

// The WHOLE-FUNCTION sweep: every block's peak rank, folded. After the first split only the blocks it
// dirtied are re-swept (`refreshPeak`), which runs the same per-block routine — and reads the full-pool
// half of it off `PressureIndex` rather than sweeping for it.
//
// `intoTree` also seeds the per-block TOURNAMENT as it goes — which only a function that turns out to
// SPLIT needs. The overwhelming majority never do (1202 of 1202 in the call-heavy benchmark), so the
// tree's nine block-sized columns are not sized for them at all, and the plain fold below is the whole
// cost. A splitting function pays one extra whole-function sweep, once, to seed it. (Same discipline as
// `ReachCache` and `SplitScratch.beginSplitting`.)
function analyzePressure(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask, scratch SplitScratch, intoTree bool) returns PressurePeak
	let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
	scratch.sweep.widenRow(liveness.wordsPerRow)
	// The Hall machinery's one dense `value -> popcount(pool ∖ forbidden(v))` column. Filled in full
	// here, and MAINTAINED per value from now on — a split changes the mask of the victim and of the ids
	// it minted, and of nothing else.
	scratch.effPools.fillAll(liveness, pool: pool)
	// A value with no def point at all keeps this sentinel, so it is never mistaken for store-safe.
	// VERIFY MODE only, like the column itself.
	if VerifyIncrementalSplit 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'
		scratch.defLiveAfter.refill(liveness.valueCount as ElementIndex, value: NoStoreAnchorPressure)
	end 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'

	if intoTree 'sizeTheTournament'
		scratch.tree.reset(topo.numBlocks)
	end 'sizeTheTournament'

	// ⚠ EXACTLY ONE OF THE TWO ANSWERS IS COMPUTED, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT WILL BE READ. The fold and
	// the tournament are two mechanisms for one question — "which block holds the function's peak?" —
	// and this call used to run BOTH and return the FOLD's, after which every `refreshPeak` returned
	// the TREE's. Nothing compared them, so a disagreement would have moved the peak silently between
	// the first split and the second. Whenever a tree is being seeded it is also the answer from here
	// on, so it answers this call too; the fold exists only for the pass that seeds no tree (the
	// driver's first analysis, and `verifyIncremental`'s independent rebuild).
	//
	// They must nevertheless AGREE, and that is checked rather than asserted: `verifyIncremental`
	// compares the tree-derived peak against a full `analyzePressure(intoTree: false)` rebuild — i.e.
	// against the fold — after every single split.
	//
	// Blocks in ASCENDING index, and the fold replaces on an exact tie, so its winner is the LAST
	// maximal rank offered. The tournament reproduces exactly that (see `PeakTree`).
	var best = PeakRank.create(false, pressure: 0, blockLocal: 0, forwardPos: 0, peakSeq: 0, clobberOnly: false, witness: pool, witnessClass: RegClass.gpr, atFixedRegisterOp: false)
	for i in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let rank = analyzeBlockPressure(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool, scratch: scratch, blockLocal: i, wantFullPool: not intoTree, wantConfined: true)
		if intoTree 'recordTheLeaf'
			scratch.tree.setLeaf(i, rank: rank)
		end 'recordTheLeaf' else 'foldIt'
			if rankOutranks(rank, best: best) 'aNewBest'
				best = rank
			end 'aNewBest'
		end 'foldIt'
	end 'eachBlock'

	if intoTree 'buildTheTournament'
		// Into the seed rank the fold above did not use, rather than into a fresh one — this call is
		// the only place a `PeakTree` query has an empty rank of its own already lying spare.
		scratch.tree.build()
		scratch.tree.fillBest(best)
	end 'buildTheTournament'

	return materializePeak(module, liveness: liveness, rank: best, scratch: scratch)
end 'analyzePressure'

// Does `candidate` outrank `best`? THE one ranking, shared by the whole-function fold and by the
// tournament that replaces it — so the two cannot resolve a tie differently, which they must not: in a
// pressured loop nearly every op ties, and a different tie-break is a different victim and different
// code. It returns TRUE on an exact tie, which is how the sweep resolves one (the last offered wins).
function rankOutranks(candidate PeakRank, best PeakRank) returns bool
	if not candidate.found 'nothingOffered'
		return false
	end 'nothingOffered'
	return peakOutranks(not candidate.clobberOnly, atFixedRegisterOp: candidate.atFixedRegisterOp, pressure: candidate.pressure, best: best)
end 'rankOutranks'

// ONE BLOCK's backward sweep, computing both quantities a spill decision needs: that block's peak
// OVER-POOL op (a candidate for where a victim is chosen) and, for every value DEFINED in it, the
// live-set population right after its def (the pressure its spill store would face, see
// `storeFitsPool`).
//
// IT IS PER BLOCK BECAUSE A SPLIT DIRTIES ONLY A FEW. A block's peak is a function of its own ops, its
// own live sets, its own dead-phi count, and the effective pools of the values live in it — every one
// of which a split leaves alone outside the region it touched. `defLiveAfter[w]` likewise moves only
// for a `w` DEFINED in a dirty block: the live set at any other def point is unchanged.
//
// The peak is the op whose demand most exceeds the registers actually available to it. Three things
// make this agree with the colorer rather than leaving it to panic:
//
//  1. EFFECTIVE pressure carries the SAME corrections TargetLiveness folds into `maxPressure`:
//     the reuse-copy transient (`reuseInputOutlivesOp` — a two-address input that outlives its
//     op costs one extra register for the `mov dest,input` the colorer inserts before it) and
//     the per-block dead-phi count (`deadPhiCountForBlock` — the colorer reserves a register for
//     each dead block-arg across the whole block). Without them a transient/dead-phi overflow
//     enters the driver loop yet finds no RAW peak here — a false panic.
//
//  2. HALL'S CONDITION on the per-value effective pools (`HallCondition`) decides the rest. A
//     forbidden set SHRINKS a value's pool — a value live across a call is confined to the 5
//     callee-saved registers, one live across an `idiv` to `pool ∖ {rax,rdx}` — so values at one
//     point can be shopping in differently sized register sets, and no single number decides
//     whether they fit. The test is: no register subset may have more values CONFINED to it than it
//     has registers. That subsumes the old `raw > popcount(pool ∖ op.implicitDefs)` check (at a
//     call, every live-across value is confined to the callee-saved subset, so the witness is that
//     subset and the count is the same `raw`) AND catches what that check structurally could not —
//     values confined by DIFFERENT calls, colliding at a point that is not itself a call, where the
//     nominal pool is the full 14 and nothing looked wrong. That was a colorer panic; see
//     `HallCondition`'s header.
//
//  3. The census the screen reads is maintained INCREMENTALLY (`stepCensusBackwardOverOp`), like the
//     live count, so no op walks its live set — and it is skipped ENTIRELY, not merely screened out,
//     whenever nothing will read it. `censusNeeded` below lists the three ways that happens and why
//     the gate has to be on the STEP rather than on the screen.
//
// Every quantity comes from the operand model + the liveness `forbidden` column, never a hardcoded
// opcode, so calls, `idiv`, `cqo` and argument pre-moves are handled uniformly. Mirrors
// TargetLiveness's pressure sweep.
//
// ⚠⚠ IT WALKS THE SAME DIRTY BLOCK BACKWARD OVER THE SAME OPS THAT `sweepBlockPressure` JUST DID, AND
// THE TWO CANNOT BE FUSED. They are ORDERED, not redundant, and the order is a fact about what each
// one WRITES and what the other READS — so anyone who reaches for the obvious saving needs this
// paragraph rather than a benchmark. Both walks are named as separate per-split terms in
// ARCHITECTURE.md's shape warning; that is what they are, and it is not a lapse.
//
//  • `sweepBlockPressure` WRITES `forbidden`, and this reads `EffectivePools`, which is
//    `popcount(pool ∖ forbidden(v))`. A split clears the victim's mask and the fresh ids' and the
//    dirty sweep rebuilds them by ORing in each dirty block's clobbers — so the victim's mask is not
//    final until the LAST dirty block has been swept, and `refreshAfterSplit` refreshes their pools
//    only after that loop. A fused walk would read block 0's effective pools before block 5's
//    clobbers had been folded into them, and Hall's condition would be tested against a value's pool
//    as it looked partway through a rebuild.
//  • Even WITHIN one block the order holds: `sweepBlockPressure`'s dead-phi count is computed at the
//    END of its backward walk (the row is only the live-IN view once), and this adds that count to
//    EVERY op's effective demand. The number is not available at the first op of a walk that is also
//    producing it.
//  • And the two walks do not even run over the same block SET. `refreshAfterSplit` sweeps the dirty
//    region; `refreshPeak` sweeps the dirty region OR, on the mode flip, the whole function.
//
// ⭐ THE PEAK HALF OF THAT SECOND WALK IS GONE — `PressureIndex` is the per-op incremental structure
// ARCHITECTURE.md names, and `wantFullPool: false` is what reads the answer off it instead of sweeping
// for it. What remains here is the CONFINED half, whose verdict is a bipartite matching over the live
// values rather than a number a suffix sum can carry, and which only runs at all once no op in the
// function overflows its full pool. `sweepBlockPressure`'s own walk (the `forbidden` masks) is the other
// half of the pair and is still a walk.
//
// ⚠ `wantConfined` IS A COST GATE, NOT A CORRECTNESS ONE, and it is the whole reason the confined half
// is separable. A FULL-POOL overflow outranks EVERY confined one unconditionally (`peakOutranks`), so a
// confined rank can only ever BE the peak when no op in the WHOLE FUNCTION overflows its full pool.
// While one does, every exact Hall confirmation this sweep runs is computed and then discarded by the
// tournament — and the exact confirmation is a bipartite matching, three orders of magnitude dearer
// than the rest of the per-op step. `refreshPeak` therefore sweeps with `wantConfined: false` first and
// only re-sweeps for the confined half when the full-pool half comes back empty. Passing `false` when
// a confined rank COULD have won would silently lose a peak, so the caller owns that argument.
// ⚠ `wantFullPool` IS THE SECOND COST GATE, AND UNLIKE `wantConfined` IT SWAPS THE SOURCE RATHER THAN
// SKIPPING AN ANSWER. FALSE reads the full-pool half off the POSITIONAL INDEX in O(log ops) instead of
// deriving it here; TRUE derives it in this walk, which is what the whole-function analysis and
// `verifyIncremental`'s independent rebuild want — the walk is the ORACLE the index is checked against,
// so it must stay a second, genuinely separate derivation and not a call into the thing it checks.
//
// EXACTLY ONE of the two ever runs, for the reason the whole-function/tournament pair gives: two live
// answers to one question move the peak silently the day they disagree.
function analyzeBlockPressure(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask, scratch SplitScratch, blockLocal CfgIndex, wantFullPool bool, wantConfined bool) returns PeakRank
	let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
	let wordsPerRow = liveness.wordsPerRow
	// Every buffer below is the FUNCTION's, not this call's (see `SplitScratch`). `liveRow` is zero on
	// entry and zero again on exit (`clearRowAfterSweep`), which is the invariant that lets a per-split
	// sweep skip the O(values/64) wholesale clear the whole-function one could afford.
	var liveRow = scratch.sweep.liveRow
	var buf = scratch.sweep.buf
	var ordered = scratch.sweep.ordered
	var seedBuf = scratch.seedBuf

	let effPoolSizes = scratch.effPools.sizes
	// THREE ways the census is unnecessary, and it is skipped ENTIRELY — not merely screened out — under
	// any of them, because the gate has to be on the STEP and not just on the screen: the screen is
	// per-op, but the census is stepped over every OPERAND of every op.
	//
	//  • THIS SWEEP IS NOT ASKING THE CONFINED QUESTION (`wantConfined`). `screenViolates` is the
	//    census's only reader, so a full-pool-only sweep maintains a distribution nothing will look at.
	//  • NOTHING IN THIS FUNCTION IS CONFINED to a register subset (no call, no idiv, no argument
	//    pre-move — `EffectivePools.anyConstrained`), so Hall's condition cannot fail against a proper
	//    subset of the pool and the answer is already known.
	//  • AND THE FUNCTION HOLDS NO FLOAT (`anyXmm`), so the XMM half of every figure is identically
	//    zero and the two files cannot disagree — exactly as it was when there was one register file.
	let censusNeeded = wantConfined and (scratch.effPools.anyConstrained() or scratch.effPools.anyXmm())
	var census = scratch.census
	var censusIds = scratch.censusIds
	var censusWasLive = scratch.censusWasLive
	var liveAtOp = scratch.liveAtOp
	var defLiveAfter = scratch.defLiveAfter

	// The FUNCTION's one running rank, reset for this block and mutated by every op that outranks it —
	// `snapshot`ted into a fresh object once, at the return.
	var best = scratch.rank
	best.clear(blockLocal, pool: pool)

	if not wantFullPool 'fullPoolOffTheIndex'
		offerIndexedFullPoolPeak(module, liveness: liveness, pool: pool, pressure: scratch.pressure, best: best, blockLocal: blockLocal)
		if not wantConfined 'nothingLeftToWalkFor'
			// Neither half needs the walk, which is the whole point: in FULL-POOL mode a dirty block's rank
			// is a handful of node reads, not a pass over its ops.
			return best.snapshot()
		end 'nothingLeftToWalkFor'
	end 'fullPoolOffTheIndex'

	let i = blockLocal
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: i)
	// Seed the ONE dense working row from the SPARSE live-out list — O(live), not a full
	// value-space row copy per block. `clearRowAfterSweep` below returns it to zero the same way.
	liveness.seedRowFromLiveOut(i, row: liveRow, scratch: seedBuf)
	// The row's live population, PER FILE, maintained INCREMENTALLY across the block's ops by the same
	// step that flips the bits. A popcount over the whole row at every op would be O(values/64) per op
	// — O(ops × values/64) per function, and on a large function the single biggest cost left in the
	// allocator. `classRowTotal` is the mixed population when one is wanted; it is derived, so there is
	// no second counter to drift.
	seedClassLive(scratch.sweep.liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass, values: seedBuf)

	// The census tracks the SAME set as `liveRow`, so it is seeded and cleared with it.
	if censusNeeded 'seedTheCensus'
		census.reset(pool, file: liveness.registerFile)
		for v in seedBuf 'seedCensus'
			census.add(try effPoolSizes.get(v) otherwise panic("analyzeBlockPressure: live-out value {v} is outside the function's value space"), class: liveness.classOf(v))
		end 'seedCensus'
	end 'seedTheCensus'

	// A per-block constant added to every op's effective pressure — the colorer holds a
	// register for each dead block-arg across the WHOLE block. Read from the column the
	// liveness sweep already filled; recomputing it here meant re-sweeping the whole block and
	// heap-allocating two arrays, once per block, on every splitter iteration.
	// PER CLASS: a dead phi reserves a register of ITS OWN file and competes for nothing in the other.
	var liveByClass = scratch.sweep.liveByClass
	var effective = scratch.opEffective

	ordered.clear()
	collectBlockOps(block, out: ordered)
	let opCount = ordered.count()
	// The sweep runs BACKWARD (to fold live-out into live-before). The sequence number
	// must be the FORWARD layout position so it aligns with `UseIndex`'s numbering (which is
	// forward): op at reverse-index `ri` sits at forward position `opCount - 1 - ri`.
	for ri in 0 upto opCount 'eachOpRev'
		let forwardPos = opCount - 1 - ri
		let opIdx = try ordered.get(forwardPos) otherwise panic("analyzeBlockPressure: ordered.get OOB")
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("analyzeBlockPressure: opIdx not in module.ops")
		targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)

		// `liveRow` here is this op's live-AFTER set (the store point for anything it defines), and
		// `liveByClass` is its population — no popcount needed. VERIFY MODE only: the shipping answer to
		// "what pressure would this value's store face?" comes off the positional index (`defPointPressure`),
		// and this column exists to be the second, independent derivation that check compares against.
		if VerifyIncrementalSplit 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'
			recordDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter, buf: buf, liveByClass: liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass)
		end 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'

		// The op's transient is decided on the live-AFTER set — BEFORE the backward step re-adds this
		// op's uses — exactly as TargetLiveness.stepBackwardOverOp reads it, through the same one
		// implementation, so the two cannot disagree about which FILE it lands in.
		classRowZero(effective)
		addOpTransientPressure(buf, liveRow: liveRow, classes: liveness.valueClass, pressureOut: effective)
		// The CONFINED half of the same question, which the full-pool walk above cannot answer — see
		// `opConfinedTransient`.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT MUST BE ASKED HERE AND CANNOT BE DEFERRED INTO `maybeConfined` BELOW, which is where
		// its answer is USED.** It reads the live-AFTER row, and the backward step a few lines down
		// turns that row into live-before; asking it after the step would silently answer a different
		// question. So the saving is a GUARD, not a move: both consumers of the answer sit inside
		// `censusNeeded`, so a block without a census pays nothing and one with a census is unchanged.
		var transientHeld = false
		var transientValue = 0 as ValueId
		if censusNeeded 'confinedTransientNeedsACensusToMatter'
			let (held, heldBy) = opConfinedTransient(buf, liveRow: liveRow, liveness: liveness)
			transientHeld = held
			transientValue = heldBy
		end 'confinedTransientNeedsACensusToMatter'

		if censusNeeded 'stepWithCensus'
			stepCensusBackwardOverOp(liveRow, buf: buf, census: census, effPoolSizes: effPoolSizes, classes: liveness.valueClass, liveByClass: liveByClass, ids: censusIds, wasLive: censusWasLive)
		end 'stepWithCensus' else 'stepPlain'
			stepRowBackwardOverOperands(liveRow, base: 0, buf: buf, liveByClass: liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass)
		end 'stepPlain'

		// This op's EFFECTIVE demand, per file: the live-before population of that file, plus the
		// transient already folded in above, plus that file's dead-phi reservations.
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			classRowSet(effective, class: class, value: classRowGet(effective, class: class) + classRowGet(liveByClass, class: class) + liveness.deadPhiCountOf(i, class: class))
		end 'eachClass'
		let seq = seqAt(i, pos: forwardPos)

		// TWO overflows, counting TWO different demands. Lumping them over-counts the confined
		// case — it would over-spill at every call and inflate an E5001 deficit.
		//
		//  • FULL-POOL demand is `effective`: every register the colorer holds at this point,
		//    INCLUDING the dead-phi reservations and the reuse-copy transient. A dead phi is not
		//    live and a transient is not a value, so neither is CONFINED by anything — they
		//    compete for the machine, never for the callee-saved subset — which is exactly why
		//    they belong here and nowhere else. Every value is confined to the pool, so the
		//    witness is the pool itself and the overflow is plain pigeonhole: no Hall search is
		//    needed, and none is run.
		//
		//  • CONFINED demand is Hall's, over the LIVE values only, against a proper subset of the
		//    pool. Checked only when the point fits the full pool — which also bounds the live set
		//    at `poolSize`, so the exact confirmation below can never be a hot-path cost.
		//
		// A FULL-pool overflow outranks any confined one, so full-pool peaks are always relieved
		// FIRST. That ordering is what makes `storeFitsPool` sound at a confined peak: by the time
		// one is reached, no program point exceeds the full pool, so every value's def-point
		// pressure fits and a store anchor is always available.
		let atFixedRegisterOp = opIsFixedRegisterPoint(op, opFacts: liveness.opFacts)

		// THE FULL-POOL PIGEONHOLE, PER FILE. Each file is checked against ITS OWN pool, and its
		// witness is ITS OWN pool — every value of that class is confined to it, so the overflow is
		// plain pigeonhole and no Hall search is needed. Asking one MIXED figure against one MIXED
		// pool is what stops this firing at all: 15 live ints never trip `15 > 30`, the splitter
		// relieves nothing, and the colorer panics with every register blocked.
		// ⚠ THE OVERFLOW TEST RUNS WHATEVER `wantFullPool` SAYS, because the CONFINED half is gated on it
		// ("checked only where NO file overflows its own pool"). Only the OFFER is conditional.
		var anyFullPoolOverflow = false
		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			if classRowGet(effective, class: class) > classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: class, file: liveness.registerFile) 'fullPoolOverflow'
				anyFullPoolOverflow = true
				if wantFullPool 'thisWalkOwnsTheFullPoolHalf'
					betterPeak(best, pressure: classRowGet(effective, class: class), blockLocal: i, forwardPos: forwardPos, peakSeq: seq, clobberOnly: false, witness: poolOfClass(pool, class: class, file: liveness.registerFile), witnessClass: class, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp)
				end 'thisWalkOwnsTheFullPoolHalf'
			end 'fullPoolOverflow'
		end 'eachClass'

		// CONFINED demand is Hall's, over the LIVE values only, against a proper subset of one file's
		// pool. Checked only where NO file overflows its own pool — which also bounds each file's live
		// set at its pool size, so the exact confirmation can never be a hot-path cost.
		if not anyFullPoolOverflow 'maybeConfined'
			// ⭐⭐ **THE TRANSIENT JOINS THE CONFINED QUESTION TOO, AS AN ORDINARY PARTICIPANT.** It is a
			// reuse def holding its own register alongside everything live BEFORE this op, and it is an
			// SSA value with its own admissible set — so when it is live across a call it competes for
			// the SAME five callee-saved registers every other call-crossing value competes for.
			// `addOpTransientPressure` names it; its header carries the false premise this replaces.
			// It is added to the census and to the row the exact matching runs over, and taken back out
			// immediately after, so the INCREMENTAL census stays an exact mirror of `liveRow` for the
			// next op (it is stepped, never rebuilt).
			let transientCompetes = censusNeeded and transientHeld
			if transientCompetes 'transientEntersTheCensus'
				census.add(effectivePoolOf(scratch, valueId: transientValue), class: liveness.classOf(transientValue))
			end 'transientEntersTheCensus'

			if censusNeeded and census.screenViolates(maxClassPoolSize(pool, file: liveness.registerFile)) 'screenFired'
				bitsetCollectRow(liveRow, base: 0, wordsPerRow: wordsPerRow, out: liveAtOp)
				if transientHeld 'theTransientIsAtThisPointToo'
					liveAtOp.push(transientValue)
				end 'theTransientIsAtThisPointToo'
				confinedPeakAt(liveness, live: liveAtOp, pool: pool, best: best, blockLocal: i, forwardPos: forwardPos, peakSeq: seq, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp, hall: scratch.hall)
			end 'screenFired'

			if transientCompetes 'transientLeavesTheCensus'
				census.remove(effectivePoolOf(scratch, valueId: transientValue), class: liveness.classOf(transientValue))
			end 'transientLeavesTheCensus'
		end 'maybeConfined'
	end 'eachOpRev'

	// A PHI has no defining op, so the sweep above never recorded a def-point pressure for it.
	// Its def IS this block's ENTRY (block-args arrive in their registers via the edge copies SSA
	// destruction places at the predecessors' ends), so its store anchors at the top of the block
	// and the pressure that store faces is the block's LIVE-IN population — which `liveRow` now
	// holds, the backward sweep having folded every op back to the live-before-first-op view.
	//
	// Without this a phi keeps the "no def point" sentinel (`NoStoreAnchorPressure`), `storeFitsPool`
	// rejects it forever, and NO loop-carried accumulator could ever be spilled at a call — which
	// is precisely the false E5001 the amended Rule 2 exists to eliminate (`sum = sum + f(i)`
	// makes `sum` a phi). `noVictimAtPeak`'s clobber-only panic is what caught this.
	if VerifyIncrementalSplit 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'
		recordPhiDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter, block: block, liveInByClass: liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass)
	end 'theVerifiersOwnDerivation'

	// Return the working row to zero for the next block, in O(live) — see clearRowAfterSweep.
	liveness.clearRowAfterSweep(i, block: block, row: liveRow, scratch: seedBuf)

	// ONE object per block sweep, not one per winning op — and it must be a COPY of the scratch rank
	// rather than the scratch rank itself, because `analyzePressure` folds this block's result against
	// the running best of the blocks before it. See `PeakRank.snapshot`.
	return best.snapshot()
end 'analyzeBlockPressure'

// The effective pool size recorded for `valueId` — how many registers it may actually take, after its
// own `forbidden` set. Named because the census add and the matching remove must quote the same
// number, and a second `sizes.get` spelled at each would be that number written twice.
function effectivePoolOf(scratch SplitScratch, valueId ValueId) returns RegCount
	return try scratch.effPools.sizes.get(valueId) otherwise panic("effectivePoolOf: value {valueId} is outside the function's value space — `effPools.growTo` sizes the column to the CFG's value count before any sweep reads it")
end 'effectivePoolOf'

// The winning rank, with its live-before row attached — the `PressurePeak` the driver, `chooseVictim`
// and the E5001 diagnostic all read. Built ONCE per analysis, for the winner alone.
function materializePeak(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, rank PeakRank, scratch SplitScratch) returns PressurePeak
	// The FUNCTION's row, returned to zero (which is what `fillLiveBeforeOp` requires on entry) — not a
	// fresh one per analysis, which is the per-split byte term the file header's fifth bullet names. The
	// peak this returns POINTS AT it, so it stays the current peak's row until the next analysis refills
	// it (`SplitScratch.peakLiveBefore`).
	var liveBefore = scratch.peakLiveBefore
	zeroRow(liveBefore, wordsPerRow: liveness.wordsPerRow)
	var peakOpIdx = 0 as OpIndex
	if rank.found 'derivePeakRow'
		peakOpIdx = fillLiveBeforeOp(module, liveness: liveness, blockLocal: rank.blockLocal, forwardPos: rank.forwardPos, row: liveBefore, scratch: scratch)
	end 'derivePeakRow'
	return PressurePeak.create(rank.found, pressure: rank.pressure, blockLocal: rank.blockLocal, liveBefore: liveBefore, peakSeq: rank.peakSeq, peakOpIdx: peakOpIdx, clobberOnly: rank.clobberOnly, witness: rank.witness, witnessClass: rank.witnessClass, atFixedRegisterOp: rank.atFixedRegisterOp)
end 'materializePeak'

// The live-BEFORE set at ONE op, re-derived by re-walking its block backward from the block's live-out
// set down to that op — and the `module.ops` index of that op, RETURNED rather than looked up. `row`
// must be zero on entry.
//
// This is the SAME seed (`seedRowFromLiveOut`) and the SAME backward transfer
// (`stepRowBackwardOverOperands`) the sweep itself steps with — not a second implementation of either
// — so it lands on exactly the row the sweep held there. There is no transfer function here to drift
// out of step with the one that found the peak.
//
// ⚠ THE OP INDEX COMES OUT OF THIS WALK BECAUSE THE BLOCK'S LAYOUT MUST BE STATED ONCE. The walk's
// LAST step is by definition the op at `forwardPos`, so taking `peakOpIdx` from it costs nothing and
// cannot disagree with the op the row was folded to. That matters because a disagreement would not
// fail loudly: `peakOpIdx` feeds `usesAtPeakOp` (which values the peak op READS, hence which are
// excluded as victims) and the mutators, so it is a different victim and different code, never a crash.
//
// The ONE statement of a block's execution order is `blockOpAt` — body `opRefs`, then the terminator —
// and `collectBlockOps` is now BUILT from it rather than spelling it out a second time. So reading the
// suffix positionally here is the same statement the list-building consumers read, which is what makes
// it safe to skip building the list at all: this walk usually wants a fraction of a block, and on a
// function that is one basic block `collectBlockOps` was Θ(the function) once per split before the
// walk even started.
//
// It costs ONE partial block sweep per pressure analysis, and it is what buys the removal of a heap
// row plus a full `wordsPerRow` copy at EVERY tying op of EVERY block (see `PeakRank`).
function fillLiveBeforeOp(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, blockLocal CfgIndex, forwardPos BlockOpPos, row BitWordArray, scratch SplitScratch) returns OpIndex
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: liveness.cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	liveness.seedRowFromLiveOut(blockLocal, row: row, scratch: scratch.seedBuf)
	// This walk wants the ROW alone, but the step maintains the per-class tally as it flips bits and
	// that tally panics on underflow — so seed it truthfully, as every other sweep does.
	seedClassLive(scratch.sweep.liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass, values: scratch.seedBuf)

	let opCount = blockOpCount(block)
	if forwardPos >= opCount 'positionPastTheEnd'
		panic("fillLiveBeforeOp: position {forwardPos} past the end of block {blockLocal}, which lays out {opCount} op(s) — a `PeakRank`'s forward position always names an op the sweep that produced it visited")
	end 'positionPastTheEnd'

	// Step back over the ops at forward positions `opCount-1` down to `forwardPos` INCLUSIVE: after the
	// step over the op AT `forwardPos`, the row is that op's live-BEFORE set — and the op it just
	// stepped over is the one at `forwardPos`, which is what comes back.
	//
	// ⚠ READ POSITIONALLY, NOT FROM A LIST. `collectBlockOps` would be Θ(the block) before this walk even
	// starts, which on a function that is one basic block is the whole function once per split — for a
	// suffix that is usually a fraction of it. `blockOpAt` is the same statement of the block's order the
	// list is built from, so the two cannot disagree about where the terminator sits.
	var atForwardPos = 0 as OpIndex
	for ri in 0 upto opCount - forwardPos 'stepBackToPeak'
		let opIdx = blockOpAt(block, pos: opCount - 1 - ri)
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("fillLiveBeforeOp: opIdx not in module.ops")
		targetOpOperands(op, buf: scratch.sweep.buf)
		stepRowBackwardOverOperands(row, base: 0, buf: scratch.sweep.buf, liveByClass: scratch.sweep.liveByClass, classes: liveness.valueClass)
		atForwardPos = opIdx
	end 'stepBackToPeak'
	return atForwardPos
end 'fillLiveBeforeOp'

// The better of `best` and a CONFINED overflow at this op — but only if there IS one. The
// cardinality screen that got us here NEVER MISSES a real violation and is not exact about the rest
// (the forbidden masks are not laminar — see `HallCondition`), so the exact verdict decides. A false
// peak here would spill a value the program could have coloured, which is the same class of harm as
// a false E5001 in a smaller key: pressure relief added to code that was already fine.
// ⚠ PER CLASS, OVER A CLASS-FILTERED LIVE LIST, AND BOTH HALVES MATTER. Hall's condition is a
// matching between values and registers, and the two files share no register — so a matching run over
// a MIXED live list is two independent problems solved as one, and its witness is a set no single
// file's values are confined to. The consistency assert would still pass: with 6 ints and 3 floats,
// `popcount(witness) + (valueCount - matched)` can equal `tight` by coincidence (3 float + 1 int),
// and the deficit that comes back is right by accident and structurally meaningless. `chooseVictim`
// then spills 4 ints to relieve a peak that needed a float moved, or a float to relieve a GPR peak —
// freeing a register no confined value can use, so the peak survives and the driver re-picks forever.
//
// Running it once per file, over that file's values alone, makes every figure it returns a fact about
// one register file: the witness is a subset of that file, the deficit is that file's, and the
// victims `chooseVictim` may offer are exactly the values that can relieve it.
function confinedPeakAt(liveness LivenessResult, live ValueIdArray, pool RegMask, best PeakRank, blockLocal CfgIndex, forwardPos BlockOpPos, peakSeq OpSeq, atFixedRegisterOp bool, hall HallScratch)
	for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
		let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
		classFilterLive(liveness, live: live, class: class, out: hall.classLive)
		if hall.classLive.count() == 0 'noValuesOfThisFile'
			continue
		end 'noValuesOfThisFile'

		let classPool = poolOfClass(pool, class: class, file: liveness.registerFile)
		match confinementOnly(hallVerdictAt(liveness, live: hall.classLive, pool: classPool, scratch: hall), classPool: classPool) 'verdict'
			feasible then continue
			overflow(witness, tight) then betterPeak(best, pressure: tight, blockLocal: blockLocal, forwardPos: forwardPos, peakSeq: peakSeq, clobberOnly: true, witness: witness, witnessClass: class, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp)
		end 'verdict'
	end 'eachClass'
end 'confinedPeakAt'

// Hall's verdict with one shape reported as FEASIBLE: an overflow whose witness is the file's WHOLE
// pool. Such a witness is not a CONFINEMENT — it says the values do not fit the pool at all, which is
// the FULL-POOL overflow — and calling it `clobberOnly` would route the pigeonhole down the
// forced-bracket path that exists for values the ABI has excluded from PART of the pool.
//
// In the block sweep the gate is vacuous, and provably so: Hall runs there only where no file
// overflows its own pool, so each file's live count is at most its pool size and a saturating matching
// is only blocked by a proper subset. It is load-bearing for `confinedOverflowAtPeak`, which asks Hall
// AT a full-pool peak precisely to find the confinement hiding underneath it — and where a "witness"
// of the whole pool is exactly the answer that must not come back.
function confinementOnly(verdict HallVerdict, classPool RegMask) returns HallVerdict
	return match verdict 'v'
		feasible gives HallVerdict.feasible
		overflow(witness, tight) gives HallVerdict.feasible if regMaskPopcount(witness) >= regMaskPopcount(classPool) else HallVerdict.overflow(witness, tight: tight)
	end 'v'
end 'confinementOnly'

// The live values of ONE file, in the ascending id order `bitsetCollectRow` produced — so the
// matching's augmenting order, and therefore its witness and every tie-break downstream of it, stay
// deterministic.
function classFilterLive(liveness LivenessResult, live ValueIdArray, class RegClass, out ValueIdArray)
	out.clear()
	for v in live 'eachLive'
		if liveness.classOf(v) == class 'thisFile'
			out.push(v)
		end 'thisFile'
	end 'eachLive'
end 'classFilterLive'

// ONE block's FULL-POOL rank, READ off the positional index rather than swept for.
//
// ⭐ IT REPRODUCES THE SWEEP'S ORDER, WHICH IS THE WHOLE CONTRACT (see `peakOutranks`). Within one file
// the index's own combine already resolves a tie to the EARLIEST op. ACROSS files the sweep visits ops
// in DECREASING position and, at each op, classes in ASCENDING ordinal, replacing on an exact tie — so
// POSITION dominates and, at one position, the LATER file wins. Slots are monotone in position, so the
// two candidates are compared as SLOTS and no position has to be materialized to decide.
//
// The dead-phi count is a per-BLOCK constant, so adding it after the max is the same as adding it at
// every op — which is why the index does not carry it and the argmax cannot move by it.
function offerIndexedFullPoolPeak(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, pool RegMask, pressure PressureIndex, best PeakRank, blockLocal CfgIndex)
	var haveOne = false
	var winClass = RegClass.gpr
	var winDemand = 0 as PressureDelta
	var winSlot = 0 as PressureSlot

	for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
		let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
		let blockPeak = pressure.blockPeak(blockLocal, class: class)
		if not blockPeak.found 'noOpsInThisBlock'
			continue
		end 'noOpsInThisBlock'

		let demand = blockPeak.pressure + (liveness.deadPhiCountOf(blockLocal, class: class) as PressureDelta)
		if demand <= (classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: class, file: liveness.registerFile) as PressureDelta) 'fitsItsOwnPool'
			continue
		end 'fitsItsOwnPool'
		if haveOne and (demand < winDemand or (demand == winDemand and blockPeak.slot > winSlot)) 'theIncumbentStands'
			continue
		end 'theIncumbentStands'

		haveOne = true
		winClass = class
		winDemand = demand
		winSlot = blockPeak.slot
	end 'eachClass'

	if not haveOne 'nothingOverflowsHere'
		return
	end 'nothingOverflowsHere'

	let forwardPos = pressure.positionOfSlot(blockLocal, slot: winSlot)
	let op = try module.ops.get(pressure.opAtSlot(winSlot)) otherwise panic("offerIndexedFullPoolPeak: the op at slot {winSlot} is not in module.ops")
	betterPeak(best, pressure: winDemand as RegCount, blockLocal: blockLocal, forwardPos: forwardPos, peakSeq: seqAt(blockLocal, pos: forwardPos), clobberOnly: false, witness: poolOfClass(pool, class: winClass, file: liveness.registerFile), witnessClass: winClass, atFixedRegisterOp: opIsFixedRegisterPoint(op, opFacts: liveness.opFacts))
end 'offerIndexedFullPoolPeak'

// `best` OVERWRITTEN by the candidate peak iff the candidate outranks it. The ONE place a candidate
// enters the ranking, so both overflow kinds agree on it.
//
// It mutates rather than returning a replacement because `best` is the sweep's ONE scratch rank — see
// `PeakRank`'s mutators for why a fresh object per winning op was Θ(block) allocations per block sweep.
function betterPeak(best PeakRank, pressure RegCount, blockLocal CfgIndex, forwardPos BlockOpPos, peakSeq OpSeq, clobberOnly bool, witness RegMask, witnessClass RegClass, atFixedRegisterOp bool)
	if not peakOutranks(not clobberOnly, atFixedRegisterOp: atFixedRegisterOp, pressure: pressure, best: best) 'keepBest'
		return
	end 'keepBest'
	best.takeOver(pressure, atBlock: blockLocal, atPos: forwardPos, seq: peakSeq, confined: clobberOnly, tightSet: witness, tightClass: witnessClass, atClobber: atFixedRegisterOp)
end 'betterPeak'

// Is `op` itself a FIXED-REGISTER point — does it clobber registers implicitly (a call's 9
// caller-saved, an `idiv`'s RAX/RDX)? Read off the operand model's metadata, so a new fixed-register
// op needs no edit here — through the per-variant memo, because this is asked at EVERY op of every
// re-swept block and the metadata read is a select chain over the whole union (`OpVariantFacts`).
function opIsFixedRegisterPoint(op TargetOp, opFacts OpVariantFacts) returns bool
	return opFacts.implicitDefsOf(op) != 0
end 'opIsFixedRegisterPoint'

// Does a candidate overflow outrank the best peak so far?
//
// A FULL-pool overflow always outranks a CONFINED (clobber-only) one, whatever their pressures —
// which is what guarantees full-pool peaks are relieved FIRST (see the caller). Within a class, the
// higher pressure wins.
//
// At EQUAL pressure a confined candidate AT A FIXED-REGISTER OP outranks one at a normal op, and
// that tie-break is load-bearing rather than cosmetic. A value confined by a call is confined
// EVERYWHERE it is live, not just at the call — so Hall's condition sees the same violation at every
// op the confined values span, including the loop header's `cmp`. Relieving it at the CALL is the
// minimal repair: the value keeps its register at every other point, and the store/reload bracket
// sits against the op whose own clobber mask created the confinement — the placement the ABI forces,
// and the one it pays for. Without this preference the peak would drift to the earliest op the
// values happen to be live at, widening the bracket for no gain (and moving every forced-spill
// program's code for no reason). A violation visible ONLY at normal ops — values confined by
// DIFFERENT calls, converging where none of them is — has no such op, and is relieved where it is
// seen. That is the case this whole test exists to find.
//
// OTHERWISE THE LAST CANDIDATE OFFERED WINS — this function returns `true` on an EXACT tie, so a tying
// candidate REPLACES the incumbent. Read with the sweep's visit order (`analyzePressure` walks blocks
// in ASCENDING local index and, within a block, ops in DECREASING forward position), that resolves an
// exact tie to **the EARLIEST op of the HIGHEST-INDEXED tying block**.
//
// This comment used to say "ties resolve toward the EARLIER op", which is true WITHIN ONE BLOCK and
// false across blocks — and it is not a harmless imprecision. A confined value is confined at every op
// it is live at, so in a pressured loop Hall reports the same `tight` at every op the confined values
// span, across several blocks: nearly every op is an exact tie, and a peak-finder rewritten from the
// old wording (an argmax over descending GLOBAL sequence, say) would pick a different op, hence a
// different victim, hence different slots — in most spill-heavy programs there are. Anything that
// re-derives this ranking must reproduce the ORDER, not the sentence.
function peakOutranks(fullOverflow bool, atFixedRegisterOp bool, pressure RegCount, best PeakRank) returns bool
	if not best.found 'firstPeak'
		return true
	end 'firstPeak'

	let bestIsFullPool = not best.clobberOnly
	if fullOverflow != bestIsFullPool 'differentClass'
		// The full-pool candidate wins, whichever side it is on.
		return fullOverflow
	end 'differentClass'

	if pressure != best.pressure 'byPressure'
		return pressure > best.pressure
	end 'byPressure'

	if (not fullOverflow) and atFixedRegisterOp != best.atFixedRegisterOp 'byConfiningOp'
		return atFixedRegisterOp
	end 'byConfiningOp'

	return true
end 'peakOutranks'

// Record each block-arg's def-point live count — the block's live-in population (see the caller).
function recordPhiDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter RegCountColumn, block IrBlock, liveInByClass RegCountColumn, classes ValueClassColumn)
	for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
		let class = try classes.get(arg.valueId) otherwise panic("recordPhiDefLiveAfter: phi {arg.valueId} has no recorded register class")
		try defLiveAfter.set(arg.valueId, value: classRowGet(liveInByClass, class: class)) otherwise panic("recordPhiDefLiveAfter: defLiveAfter.set OOB for phi {arg.valueId}")
	end 'eachPhi'
end 'recordPhiDefLiveAfter'

// Record each of the op's virtual defs as living-after at the pressure of ITS OWN FILE (its store
// point). `buf` already holds the op's operands, and `liveByClass` is still the live-AFTER tally.
//
// ⚠ PER FILE, because `storeFitsPool` asks whether the store has a register-free ANCHOR, and a
// register of the other file is no anchor at all: a float's store needs an XMM free at its def, and
// fourteen idle GPRs do not provide one. Recorded as the MIXED total, the question becomes "does the
// whole machine have a register spare", which is both too weak (30 is never exceeded, so every value
// looks anchorable) and about the wrong thing.
function recordDefLiveAfter(defLiveAfter RegCountColumn, buf OperandList, liveByClass RegCountColumn, classes ValueClassColumn)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if operandIsDef(o) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'def'
			try defLiveAfter.set(operandValueId(o), value: classRowGet(liveByClass, class: operandClass(o, classes: classes))) otherwise panic("recordDefLiveAfter: set OOB")
		end 'def'
	end 'eachOperand'
end 'recordDefLiveAfter'

// Return one of the splitter's `SplitScratch` bitset ROWS to `wordsPerRow` words of ZERO, reusing the
// storage it already has. The two callers are the per-split pair `materializePeak` (the peak's
// live-before set) and `usesAtPeakOp` (the peak op's own uses), and both want the same thing: a row of
// the CURRENT value space with nothing left in it from the previous split.
//
// ⚠ GROWN WITH `growFilled`, WHICH IS NOT INTERCHANGEABLE WITH `resize`/`refill` HERE — and `refill` is
// what this used to be. The value space widens with every split (each mints reload ids), so this row is
// a column that is EXTENDED, and `resize` goes through `reserve`, which takes the ask at FACE VALUE: a
// row extended a word at a time is reallocated and copied IN FULL at every tick of `wordsPerRow`. That is
// Θ(wordsPerRow²) words — one whole-row realloc per 64 ids minted, NOT one per split — so it is still
// superlinear in the value space, at about 1/64 of the term being removed here rather than none of it.
// (Worth stating exactly: "it only reallocates on a tick" is TRUE, and is not a reason to go back.)
// `growFilled` grows capacity GEOMETRICALLY (see `grownCapacity` in stdlib/Array.maxon), so a row's
// whole growth is amortized linear in its final value count and this function allocates NOTHING once
// the row is wide enough.
//
// The zeroing is WHOLESALE — O(words), no allocation — rather than an undo of exactly the bits the
// previous fill set. These rows are LIVENESS data: a bit left standing from the previous split is a
// wrong live set, hence a wrong victim and a silently wrong program, and "every word is 0" is checkable
// where "exactly the bits that fill could have set" is an argument. It adds no growth term the pass does
// not already pay per split anyway — `chooseVictim` walks this same row with `bitsetCollectRow`, which
// is O(words) too.
function zeroRow(row BitWordArray, wordsPerRow BitWordCount)
	row.growFilled(wordsPerRow as ElementIndex, value: 0)
	for w in 0 upto wordsPerRow 'eachWord'
		try row.set(w, value: 0) otherwise panic("zeroRow: set OOB at word {w} — the row was just grown to {wordsPerRow} words")
	end 'eachWord'
end 'zeroRow'

// ==========================================================================
// Choosing a victim (Belady/MIN, with remat preferred)
// ==========================================================================

// Choose the value to relieve at the peak, and the SCOPE its split must have. Candidates are the
// values CROSSING the peak op (live-before it, minus the values the peak op itself uses — those are
// needed in a register there) that the peak's witness CONFINES. `none` when no candidate qualifies →
// at a FULL-pool peak that is the HOT overflow (E5001, `raiseRegisterPressure`); at a CONFINED one it
// is a splitter bug (`noVictimAtPeak`).
//
// `placement` is the DRIVER's, not the peak's: it is `placementOf(peak)` for every peak in code an
// author wrote, and `forced` for a full-pool peak in code the COMPILER emitted, which has no author
// E5001 could instruct (`relievePressure`'s `noAuthorToRefuse`). It is what tiers 3 and 4 gate on, so
// "CONFINED-only" below means "forced-placement only" — the two coincide everywhere an author is
// involved, which is everywhere the wording was written for.
//
// FOUR TIERS, IN STRICT ORDER — and the order is the whole cost model:
//
//  1. REMAT of a constant that the eviction-point split KILLS. Free: re-emitting `mov r, imm` before
//     each after-peak use costs no store, no slot and no load.
//  2. THE EVICTION-POINT SPLIT (`SplitScope.afterPeakUses`) of a value not yet in a slot. One store at
//     the def, one reload per after-peak use-block; the before-peak uses keep the original in its
//     register for free. This is the tier every peak, cold or confined, is relieved by whenever it is
//     non-empty — which is every program that compiled before tiers 3 and 4 existed, so adding them
//     cannot change a single instruction of code that already built.
//  3. FULL REMAT (`SplitScope.everyUse`) — CONFINED peaks only. A constant read both before and after
//     a call inside a loop is live across the call around the BACK EDGE, so tier 1 cannot kill it; but
//     re-emitting it before EVERY use can, and that is still free. Preferred over any spill for the
//     same reason tier 1 is.
//  4. THE FULL SPLIT (`SplitScope.everyUse`) at a CONFINED peak, or a RE-RELIEF of a value already in a
//     slot at EITHER kind. The forced half is the bracket the amended Rule 2 owes a loop-invariant that
//     a call inside the loop reads on both sides of itself: it fits the machine (14 registers, 6
//     values), the ABI merely denies it the 9 caller-saved ones, and the only placement that works is a
//     load before each use. The re-relief half is a value tier 2 already spilled at a LATER peak, whose
//     pre-peak use kept the original alive here — see `isForcedBracketVictim` for the measurement.
//     Nothing is searched. See `SplitScope` and `noVictimAtPeak`.
//  5. THE SPILL WHOSE STORE ANCHOR IS ITSELF OVER-POOL — tier 2 with `storeFitsPool` dropped and
//     `killsValueAtPeak` left to `splitScopeAt` (tier 4's arrangement, for tier 4's reason: the FULL
//     split reloads before EVERY use, so it kills whatever the eviction-point split cannot), reached
//     only when tiers 1-4 are all empty. That gate defers a value on the promise of a later iteration,
//     and the promise fails on a function whose pressure stays over the pool across the whole stretch
//     that holds its defs: nothing is ever schedulable and a program that fits the machine is refused.
//     See `isCrowdedStoreVictim`.
//
// Tier 3 is CONFINED-only, and so is tier 4's FULL-SPLIT half, because a FULL-pool peak that tier 2
// cannot relieve by writing a NEW store is precisely the program E5001 exists to refuse: the loop's
// genuine working set is larger than the machine, and reloading at every use inside the loop is the
// per-iteration cost this design will not silently pay. That argument is about a LOOP, and it does not
// reach a value whose def and every use are at depth 0 — which is why neither tier 4's re-relief half
// nor tier 5 is confined to confined peaks.
//
// Within a tier the victim is the one with the FARTHEST next use (Belady/MIN). Tiers 2 and 4 additionally
// want the store to FIT the pool (`storeFitsPool`, `defLiveAfter[v] ≤ pool`), preferring a value whose def
// point a later iteration need not revisit; tier 5 is where that preference gives way. A remat and the
// re-relief of a value already in a slot write no store at all, so neither carries the constraint.
//
// The candidates are the SET BITS of the peak's live row, walked with `bitsetCollectRow` (which
// yields them in ascending id order, so the strict-improvement tie-break below still resolves ties
// to the lowest id). Iterating `0 upto valueCount` instead — testing each id for liveness — was
// O(values) per split for an answer of size O(pressure), and every eligibility question it then
// asked walked the whole function. That is what the `UseIndex` retires.
function chooseVictim(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, pool RegMask, scratch SplitScratch, placement SpillPlacement) returns SpillChoice
	let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
	// Every candidate below is of the peak's own file (the class gate in the loop), so the pool a
	// store anchor is measured against is THAT file's — 14 for an int, 16 for a float, never the 30
	// the two files hold between them.
	let poolSize = classPoolSizeOf(pool, class: peak.witnessClass, file: liveness.registerFile)
	// The cut this peak names, re-pointed on the ONE anchor the function owns. `relievePressure` reads
	// it back for the mutation, which is the same cut and must not be a second derivation of it.
	var anchor = scratch.anchor
	anchor.takePeak(peak, cutPlacement: placement)
	var usedAtPeak = usesAtPeakOp(module, liveness: liveness, peak: peak, scratch: scratch)
	var candidates = scratch.candidates
	bitsetCollectRow(peak.liveBefore, base: 0, wordsPerRow: liveness.wordsPerRow, out: candidates)

	// `killsValueAtPeak` walks the CFG forward from the peak, stopping at the candidate's def
	// block — so the walk depends only on that def block, and the candidates crossing one peak
	// overwhelmingly share it (a loop's accumulators are all phis of the same header). Memoized
	// per def block, the whole `chooseVictim` call costs a handful of walks instead of one per
	// candidate — and the memo SURVIVES the split, because the rows depend only on the CFG (which
	// splitting cannot change) and on the peak block (which it usually does not move: N accumulators
	// across ONE call in a loop peak at that same call for every one of their splits).
	var reach = scratch.reach
	reach.retargetTo(peak.blockLocal)

	var bestRemat = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestSpill = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestFullRemat = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestBracket = BeladyBest.create()
	var bestCrowdedStore = BeladyBest.create()

	for v in candidates 'eachValue'
		if bitsetTest(usedAtPeak, base: 0, bit: v as BitIndex) 'consumedHere'
			continue
		end 'consumedHere'
		if liveness.classOf(v) != peak.witnessClass 'otherFile'
			// A value of the OTHER file can never relieve this overflow: spilling it frees a register no
			// confined value here is able to take, so the peak survives, the potential Φ does not fall,
			// and the driver re-picks forever. The witness carries its file for exactly this test —
			// deriving it from the mask cannot work, because a float live across a call is forbidden every
			// XMM and Hall's tight set for it is EMPTY, naming no bits at all.
			continue
		end 'otherFile'
		if not valueConfinedTo(liveness, valueId: v, pool: pool, witness: peak.witness) 'notConfinedByThisPeak'
			// Only a value the overflow CONFINES can relieve it. At a CONFINED peak the witness is a
			// proper subset of the pool (the 5 callee-saved registers at a call), and spilling a value
			// that could have taken a register OUTSIDE it frees a register the confined values were
			// never able to use — the peak survives, the potential Φ does not fall, and the driver
			// re-picks forever. At a FULL-POOL peak the witness IS the pool, so every live value is
			// confined to it and this gate is a no-op: the E5001 path chooses from exactly the same
			// candidates it always did.
			continue
		end 'notConfinedByThisPeak'

		let nu = nextUseAfterPeak(index, valueId: v, peakSeq: peak.peakSeq)
		if isRematVictim(module, index: index, topo: topo, valueId: v, anchor: anchor, reach: reach) 'const'
			bestRemat.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'const' else if isSpillVictim(index, liveness: liveness, topo: topo, valueId: v, anchor: anchor, pressure: scratch.pressure, poolSize: poolSize, reach: reach) 'spillable'
			bestSpill.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'spillable' else if isFullRematVictim(module, index: index, valueId: v, placement: anchor.placement) 'fullConst'
			bestFullRemat.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'fullConst' else if isForcedBracketVictim(index, liveness: liveness, valueId: v, placement: anchor.placement, pressure: scratch.pressure, poolSize: poolSize) 'bracketable'
			bestBracket.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'bracketable' else if isCrowdedStoreVictim(index, valueId: v, placement: anchor.placement) 'anchorStillOverPool'
			bestCrowdedStore.offer(v, nextUse: nu)
		end 'anchorStillOverPool'
	end 'eachValue'

	if bestRemat.found 'haveRemat'
		return SpillChoice.remat(bestRemat.valueId, scope: SplitScope.afterPeakUses)
	end 'haveRemat'
	if bestSpill.found 'haveSpill'
		return SpillChoice.spill(bestSpill.valueId, scope: SplitScope.afterPeakUses)
	end 'haveSpill'
	if bestFullRemat.found 'haveFullRemat'
		return SpillChoice.remat(bestFullRemat.valueId, scope: SplitScope.everyUse)
	end 'haveFullRemat'
	if bestBracket.found 'haveBracket'
		// The scope is resolved for the WINNER alone: `killsValueAtPeak` is the one eligibility question
		// tier 4 does not need to ask to know the value is relievable (it is relievable either way), so
		// asking it per candidate would be a walk bought for nothing.
		return SpillChoice.spill(bestBracket.valueId, scope: splitScopeAt(index, topo: topo, valueId: bestBracket.valueId, anchor: anchor, reach: reach))
	end 'haveBracket'
	if bestCrowdedStore.found 'haveCrowdedStore'
		return SpillChoice.spill(bestCrowdedStore.valueId, scope: splitScopeAt(index, topo: topo, valueId: bestCrowdedStore.valueId, anchor: anchor, reach: reach))
	end 'haveCrowdedStore'
	return SpillChoice.none
end 'chooseVictim'

// The best victim offered to ONE tier so far, under Belady/MIN. Four tiers each run this same
// tournament, so it is one type rather than four copies of an `id`/`nextUse`/"is it set?" triple —
// and `found` is a field rather than an `id == valueCount` sentinel, which the tiers previously each
// had to spell out (and which only works while `valueCount` happens to be in scope).
type BeladyBest
	export var found as bool
	export var valueId as ValueId
	var farthestNextUse as OpSeq

	export static function create() returns BeladyBest
		return Self{found: false, valueId: 0, farthestNextUse: 0}
	end 'create'

	// Offer a candidate. It wins iff it is the first, or its next use is STRICTLY farther away —
	// strict, and `chooseVictim` offers candidates in ascending id order, so ties resolve to the
	// LOWEST value id and the choice is deterministic.
	export function offer(candidate ValueId, nextUse OpSeq)
		if self.found and nextUse <= self.farthestNextUse 'noImprovement'
			return
		end 'noImprovement'

		self.found = true
		self.valueId = candidate
		self.farthestNextUse = nextUse
	end 'offer'
end 'BeladyBest'

// The global sequence number of `valueId`'s FIRST OP use strictly after `peakSeq` (Belady's
// next-use distance from the peak), or the `seqLimit` sentinel for "no later use".
//
// EDGE uses are deliberately NOT counted — an edge arg is a use the SPLIT must rewrite, but it is
// not a next-use DISTANCE the eviction ranks on, and the forward sweep over op OPERANDS this
// replaces did not see them either.
//
// The sentinel is `seqLimit` (`numBlocks * SeqStride`), which is strictly greater than every real
// sequence number the function can produce, so a value never used again sorts as the FARTHEST next
// use — the best Belady candidate — while still comparing correctly under `>`. (A `u64.max`
// sentinel does NOT: on
// `int(0 to u64.max)` its all-ones pattern compares as negative under `>`, so Belady would collapse
// to first-fit and evict the lowest id rather than the farthest next use.)
//
// A value's use thread is in ascending sequence order (it is appended to in layout order), so the
// FIRST entry past the peak is the nearest — O(uses of this one value), never a sweep.
function nextUseAfterPeak(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, peakSeq OpSeq) returns OpSeq
	let useSites = index.useCount(valueId)
	if useSites == 0 'neverUsed'
		return index.seqLimit
	end 'neverUsed'

	var record = index.firstUseOf(valueId)
	for _ in 0 upto useSites 'each'
		let seq = index.useSeqAt(record)
		let isOpUse = match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
			op gives true
			edge gives false
		end 'kind'
		// An EDGE use is not a next-use candidate: Belady ranks by the next READ THROUGH AN OP.
		if isOpUse and seq > peakSeq 'later'
			return seq
		end 'later'
		record = index.nextUseOf(record)
	end 'each'
	return index.seqLimit
end 'nextUseAfterPeak'

// True iff `valueId`'s def-point live-set fits the pool. The store ALWAYS anchors at the def (that
// is the only point that dominates every reload — see the "STORE ANCHOR IS THE VALUE'S DEF" note),
// and `defLiveAfter[v]` is the pressure exactly there, so this asks whether the def point a store would
// land at is one a later iteration must come back to anyway.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A PREFERENCE, NOT A LEGALITY CONDITION**, and the tier order is what keeps that honest: tiers
// 2 and 4 consult it to spend the cheap victims first, and TIER 5 spills without it when they are
// exhausted. A store needs no register of its own — it reads a value the defining op has just put in one
// — so a def point still over the pool costs only its own later relief, and refusing every candidate on
// that ground refuses a program that fits the machine (`isCrowdedStoreVictim`).
//
// A value already IN a slot skips this gate entirely — its store exists and is not re-emitted.
//
// ⚠ THE "CANNOT REFUSE ANYTHING AT A CONFINED PEAK" CLAUSE ABOVE HOLDS FOR A PEAK THE ANALYSIS RANKED,
// AND NOT FOR THE ONE `confinedOverflowAtPeak` SYNTHESIZES. That peak is asked for precisely because a
// full-pool point still exists, so a def point CAN still be over-pool and this gate CAN refuse there.
// That is not a hole: it is why the driver treats an unrelievable synthesized peak as "no relief
// available" and reports the FULL-POOL overflow it originally ranked, rather than letting it reach
// `noVictimAtPeak`'s confined panic, which asserts an invariant only a ranked peak carries.
function storeFitsPool(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, valueId ValueId, poolSize RegCount) returns bool
	return defPointPressure(liveness, pressure: pressure, valueId: valueId) <= (poolSize as PressureDelta)
end 'storeFitsPool'

// The live population of `valueId`'s own file at the point its spill STORE would land — right after its
// defining op, or at its block's entry when it is a phi. Read off the positional index in O(log ops)
// rather than carried as a per-value column: a split moves the population over a stretch Θ(the
// function) wide, so every value DEFINED in that stretch would have to be restated, and there is no
// bound on how many that is.
function defPointPressure(liveness LivenessResult, pressure PressureIndex, valueId ValueId) returns PressureDelta
	let index = liveness.useIndex
	match index.defKindOf(valueId) 'kind'
		none then return NoStoreAnchorPressure as PressureDelta
		opDef then return pressure.liveAfterSlot(index.defBlockOf(valueId), slot: pressure.defSlotOf(valueId), class: liveness.classOf(valueId))
		phiDef then return pressure.blockLiveIn(index.defBlockOf(valueId), class: liveness.classOf(valueId))
	end 'kind'
end 'defPointPressure'

// The set of values the peak op READS (its virtual uses) — excluded from the candidate pool
// because they are needed in a register AT the peak, so spilling them across it is impossible.
//
// This exclusion can never empty the candidate pool at a CONFINED peak, which is one leg of the
// invariant `noVictimAtPeak` asserts: an op names at most TWO virtual registers, while the tight set
// outnumbers the witness and any witness a clobber produces holds at least the five callee-saved
// registers — so at least four confined values survive this cut.
function usesAtPeakOp(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, scratch SplitScratch) returns BitWordArray
	var row = scratch.usedAtPeak
	zeroRow(row, wordsPerRow: liveness.wordsPerRow)
	let op = try module.ops.get(peak.peakOpIdx) otherwise panic("usesAtPeakOp: peak op not in module.ops")
	var buf = scratch.sweep.buf
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) 'use'
			bitsetAdd(row, base: 0, bit: operandValueId(o) as BitIndex)
		end 'use'
	end 'eachOperand'
	return row
end 'usesAtPeakOp'

// ==========================================================================
// Victim eligibility
// ==========================================================================

// Is `valueId` a TIER-1 victim — a constant the EVICTION-POINT re-emit (`SplitScope.afterPeakUses`)
// actually KILLS at the peak? Its def must be a constant materialization, AND re-emitting only its
// after-peak uses must leave it dead across the peak — the same second gate the eviction-point spill
// has, and for the same reason.
//
// `killsValueAtPeak` is not a spill-specific test: remat partitions a value's uses around the peak
// EXACTLY as a spill does (after-peak uses are rewritten to fresh copies, before-peak uses keep the
// original), so the original is dead across the peak under precisely the same condition. Without
// this gate a constant whose every use PRECEDES the peak in layout order but which is live across it
// AROUND A BACK EDGE — `let c = 7` used only in a `while i mod c < 3` header, with the loop's peak
// in the body below — is chosen, re-emits NOTHING (it has no after-peak use), drops nothing, and
// relieves nothing. The potential Φ does not decrease, the next iteration re-picks the same value,
// and the driver spins until the runaway bound panics. That is exactly the failure the spill path's
// `killsValueAtPeak` was added to prevent; the remat path simply never got it. Such a constant is not
// un-relievable — at a CONFINED peak `isFullRematVictim` re-emits it before EVERY use, which is still
// free — it merely cannot be relieved by the narrow re-emit.
//
// Neither remat tier needs a `hasSpillStore`-style already-done guard, because a rematerializable
// value is NEVER spilled: at every peak it either takes a remat tier or is refused, and the spill
// tiers sit behind them in the same `else if` chain. So it never acquires a store to guard against.
function isRematVictim(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, anchor SplitAnchor, reach ReachCache) returns bool
	if not isRematerializable(module, index: index, valueId: valueId) 'notConst'
		return false
	end 'notConst'
	return killsValueAtPeak(index, topo: topo, valueId: valueId, anchor: anchor, reach: reach)
end 'isRematVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-2 victim — the eviction-point split, the cheapest spill there is? Four
// independent gates, all necessary:
//
//  1. not already stored — a value an earlier iteration already wrote to a slot needs no second
//                          store, so its relief is tier 4's (reloads only), not this one's.
//                          ⚠ It asks `hasSpillStore`, the NARROW question, deliberately: a RELOAD id
//                          is slot-RESIDENT without ever having been stored, and refusing those here
//                          would leave them with no tier at all at a COLD peak (tiers 3 and 4 are
//                          confined-only) — a false E5001 on a program that compiles today. Tier 2
//                          relieves them correctly: `spillOneValue` skips the store for anything
//                          resident, so the emitted code is the reloads-only bracket either way.
//  2. `isSpillable`      — its shape suits the peak's PLACEMENT (cold needs a value whose def and
//                          every use — op operand, PHI block entry and branch-edge arg alike — are at
//                          loop depth 0; forced needs only a store anchor). ⚠ It said "non-phi,
//                          non-edge-passed" until BATCH2 removed those two blanket bars: both were
//                          over-approximations of "inside a loop" made while no depth was recorded for
//                          a phi or an edge, and both were FALSE-E5001 generators. The fact is a
//                          DEPTH — see `isColdSpillable`.
//  3. `storeFitsPool`    — its def point is not itself over-pool, so the store lands somewhere a later
//                          iteration need not revisit. A PREFERENCE, and the one tier 5 spends last.
//  4. `killsValueAtPeak` — the eviction-point split actually makes it DEAD at the peak. Without this
//                          the splitter emits stores that relieve nothing (see `killsValueAtPeak`).
//                          A value that fails HERE is not un-relievable — at a confined peak the FULL
//                          split still kills it (tier 4) — it just cannot be relieved this cheaply.
function isSpillVictim(index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, anchor SplitAnchor, pressure PressureIndex, poolSize RegCount, reach ReachCache) returns bool
	if index.hasSpillStore(valueId) 'alreadyStored'
		return false
	end 'alreadyStored'
	if not isSpillable(index, valueId: valueId, placement: anchor.placement) 'wrongShape'
		return false
	end 'wrongShape'
	if not storeFitsPool(liveness, pressure: pressure, valueId: valueId, poolSize: poolSize) 'noStoreAnchor'
		return false
	end 'noStoreAnchor'
	return killsValueAtPeak(index, topo: topo, valueId: valueId, anchor: anchor, reach: reach)
end 'isSpillVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-3 victim — a constant the eviction-point split could not kill, but re-emitting
// at EVERY use can? Still free (no store, no slot, no load), so it outranks every spill.
//
// CONFINED peaks only. At a FULL-pool peak a constant the loop reads every iteration is part of the
// loop's genuine working set, and rescuing it here would silently move the E5001 cliff — the line this
// design draws between "the placement is forced" and "the machine is too small" (see `chooseVictim`).
function isFullRematVictim(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	return isForcedPlacement(placement) and isRematerializable(module, index: index, valueId: valueId)
end 'isFullRematVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-4 victim — relievable by the FORCED bracket, whatever `killsValueAtPeak` says,
// or by a RE-RELIEF of a value memory ALREADY HOLDS?
//
// This is the tier that makes `noVictimAtPeak`'s confined panic unreachable, and it is deliberately
// TOTAL over the confined values: given a store anchor and somewhere to put the store, EVERY value can
// be made dead across the peak (`splitScopeAt` picks how). It refuses only what it structurally must:
//
//  • the wrong SHAPE for the placement — `hasStoreAnchor` at a confined peak (impossible for a value
//    this function defines, but this is the one place it is stated, so it is asked rather than
//    assumed), and the full Rule 2 shape at a cold one, for the reason below;
//  • a NEW store that does not fit the pool — a value memory already holds needs no new store at all,
//    so that gate applies only to a spill that must write one;
//  • at a COLD peak, a value with no slot yet — that is exactly what TIER 2 is, and tier 2 has already
//    declined it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RE-RELIEF ARM SERVES BOTH KINDS OF PEAK, AND THE "CONFINED PEAKS ONLY" RULE THAT USED TO
// COVER THIS WHOLE TIER WAS TOO WIDE BY ITS OWN ARGUMENT.** That rule's reason is tier 3's: *"at a
// FULL-pool peak, reloading a value the LOOP uses at every one of its uses is exactly the
// per-iteration cost E5001 refuses to pay silently."* A COLD-spillable value has its def and EVERY use
// at loop depth 0 (`isColdSpillable`) — there is no loop and so no per-iteration cost, and Rule 2 holds
// for the same reason it holds for tier 2. What the wider rule actually refused was a second split of a
// value tier 2 had already spilled, which is not "the machine is too small" at all:
//
//   MEASURED (P1.9, 16 guarded values live across their own guard chain). `chooseVictim` resolves an
//   exact pressure tie to the HIGHEST-INDEXED tying block (`peakOutranks`), so the driver relieved the
//   LAST cascade first and walked backwards. Each `afterPeakUses` split rewrote only the uses after
//   THAT peak, leaving the value's earlier guard `cmp` holding the original in a register — so every
//   earlier peak still saw all 16 live, and by the time the FIRST cascade was reached 14 of them were
//   `hasSpillStore` and tier 2 refused them all. E5001 on a program that fits the machine in two
//   registers: every value could have sat in memory and been reloaded before each of its two uses.
//
// Re-relieving one of those at its earliest peak rewrites every use after it — which is all of them —
// and the peak falls. It is STRICTLY ADDITIVE: tiers run in order, so this arm is reached only when
// tiers 1 and 2 found nothing, which is otherwise precisely the state that raises E5001. No program that
// compiles today can take a different decision because of it.
function isForcedBracketVictim(index UseIndex, liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement, pressure PressureIndex, poolSize RegCount) returns bool
	if not isSpillable(index, valueId: valueId, placement: placement) 'wrongShape'
		return false
	end 'wrongShape'
	if index.isSlotResident(valueId) 'alreadyInASlot'
		return true
	end 'alreadyInASlot'
	if not isForcedPlacement(placement) 'coldPeakWithNoSlotIsTier2sToRefuse'
		return false
	end 'coldPeakWithNoSlotIsTier2sToRefuse'

	return storeFitsPool(liveness, pressure: pressure, valueId: valueId, poolSize: poolSize)
end 'isForcedBracketVictim'

// Is `valueId` a TIER-5 victim — a value that still has a store anchor after tiers 1-4 have all declined
// it? It is tier 2 with two gates gone: `storeFitsPool`, for the reason below, and `killsValueAtPeak`,
// which `splitScopeAt` answers instead exactly as it does for tier 4 — the FULL split reloads before
// EVERY use, so it kills across the peak whatever the eviction-point split leaves alive.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`storeFitsPool` IS A SCHEDULING PREFERENCE, NOT A LEGALITY CONDITION, AND WHEN IT REFUSES EVERY
// CANDIDATE THE SCHEDULE HAS DEADLOCKED.** It defers a value whose def point is over-pool on the ground
// that "a spilled sibling frees a register there first, which a later iteration does" — and that
// argument holds only while some OTHER value's def point is already within the pool. A function whose
// pressure stays over the pool across a contiguous stretch wide enough to contain the DEFS of every
// value live at its peak has no such sibling: the peak outranks every def point (`peakOutranks` ranks by
// pressure), so the later iteration the deferral counts on never runs, and a program that fits the
// machine is refused.
//
// MEASURED on `MachOWriter.writeMachO` at x64's 14-GPR pool: 32 values live at one point, every one of
// them cold-spillable, store-anchored and killed by the eviction split — and every one deferred, because
// the cheapest def point among them already carried 15.
//
// The store needs no register of its own: `storeSlotReg slot, v` is spliced immediately after the op
// that defines `v`, so it READS a value that op has just put in a register and defines nothing. A def
// point still over the pool is a peak of its own, and the driver relieves it on a later iteration — the
// loop runs until NO point overflows — so deferring the store buys nothing there either.
//
// STRICTLY ADDITIVE: the `else if` chain offers a value to the first tier that takes it, and this tier's
// winner is consulted only after tiers 1-4 have all come back empty — which is precisely the state that
// otherwise raises E5001. No program that compiles today can take a different decision because of it.
//
// ⛔ **IT DOES NOT MOVE THE E5001 CLIFF.** `isSpillable` still applies, so at a COLD peak the value's def
// and every use must be at loop depth 0: there is no iteration, hence none of the per-iteration reload
// cost Rule 2 refuses to pay behind the author's back. A value the LOOP carries is not cold-spillable and
// this tier cannot reach it, so every committed loop overflow still refuses, byte for byte.
function isCrowdedStoreVictim(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	if index.hasSpillStore(valueId) 'alreadyStored'
		// Memory holds it; relieving it again is the re-relief tier 4 owns, and that tier has declined.
		return false
	end 'alreadyStored'
	if not hasStoreAnchor(index, valueId: valueId) 'nowhereToPutTheStore'
		// A LEGALITY condition, unlike the pressure at the anchor, and it must be asked here rather than
		// inherited: the COLD shape test is about loop depth alone, and `storeFitsPool` — which carried
		// this for tier 2, through the `NoStoreAnchorPressure` sentinel — is the gate this tier drops.
		// Without it, malformed IR reaches `insertStore`'s panic instead of failing a gate.
		return false
	end 'nowhereToPutTheStore'
	return isSpillable(index, valueId: valueId, placement: placement)
end 'isCrowdedStoreVictim'

// The narrowest split scope that leaves `valueId` DEAD across the peak: the eviction-point split when
// it suffices, the full split when a before-peak use is still reachable from the peak (see
// `killsValueAtPeak` and `SplitScope`).
function splitScopeAt(index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, anchor SplitAnchor, reach ReachCache) returns SplitScope
	return SplitScope.afterPeakUses if killsValueAtPeak(index, topo: topo, valueId: valueId, anchor: anchor, reach: reach) else SplitScope.everyUse
end 'splitScopeAt'

function isForcedPlacement(placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	return match placement 'p'
		cold gives false
		forced gives true
	end 'p'
end 'isForcedPlacement'

// The placement a peak's relief is entitled to IN CODE AN AUTHOR WROTE: a clobber-only overflow gets
// the FORCED store/reload bracket (any loop depth); a full-pool overflow gets only a COLD split,
// because refusing it is a message to the author and the message is the remedy.
//
// It takes the PEAK alone, so it cannot answer for the one case where whose code it is decides: a
// full-pool peak in a function the compiler emitted also gets the forced bracket, because there is no
// author to refuse to. That override is `relievePressure`'s (`noAuthorToRefuse`) — the only party that
// holds the function — and it passes the result in rather than re-deriving it here.
function placementOf(peak PressurePeak) returns SpillPlacement
	return SpillPlacement.forced if peak.clobberOnly else SpillPlacement.cold
end 'placementOf'

// Can `valueId` be relieved at a peak of this kind? The two kinds have DIFFERENT eligibility, and
// that difference IS the amended Rule 2 (see `SpillPlacement`).
function isSpillable(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, placement SpillPlacement) returns bool
	return match placement 'p'
		cold gives isColdSpillable(index, valueId: valueId)
		forced gives hasStoreAnchor(index, valueId: valueId)
	end 'p'
end 'isSpillable'

// A value is COLD-spillable iff its def — an op def, or a PHI's own block entry — AND every use — an
// op operand or a BRANCH-EDGE ARG alike — are at loop depth 0. That exact shape is what a COLD spill needs: the store
// (after its def) and every reload (before a depth-0 after-peak use) then lie OUTSIDE every loop, so
// no spill code is added to a loop body. A value USED inside a loop is deliberately excluded HERE —
// at a FULL-pool overflow it is the loop's genuine working set, and if that exceeds the machine the
// answer is E5001, not a reload at every use.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THERE USED TO BE A THIRD BAR — "not passed on ANY branch edge" — AND IT WAS A FALSE-E5001
// GENERATOR.** It was the only safe reading available while `touchesLoop` saw op operands only: with
// no depth recorded for an edge use, "edge-passed" was the closest available over-approximation of
// "edge-passed inside a loop". But an edge arg is how EVERY `var` assigned in one arm of an `if`
// reaches the join, so the bar refused values that had nothing to do with any loop. MEASURED on
// `stdlib/URL.maxon`'s `URL.parse`: E5001 against a loop with 23 values live, **nineteen of them used
// ZERO times in it** — every one a result field set after the loop and merely handed across a depth-0
// join. The program fits the machine easily; the splitter was forbidden from relieving it.
// `indexBlockEdgeUses` now records the depth of an edge use like any other, so the question is asked
// once, as a depth, and the blanket bar is gone. `spillOneValue` has always rewritten branch-edge args
// alongside op uses (see its header and `hasStoreAnchor`'s), so nothing else had to change for a
// depth-0 edge use to be relievable — the capability was there and the gate was refusing to reach it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE PHI BAR WAS THE SAME OVER-APPROXIMATION, MEASURED ON THE SAME FUNCTION, AND IT IS GONE
// TOO.** A phi HAS a sound store anchor — the top of its own block, which dominates every use of it —
// and `insertStore` has always used it (`hasStoreAnchor`'s `phiDef` arm, the FORCED path's victim of
// choice). What made a phi look unspillable is that the phis which matter are usually LOOP-HEADER
// phis, and those must never be cold-spilled; but a phi of a depth-0 `if` join is not one, and in
// straight-line code nearly EVERY merged `var` is one. MEASURED on `URL.parse` after the edge bar came
// off: E5001 at the 12-field `URL{…}` literal, 15 values live, **twelve of them depth-0 join phis**
// each carrying one result field. `indexBlockArgDefs` now records a phi block's depth, so the loop
// header is still refused — by the depth, which is the fact that mattered all along.
//
// NOTE this is the FULL-POOL gate only. A fixed-register (clobber-only) peak uses
// `hasStoreAnchor` instead: there the value is NOT over the machine's capacity, so refusing to
// spill it would be a false E5001 (see `PressurePeak.clobberOnly`).
function isColdSpillable(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	return index.defAndUsesAtDepthZero(valueId)
end 'isColdSpillable'

// A value can anchor a spill STORE iff it has a point at which it is already in a register and
// which precedes the peak: an OP DEF (store right after it, or after its last pre-peak use), or a
// PHI (store at the very top of its own block — a block-arg arrives in its register via the edge
// copies SSA destruction places at the predecessors' ends, so it is live at block entry, and its
// block dominates every use of it).
//
// This is the SHAPE test for a FORCED (clobber-only) spill — no loop-depth bar, no phi bar, no
// edge-passed bar. Phis and edge-passed values are exactly the loop-carried accumulators that
// dominate the live-across-a-call set in real code (`sum = sum + f(i)` makes `sum` a phi and its
// update a back-edge arg), so excluding them would leave nothing to spill and turn every such loop
// into a false E5001. `spillOneValue` rewrites branch-edge args alongside op uses, which is what
// makes the edge-passed case sound.
//
// Every value a function DEFINES has one of the two anchors (`buildUseIndex` records an op def or a
// phi block for each), so this never refuses a value that is live at a peak — which is one of the
// four legs of the invariant `noVictimAtPeak` asserts.
function hasStoreAnchor(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	return match index.defKindOf(valueId) 'kind'
		none gives false
		opDef to phiDef gives true
	end 'kind'
end 'hasStoreAnchor'

// A value is REMATERIALIZABLE iff its single def is an integer-constant materialization
// (`movRegImm`/`movRegImm32`). Such a value is re-emitted at each use (never spilled), so —
// unlike a spill — it needs no depth-0 restriction on its uses; but it must still not be a phi
// or edge-passed value (those have no op-use site to re-emit before), so those are excluded.
function isRematerializable(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, valueId ValueId) returns bool
	if index.isPhiValue(valueId) 'phi'
		return false
	end 'phi'
	if index.isEdgePassed(valueId) 'edge'
		return false
	end 'edge'
	match index.defKindOf(valueId) 'kind'
		opDef then return opIsConstMaterialization(module, opIdx: index.requireDefOp(valueId))
		none then return false
		phiDef then return false
	end 'kind'
end 'isRematerializable'

function opIsConstMaterialization(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("opIsConstMaterialization: opIdx not in module.ops")
	return match op 'k'
		movRegImm gives true
		movRegImm32 gives true
		prologue to jcc gives false
		movRegReg to divideReg gives false
		pushReg to loadRegSlot gives false
		// The bit band. None of these is a constant materialization: every one reads at
		// least one register (or, for `setccReg`, EFLAGS), so re-emitting it at a use site
		// would need those inputs live there — which is exactly what remat exists to avoid.
		// ⚠ `leaRegGlobal` is the interesting `false` here. It reads NO register and computes a
		// value that cannot change, so it IS rematerializable in principle — which is exactly what
		// `StdOp.globalAddr`'s `isPure: true` says. But THIS predicate asks a narrower question:
		// "can this def be rebuilt by `constValueOfDef` + a `movRegImm`?" A global's address is a
		// LINK-TIME address, not an integer the compiler holds, so rebuilding it means re-emitting
		// the `lea` AND a fresh relocation — which this remat path has no way to express. Answering
		// `true` would send it to `constValueOfDef`, which would panic.
		//
		// So it is a real missed optimization (a spilled global address would reload from the stack
		// where it could re-`lea` for the same size and no slot), and it is priced: taking it means
		// generalizing remat from "a constant value" to "an op with no register inputs". Not this
		// rung's, and recorded here rather than silently ranged over.
		//
		// ⚠ `movsdRegRip` (P1.0d.4) is the SECOND instance of that exact `false`, for that exact
		// reason. An f64 constant reads no register and cannot change, but it lives behind a
		// RIP-relative relocation into `.rdata`, so rebuilding it needs the `movsd` AND a fresh
		// relocation — and there is no `movsd xmm, imm64` to rebuild it with even if the IEEE bits
		// ARE an integer the compiler holds. `constValueOfDef` would panic. When someone
		// generalizes remat to "an op with no register inputs", it buys BOTH of these at once.
		andRegReg to negF64RegReg gives false
		// The arm64 band. `arm64MovImm` IS the arm64 constant materialization (the `movz`/`movk` ladder),
		// so it is rematerializable exactly as x64's `movRegImm`/`movRegImm32` are — `constDefInfo` reads
		// its immediate and `freshConstOp` re-emits a fresh `arm64MovImm`. Every other arm64 op reads a
		// register (or is control/frame/call), so it is not rematerializable, for the same reason the
		// x64 ALU band above is not.
		arm64Ret to arm64Cbnz gives false
		arm64MovImm gives true
		arm64Prologue to arm64Fcsel gives false
		// The P1.5-A1 function-value band (x64). `leaRegFunc` is the third instance of the
		// `leaRegGlobal`/`leaRegRdata`/`movsdRegRip` `false`: it reads no register and its `.text` address
		// cannot change, but it lives behind a relocation, not an integer `constValueOfDef` holds, so this
		// integer-remat predicate answers `false`. `callReg` reads its callee register and is a call —
		// not a const materialization either. The Linux `x64Syscall` is a call, likewise `false`.
		leaRegFunc to x64Syscall gives false
		// The jump tables are terminators, not constant materializations, so neither is rematerializable.
		x64JumpTable to arm64JumpTable gives false
		// The witness call is a call, not a constant materialization — not rematerializable (like `callReg`).
		callMem gives false
		// A stack-argument store defines nothing; an incoming load's def is a MEMORY read whose address is a
		// frame fact, not a constant this pass could re-materialize. Neither is rematerializable.
		arm64StoreOutgoingArg to arm64LoadIncomingArg gives false
		// Both arm64 bit reinterpretations READ A REGISTER, so neither def can be rebuilt from a constant.
		arm64FmovGprFp to arm64FmovFpGpr gives false
		// A frame address is not a CONSTANT materialization: `constDefInfo` reconstructs a def from an
		// immediate it carries, and this op carries a slot index, not the value it produces.
		leaRegSlot gives false
		// F1b: a fill ladder DEFINES NOTHING, so there is no def for this pass to rematerialize.
		x64MemFill to arm64MemFill gives false
		// F2: every atomic READS MEMORY (or, for the two thread-token reads, the OS thread's own state), so no
		// def here can be rebuilt from an immediate this pass carries. Not rematerializable.
		x64LockCmpxchg to arm64ThreadPointer gives false
		// EC16: every scaled-index form READS registers the pass cannot reconstruct — its immediates are a
		// scale and a displacement, not the value it produces. Not rematerializable.
		leaRegBaseIndexScale to arm64AddLsl gives false
		// EC18: the high multiply DEFINES NOTHING EXPLICIT — its product lands in the implicit RAX/RDX
		// pair — and it reads a register besides. `divideReg`'s answer, for both halves of the reason.
		imulHighReg gives false
		// arm64 `fneg` reads a register. Not rematerializable.
		arm64Fneg gives false
		// G3: an ordered load reads MEMORY another thread writes — the opposite of rematerializable.
		arm64LoadAcquire or arm64StoreRelease gives false
		// EC18: `SMULH` reads two registers the pass cannot reconstruct. Not rematerializable.
		arm64Smulh gives false
	end 'k'
end 'opIsConstMaterialization'

// Would splitting `valueId` at the peak actually KILL it there? Splitting rewrites the uses AFTER
// the peak to reloads and leaves the uses BEFORE it reading the original — so the value is dead
// across the peak only if no BEFORE-peak use is still reachable FROM the peak. Under a back edge
// it often is, and then the spill relieves nothing.
//
// The loop bound in `while i < n` is the case that exposed this: `n`'s only use is the header's
// compare, which sits BEFORE the loop's call in layout order — so the splitter would store `n` and
// emit NO reload, while `n` stays live across the call via the back edge. A wasted slot, a wasted
// store in the loop header every iteration, and ZERO pressure relief. (It converged only because a
// value memory already holds is never stored again, which is an accident, not the termination
// argument: the potential Φ did not decrease that iteration.)
//
// Reachability must respect SSA. A value has exactly ONE def, so if a before-peak use is reachable
// from the peak WITHOUT passing that def, the value is genuinely still live there. Passing the def
// REDEFINES it, and the use then reads the new value — which is precisely why a loop-header PHI is
// relievable while a loop-invariant is not: every back-edge path to the header re-enters the phi's
// def. So the walk below simply never expands INTO the def's block.
//
//   `n`   (def outside the loop, used in the header) → header reachable without redef → REFUSED.
//   `i`   (a header PHI, used in the header)         → header is its own def block    → allowed.
//   `sum` (a header PHI, used after the call)        → no before-peak use at all      → allowed.
function killsValueAtPeak(index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, anchor SplitAnchor, reach ReachCache) returns bool
	let useSites = index.useCount(valueId)
	if useSites == 0 'neverUsed'
		// No use at all, so none before the peak: nothing keeps it live across.
		return true
	end 'neverUsed'

	// The thread ascends by sequence, so the FIRST record decides whether any use precedes the peak.
	if index.useSeqAt(index.firstUseOf(valueId)) >= anchor.seq 'allAfter'
		// Every use follows the peak, so every one becomes a reload. The value is dead across it.
		return true
	end 'allAfter'

	let defBlock = index.defBlockOf(valueId)
	var record = index.firstUseOf(valueId)
	for _ in 0 upto useSites 'check'
		if index.useSeqAt(record) < anchor.seq and reach.reachableFromPeak(topo, peakBlock: anchor.blockLocal, defBlock: defBlock, block: index.useBlockAt(record)) 'notKilled'
			return false
		end 'notKilled'
		record = index.nextUseOf(record)
	end 'check'
	return true
end 'killsValueAtPeak'

// Memoized "blocks reachable from the peak without passing through a def block", keyed by that
// def block. The peak is fixed for one `chooseVictim` call, so the walk is a function of the def
// block alone — and a loop's candidates are overwhelmingly phis of the SAME header, so one walk
// serves them all. Rows live in ONE flat `rows × numBlocks` array (never an array of arrays).
//
// IT OUTLIVES THE SPLIT, and that is worth more than the memo within one call. A row is a pure
// function of `(peak block, def block)` over the CFG, and the CFG is INVARIANT under splitting (a
// split inserts body ops, rewrites operands and repoints edge args — it never adds a block or moves an
// edge). So a row walked for one splitter iteration is still exact for the next one, PROVIDED the peak
// block is the same — which on the shape that spills it is: N accumulators across ONE call in a loop
// peak at that same call for every one of their splits. Rebuilding the cache per split cost an O(blocks)
// allocation and fill plus a fresh BFS per def block, K times, for rows that had not changed.
//
// `retargetTo` is the one thing that invalidates them, and `reachableFromPeak` ASSERTS it was called:
// serving a row walked from a DIFFERENT peak would flip `killsValueAtPeak`, change the victim's tier,
// and silently emit a different split.
type ReachCache
	var numBlocks as CfgIndex
	// The peak block every row in `rows` was walked FROM. `hasPeak` is false until the first
	// `retargetTo`, which is the state in which no row exists to be misread.
	var hasPeak as bool
	var peakBlockLocal as CfgIndex
	// defBlock -> its row index in `rows`, or `numBlocks` for "not walked yet" (no row index can
	// equal numBlocks: there are at most numBlocks distinct def blocks, so rows are 0..numBlocks-1).
	var rowOf as ReachRowColumn
	var rows as BoolArray
	var rowCount as ReachRow
	// The BFS worklist every row's walk steps with — owned by the CACHE, exactly as
	// `LivenessMarkScratch` owns the worklist of the other CFG walk in this pass, and for the reason the
	// file header gives. `clear()` keeps the capacity, so after a function's first walk the walks
	// allocate nothing. It carries nothing BETWEEN walks: `markBlocksReachableFromPeak` clears it on
	// entry and drains it to empty, so it is storage only.
	var worklist as CfgIndexArray

	// `rowOf` is left EMPTY here and sized by the first `retargetTo` (`Array.refill` resizes as well
	// as fills). The overwhelmingly common function needs no split at all — 1202 of 1202 in the
	// call-heavy benchmark — and so never reaches `chooseVictim` and never retargets. Sizing the
	// column eagerly would put a per-function allocation, proportional to the block count, on every
	// function in the program to serve the few that spill.
	static function create(numBlocks CfgIndex) returns ReachCache
		return Self{numBlocks: numBlocks, hasPeak: false, peakBlockLocal: 0, rowOf: ReachRowColumn.create(), rows: BoolArray.create(), rowCount: 0, worklist: CfgIndexArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Aim the cache at the peak `chooseVictim` is about to ask about. Everything walked from a
	// different peak is forgotten; a repeat of the same peak block keeps every row it already has.
	export function retargetTo(peakBlock CfgIndex)
		if self.hasPeak and self.peakBlockLocal == peakBlock 'samePeakBlock'
			return
		end 'samePeakBlock'

		self.hasPeak = true
		self.peakBlockLocal = peakBlock
		self.rowCount = 0
		self.rows.clear()
		self.rowOf.refill(self.numBlocks as ElementIndex, value: self.numBlocks as ReachRow)
	end 'retargetTo'

	// Is `block` reachable from the peak's SUCCESSORS without passing through `defBlock`?
	export function reachableFromPeak(topo FuncTopology, peakBlock CfgIndex, defBlock CfgIndex, block CfgIndex) returns bool
		if (not self.hasPeak) or self.peakBlockLocal != peakBlock 'staleTarget'
			panic("ReachCache.reachableFromPeak: the cached walks start at peak block {self.peakBlockLocal} but the caller is asking about peak block {peakBlock} — `retargetTo` must run once per peak, before any query. A row walked from a different peak would flip `killsValueAtPeak`, pick a different victim, and emit a different split.")
		end 'staleTarget'

		let row = self.rowFor(topo, defBlock: defBlock)
		return try self.rows.get(self.slotOf(row, block: block)) otherwise panic("ReachCache.reachableFromPeak: block {block} out of range")
	end 'reachableFromPeak'

	// The flat index of `(row, block)` in the row-major `rows` matrix — the ONE place its layout is
	// spelled, so the read above and the walk that fills a row cannot disagree about it.
	function slotOf(row ReachRow, block CfgIndex) returns ReachSlot
		return (row as ReachSlot) * (self.numBlocks as ReachSlot) + (block as ReachSlot)
	end 'slotOf'

	function rowFor(topo FuncTopology, defBlock CfgIndex) returns ReachRow
		let cached = try self.rowOf.get(defBlock) otherwise panic("ReachCache.rowFor: def block {defBlock} out of range")
		if cached != self.numBlocks as ReachRow 'hit'
			return cached
		end 'hit'

		let row = self.rowCount
		self.rowCount = row + 1

		// `growFilled`, NEVER `resize` — this matrix is extended ONE ROW AT A TIME, which is the exact
		// shape `resize` is quadratic on: it reserves EXACTLY the length asked, so row `r` reallocates
		// and copies all `r × numBlocks` entries already there. Θ(rows² × numBlocks) BYTES for a matrix
		// that ends up Θ(rows × numBlocks) long, and rows and numBlocks BOTH grow with the function.
		//
		// The fill is the row the walk needs — it only ever SETS marks, so the row must arrive all-false —
		// and `growFilled` adds EXACTLY this row and nothing else. Proof that its fill covers `[base,
		// base + numBlocks)`: `rows.count() == rowCount * numBlocks` holds after every step (`create` and
		// `retargetTo`'s `clear()` both leave 0 == 0 * numBlocks, and `growFilled` below re-establishes
		// it), `numBlocks` never changes for a cache's life, and `markBlocksReachableFromPeak` only
		// `set`s. So on entry `existing == rowCount_old * numBlocks == row * numBlocks == base`, and
		// `growFilled`'s fill runs over `[existing, newLength) == [base, base + numBlocks)`.
		let base = self.slotOf(row, block: 0)
		self.rows.growFilled(self.slotOf(self.rowCount, block: 0) as ElementIndex, value: false)
		markBlocksReachableFromPeak(topo, peakBlockLocal: self.peakBlockLocal, defBlock: defBlock, rows: self.rows, base: base, worklist: self.worklist)
		try self.rowOf.set(defBlock, value: row) otherwise panic("ReachCache.rowFor: rowOf.set OOB")
		return row
	end 'rowFor'
end 'ReachCache'

// Mark, into `rows[base .. base+numBlocks)`, the blocks reachable from `peakBlockLocal` WITHOUT
// passing through `defBlock` — the blocks in which the value is still carrying its pre-peak
// definition. The walk starts at the peak block's SUCCESSORS (so the peak's own block is reached
// only via a genuine cycle) and never expands into `defBlock` (entering it redefines the value;
// see `killsValueAtPeak`). Worklist-driven: no CFG traversal in the allocator may recurse.
//
// `worklist` is the CACHE's, not this walk's (see `ReachCache.worklist`) — a block is pushed once per
// in-edge, so a fresh one here was an O(edges) allocation per walk.
function markBlocksReachableFromPeak(topo FuncTopology, peakBlockLocal CfgIndex, defBlock CfgIndex, rows BoolArray, base ReachSlot, worklist CfgIndexArray)
	worklist.clear()
	let deg = topo.succ.degree(peakBlockLocal as CsrIndex)
	for k in 0 upto deg 'seed'
		worklist.push(topo.succ.neighbour(peakBlockLocal as CsrIndex, k: k))
	end 'seed'

	while worklist.count() > 0 'bfs'
		let b = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("markBlocksReachableFromPeak: worklist.pop on non-empty failed")
		if b == defBlock 'redefined'
			continue
		end 'redefined'
		let seen = try rows.get(base + (b as ReachSlot)) otherwise panic("markBlocksReachableFromPeak: rows.get OOB")
		if seen 'already'
			continue
		end 'already'
		try rows.set(base + (b as ReachSlot), value: true) otherwise panic("markBlocksReachableFromPeak: rows.set OOB")

		let d = topo.succ.degree(b as CsrIndex)
		for k in 0 upto d 'eachSucc'
			worklist.push(topo.succ.neighbour(b as CsrIndex, k: k))
		end 'eachSucc'
	end 'bfs'
end 'markBlocksReachableFromPeak'

// ==========================================================================
// Materializing a spill (Belady split at the eviction point)
// ==========================================================================

// THE STORE ANCHOR IS THE VALUE'S DEF, ALWAYS — because the def is the only point that is
// guaranteed to DOMINATE every reload, and a reload reading a slot its store does not dominate is a
// garbage read.
//
// The store used to be placed after the value's LAST use BEFORE the peak, on the reasoning that "it
// precedes the peak, the reloads follow it". LAYOUT ORDER IS NOT DOMINANCE, and that reasoning is
// wrong in two ways at once:
//
//  • A before-peak use can sit in a block that does NOT dominate the reload sites — the `then` arm
//    of an `if`, whose store never runs on the `else` path, leaving the slot unwritten under the
//    reload that follows the merge.
//  • A before-peak use can be an EDGE ARG, whose "op" is the block's TERMINATOR. A store cannot be
//    spliced after a terminator, and `positionInBlock` (body ops only) panicked outright. That is
//    the crash `register-spill.forced-spill-with-edge-arg-before-the-peak` pins.
//
// The def has neither problem: by SSA (Rule 1) it dominates every use of the value, hence every
// reload, and it is an op (or a phi's block entry) that a store can legally follow. It is also
// STRICTLY BETTER code: a value defined outside a loop and used inside it before the loop's peak
// would have anchored its store at that in-loop use and re-executed it every iteration; anchored at
// the def it runs ONCE. And it is what `storeFitsPool` already measures — that gate reads
// `defLiveAfter[v]`, the pressure at the DEF point, which is now exactly the point the store lands.
//
// Nothing about the SPLIT changes: only the after-peak uses are rewritten to reloads, so the value
// is still dead across the peak and the pressure there still drops by one. The store merely writes
// memory; it never kills the value, so anchoring it earlier costs no register and no pressure.

// Spill `valueId` by SPLITTING its live range AT the peak (`peak.peakSeq`), so it is DEAD across
// the peak — freeing its register there, which is what lowers peak pressure. The store goes at the
// value's DEF (after the defining op, or at the top of its block if it is a phi — see the note
// above on why the def is the ONLY sound anchor); a reload (a fresh value id) goes before the first
// AFTER-peak use in each use-block, and ONLY the after-peak uses in that block are rewritten to
// it — the before-peak uses keep the original value in its original register. The store DOMINATES
// every reload (the def dominates every use, by SSA) and each reload dominates the after-peak uses
// it serves, so no phi is ever needed (the dominating-reload invariant). Returns the next free
// value id.
//
// The peak's placement is the amended Rule 2: `cold` asserts every inserted op lands outside every
// loop; `forced` permits the store/reload bracket INSIDE a loop, which is the only way to relieve a
// fixed-register point whose live-across set exceeds the registers the op leaves alone.
//
// EDGE ARGS are rewritten alongside op uses. A branch-edge arg is a real use, read at the end of
// its block; leaving one pointing at the ORIGINAL would keep that original live to the block's end
// — hence still live across the peak — so the spill would not lower the pressure and the driver
// would re-pick the same value forever. Loop-carried accumulators are exactly this shape.
//
// Nothing below MOVES an op: the insertions are logged and spliced in afterwards (see `SplitEdits`), so
// every position this reads — including each use record's own, which is half of its sequence number —
// is stated in the block's pre-split layout throughout.
function spillOneValue(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, cfg FuncCfg, liveness LivenessResult, valueId ValueId, anchor SplitAnchor, scope SplitScope, slot SpillSlot, pool RegMask, nextValueId ValueId, counters RegAllocCounters, lineage SplitLineage, scratch SplitScratch) returns ValueId
	let peakSeq = anchor.seq
	let placement = anchor.placement
	let useSites = index.useCount(valueId)
	let keepsOriginal = keepsOriginalBeforePeak(scope)
	var edits = scratch.edits
	var buf = scratch.splitOperands

	// WHERE THE RUNS MUST BREAK, decided BEFORE a single op moves — see `markRunStarts`. It reads the
	// block's op list at the PRE-SPLIT positions the use records carry, so it must run before anything
	// is recorded; what it produces is a flag per USE ORDINAL, which no insertion can invalidate.
	markRunStarts(module, index: index, topo: cfg.topo, valueId: valueId, class: liveness.classOf(valueId), pool: pool, file: liveness.registerFile, runStarts: scratch.runStarts, buf: buf, opFacts: liveness.opFacts)

	// STORE: at the def, which dominates every reload below — and ONLY when memory does not already
	// hold this value. It does when an earlier split stored it, AND when this id IS a reload (its
	// defining `loadRegSlot` read it out of a slot that still holds it — see `UseIndex.slotResident`);
	// storing either one again would copy a stack slot into a second stack slot.
	if not index.isSlotResident(valueId) 'notAlreadyInMemory'
		insertStore(module, cfg: cfg, index: index, valueId: valueId, slot: slot, placement: placement, edits: edits)
	end 'notAlreadyInMemory'

	// RELOAD: one per RUN of the uses this split rewrites — a maximal group in ONE block, on ONE side of
	// the peak, with no CONFINING op between consecutive members. Use sites are in layout order, so a
	// block's uses are contiguous and its before-peak ones precede its after-peak ones; each boundary
	// starts a fresh reload id, which dominates the run it serves (it precedes the run's first use, and
	// a block's edge arg is read after every op in it).
	//
	// An `afterPeakUses` split skips the before-peak runs entirely: those uses keep the ORIGINAL value
	// in its original register, for free. An `everyUse` split rewrites them too, which is what leaves
	// the original with no reader but its own store — see `SplitScope`.
	var freshId = nextValueId
	var reloadCount = 0 as EventCount
	if useSites > 0 'hasUses'
		// A cursor over the value's use thread, and `j` counting how far along it we are — the thread is
		// circular, so the count is what ends the walk (see `UseIndex.firstUseOf`).
		var record = index.firstUseOf(valueId)
		var j = 0 as UseOrdinal
		while j < useSites 'eachUse'
			let afterPeak = index.useSeqAt(record) >= peakSeq
			if (not afterPeak) and keepsOriginal 'beforePeakUseKeepsTheOriginal'
				j = j + 1
				record = index.nextUseOf(record)
				continue
			end 'beforePeakUseKeepsTheOriginal'

			// A SPILL STORE MUST NEVER ANCHOR A RELOAD — it is the one use of the value that a reload
			// cannot be placed before. `storeSlotReg slot, valueId` READS the value, so `buildUseIndex`
			// records it as an ordinary use; but it is also the ONLY op that ever WRITES that slot, and
			// `insertReloadFor` anchors at the use's own position, which puts the `loadRegSlot` AHEAD of
			// it. The reload then reads the slot before anything has written it — and because
			// `rewriteOpAt` deliberately declines to repoint the store (it must keep reading the
			// ORIGINAL), the reload serves nobody but the REST of the run, every member of which is
			// repointed at that garbage.
			//
			// ⚠ IT TAKES A VALUE SPILLED TWICE to get here at all, which is why it hid: the store only
			// exists from the FIRST split, and only a SECOND split of that same value walks over it. The
			// measured case was an `everyUse` split (tier 4), and that is the common route — a store is
			// anchored at the value's def, so it usually precedes a later peak and the before-peak skip
			// above already covers it. But that is a TENDENCY, NOT AN INVARIANT: a def sitting
			// immediately before the peak puts its store at or after `peakSeq`, and then an
			// `afterPeakUses` split reaches it too. So this guard is unconditional rather than scoped to
			// `everyUse`.
			//
			// It asks `OpVariantFacts.slotRoleOf`, which is the SAME question `rewriteOpAt` asks from the
			// same memo. Written as its own predicate here, the placer and the rewriter cannot drift into
			// disagreeing about which ops are spill stores — the drift that WAS this bug: the rewriter
			// knew, and the placer did not.
			if useIsSpillStore(module, index: index, record: record, opFacts: liveness.opFacts) 'theStoreWritesTheSlotAReloadWouldRead'
				j = j + 1
				record = index.nextUseOf(record)
				continue
			end 'theStoreWritesTheSlotAReloadWouldRead'

			let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
			insertReloadFor(module, cfg: cfg, index: index, useRecord: record, valueId: valueId, freshId: freshId, slot: slot, placement: placement, edits: edits)
			// The reload id derives from the value it reloads — record it so a reload id in an
			// E5001 blocking set chases back to that value's source origin (see SplitLineage).
			lineage.record(freshId, sourceId: valueId)

			let runStart = j
			while j < useSites and useContinuesRun(scratch.runStarts, useOrdinal: j, runStart: runStart) and useIsInRun(index, record: record, blockLocal: blockLocal, afterPeak: afterPeak, peakSeq: peakSeq) 'sameRun'
				match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
					op then rewriteOpAt(module, opIdx: index.useOpAt(record), from: valueId, to: freshId, buf: buf, opFacts: liveness.opFacts)
					edge then rewriteEdgeArgsIn(module, topo: cfg.topo, blockLocal: blockLocal, from: valueId, to: freshId)
				end 'kind'
				j = j + 1
				record = index.nextUseOf(record)
			end 'sameRun'

			freshId = freshId + 1
			reloadCount = reloadCount + 1
		end 'eachUse'
	end 'hasUses'

	counters.valuesSplit = counters.valuesSplit + 1
	counters.reloadsInserted = counters.reloadsInserted + reloadCount
	match placement 'countForced'
		forced then counters.forcedSpills = counters.forcedSpills + 1
		cold then break 'countForced'
	end 'countForced'
	return freshId
end 'spillOneValue'

// ==========================================================================
// A RELOAD MUST NOT BE BORN WITH NOWHERE TO LIVE — where a reload run has to break
// ==========================================================================
//
// A reload serves a RUN of uses, so it is live from its own site to the last use of that run. If an
// op inside that span denies the reload's register file EVERY register, the fresh reload inherits
// that clobber into `forbidden` the moment liveness is re-derived — its allowed set is EMPTY, it
// cannot be coloured anywhere, and the driver is obliged to find it at the next peak and split it
// again. The split that was supposed to relieve the value has minted its own next victim.
//
// ⚠ THAT IS A CASCADE, AND IT IS QUADRATIC. Each re-split's dirty region is the fresh value's live
// range, which still reaches the end of the run — so N such ops cost N splits of Θ(function) each
// while the emitted code grows only linearly. MEASURED on a straight-line function holding two f64
// locals read after each of N calls (Wave 1 makes every XMM caller-saved, so a float live across a
// call is forbidden its ENTIRE file): `regalloc:splitting` bytes grew x3.84 and x3.98 per doubling
// at N=128/256/512 — 19.4 MB, 74.6 MB, 296.6 MB — against x2.02/x1.95 for the same program with the
// two locals declared `int`. `valuesSplit` was 2 per call site, and every one of them re-split the
// PREVIOUS reload, storing it to a FRESH slot each time (3 calls: six slots and six stores for two
// values).
//
// The REMAT path never had this problem: it re-emits the constant before EVERY use, so its fresh ids
// live one op each. The spill path grouped uses into per-block runs, and that asymmetry IS the
// cascade. Breaking the run removes it at the root: one split of the ORIGINAL value now mints every
// reload the value needs, none of them able to become a victim, so `valuesSplit` is 2 whatever N is.
// Same reload count, ONE store instead of N, ONE slot instead of N — and 39% less emitted code.
//
// ⚠⚠ THE TEST IS "LEAVES THIS FILE NO REGISTER AT ALL", **NOT** "clobbers something this value could
// have used", AND THE DIFFERENCE IS BOTH CORRECTNESS AND CODE SIZE.
//
//  • A reload that spans a call and keeps SOME register is FINE. An int live across a call is denied
//    the 9 caller-saved registers and takes one of the 5 callee-saved; it is coloured, it is never
//    re-picked, and there is no cascade to break. Breaking there would emit a reload per call for
//    every spilled int in the program and buy nothing — MEASURED at 112 moved goldens, almost all of
//    them a reload that merely moved from a callee-saved register to a caller-saved one.
//  • ⚠ AND IT USED TO MISCOMPILE. Call arguments are plain physical pre-moves (`mov rcx, a; mov rdx,
//    b; call`) with no parallel-copy sequencer, and the allocator kept them apart ONLY by forbidding a
//    pre-move's register to every value live ACROSS it. A value DEFINED BETWEEN two pre-moves is live
//    across neither, so it was forbidden nothing and could be coloured into an argument register an
//    EARLIER pre-move had already loaded. Breaking a GPR run at a pre-move puts a `loadRegSlot`
//    exactly there. MEASURED: all nine `streaming-subprocess` tests returned the wrong answer (a
//    `ReadFile` handed a clobbered argument reads 0 bytes and reports EOF), with the golden gate
//    silent because the runtime's own IR is not pinned.
//
//    ⛔ **THAT HOLE IS NOW CLOSED WHERE IT ACTUALLY WAS, AND THIS TEST IS NO LONGER WHAT STOPS IT.**
//    `LivenessResult.establishedAtDef` forbids a value the argument registers established ahead of
//    its def, wherever the def sits, so a `loadRegSlot` spliced between two pre-moves is safe by
//    construction rather than by never being spliced there. This bullet is kept because the COST
//    argument above is untouched and still decides the test — 112 moved goldens for reloads that only
//    changed register — and because a reader who removes it should know which of the two reasons went
//    away. ⚠ It is NOT a second guard on one invariant: this test decides where a RUN BREAKS; the
//    forbid decides what a DEF may take. Only the forbid is load-bearing for correctness.
//
// So the rule is the one the defect actually states: a value whose allowed set is EMPTY *must* be
// split again, so a split must never create one. A value that keeps a register might never be
// touched again, so leave it alone. On x64 that makes this fire for XMM values at a call and NOWHERE
// else — no op clobbers all 14 GPRs — which is why every non-float program's codegen is untouched.
//
// Fill `runStarts[k]` — must use ordinal `k` of `valueId` start a FRESH reload? A use in a different
// block, or on the other side of the peak, already starts one (`useIsInRun`); this adds the third
// reason.
//
// It scans ONLY the OPEN INTERVAL between each pair of consecutive uses, because that is the only
// stretch a run boundary can depend on — so the whole pre-pass costs Θ(the span from a value's first
// use to its last, per block), never Θ(the block).
//
// ⚠ BOTH ENDPOINTS ARE READ OUT OF THE USE RECORDS (`usePositionIn`), NOT SEARCHED FOR. Deriving a
// use's position by scanning the block for its op index is the SAME FACT `bodyPositionOfUse` reads
// straight out of the index, derived a second way — and a second derivation of a position is exactly
// what this pass was rewritten to remove. Two of them cannot disagree loudly: one would pick a run
// boundary from one notion of where a use is while the reload was spliced in at another.
//
// ⚠ THE STRETCH BEFORE THE FIRST USE IN A BLOCK IS NOT SCANNED, AND MUST NOT BE. That use already
// starts a run (`useIsInRun` breaks at a block boundary), so a clobber ahead of it decides nothing.
// Reaching it by search meant walking from position 0 and THROWING THE ANSWER AWAY: Θ(block) per
// split, Θ(splits × block) over a function — on a straight-line function with M distinct f64 locals
// all read after one call, ~M × 2M `opStrandsClass` calls whose results were all discarded. It
// allocates nothing, so `scale-test` cannot see it and neither can the memory column of
// `--metrics`; it was found by reading, and removing it left both of those columns unmoved.
function markRunStarts(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, class RegClass, pool RegMask, file RegisterFile, runStarts BoolArray, buf OperandList, opFacts OpVariantFacts)
	let useSites = index.useCount(valueId)
	runStarts.refill(useSites as ElementIndex, value: false)
	if useSites == 0 'neverUsed'
		return
	end 'neverUsed'

	let classPool = poolOfClass(pool, class: class, file: file)
	var record = index.firstUseOf(valueId)
	// `numBlocks` is "no block walked yet" — no block index can equal it (the same sentinel
	// `ReachCache` and `PeakTree` use). It is what makes the first iteration take the `newBlock` arm,
	// so the block below is re-read before anything looks at it.
	var walkedBlock = topo.numBlocks
	var block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: 0)
	var previousPos = 0 as BlockOpPos
	var havePrevious = false

	for k in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
		let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
		if blockLocal != walkedBlock 'newBlock'
			walkedBlock = blockLocal
			block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: blockLocal)
			havePrevious = false
		end 'newBlock'

		let pos = usePositionIn(index, block: block, record: record)
		// TWO records can name the SAME op, hence the same position — a value read by a block's
		// terminator (an `x64JumpTable` index) and also passed on one of that block's edges yields an op
		// use and an edge use at the same place. Nothing lies between them.
		if havePrevious and pos == previousPos 'sameOp'
			record = index.nextUseOf(record)
			continue
		end 'sameOp'

		if havePrevious 'hasAPredecessorInThisBlock'
			// Only the OPEN interval is scanned: the endpoints are ops this value is an OPERAND of, and
			// an operand's own op forbids it only through `forbidOperandsFromImplicit`, which cannot
			// empty a file (it is what keeps an `idiv` divisor out of RAX/RDX, not out of every
			// register). Every position strictly inside the interval is a BODY op — a record can name
			// the block's TERMINATOR, but only as the right-hand endpoint, since nothing follows it.
			let firstBetween = previousPos + 1
			for scan in firstBetween upto pos 'between'
				let scanned = try block.opRefs.get(scan) otherwise panic("markRunStarts: opRefs.get OOB at {scan} of block {blockLocal}, scanning between uses {k - 1} and {k} of value {valueId}")
				if opStrandsClass(module, opIdx: scanned, classPool: classPool, buf: buf, opFacts: opFacts) 'noRegisterLeft'
					try runStarts.set(k, value: true) otherwise panic("markRunStarts: runStarts.set OOB at use {k}")
					break
				end 'noRegisterLeft'
			end 'between'
		end 'hasAPredecessorInThisBlock'

		previousPos = pos
		havePrevious = true
		record = index.nextUseOf(record)
	end 'eachUse'
end 'markRunStarts'

// Would a value of this register file, live ACROSS `opIdx`, be left with NO register at all — is
// EVERY register of `classPool` inside the op's clobber? Read off the SAME mask the liveness sweep
// folds into `forbidden` (`opClobberMask`), so the splitter's question and the sweep's answer cannot
// drift: written twice, this would decide a run boundary from one notion of "clobber" while the
// sweep forbade registers by another, and a reload the splitter believed was free would come back
// as the next victim for ever.
function opStrandsClass(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex, classPool RegMask, buf OperandList, opFacts OpVariantFacts) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("opStrandsClass: opIdx not in module.ops")
	return (classPool and not opClobberMask(op, buf: buf, opFacts: opFacts)) == 0
end 'opStrandsClass'

// Does the use at ordinal `useOrdinal` CONTINUE the run that opened at `runStart`? The run's first use
// always does — the reload was placed for exactly that use, so the break flag that OPENED the run must
// not also close it — and any later one does unless `markRunStarts` found an op between it and its
// predecessor that would leave the reload no register.
function useContinuesRun(runStarts BoolArray, useOrdinal UseOrdinal, runStart UseOrdinal) returns bool
	if useOrdinal == runStart 'theUseTheReloadWasPlacedFor'
		return true
	end 'theUseTheReloadWasPlacedFor'
	return not (try runStarts.get(useOrdinal) otherwise panic("useContinuesRun: runStarts.get OOB at use {useOrdinal}"))
end 'useContinuesRun'

// Is `record` in the run one reload serves — the same block, on the same side of the peak? A run may
// NOT straddle the peak: a reload placed before a block's before-peak use would then serve its
// after-peak uses too, and that reloaded value would span the peak, so the pressure there would never
// drop. (That is the whole-range-reload failure the file header names, in the small.)
//
// The THIRD reason a run breaks — a confining op between two uses — is decided ahead of the mutation
// by `markRunStarts` and tested beside this, because it is a question about the block's
// OPS rather than about the use record.
function useIsInRun(index UseIndex, record UseRecordIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex, afterPeak bool, peakSeq OpSeq) returns bool
	if index.useBlockAt(record) != blockLocal 'otherBlock'
		return false
	end 'otherBlock'
	return (index.useSeqAt(record) >= peakSeq) == afterPeak
end 'useIsInRun'

// The slot a spill of `valueId` writes into, or reloads out of. A value is stored ONCE, at its def:
// the def dominates every reload wherever a later peak puts one, so relieving the value AGAIN needs
// only more loads out of the slot it already has — and so does relieving a RELOAD of it, which is
// resident in that same slot without ever having been stored (see `UseIndex.slotResident`).
// `nextSlot` — a fresh slot — is for a value memory does not hold at all.
function slotForSpillOf(index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, nextSlot SpillSlot) returns SpillSlot
	return match index.spillSlotOf(valueId) 'existing'
		none gives nextSlot
		slot(s) gives s
	end 'existing'
end 'slotForSpillOf'

// Insert the spill STORE at the value's def — the one point that dominates every reload (see above).
function insertStore(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, index UseIndex, valueId ValueId, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement, edits SplitEdits)
	match index.defKindOf(valueId) 'kind'
		opDef then insertStoreAfter(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId, afterOpIdx: index.requireDefOp(valueId), blockLocal: index.defBlockOf(valueId), slot: slot, placement: placement, edits: edits)
		phiDef then insertStoreAtBlockEntry(module, cfg: cfg, valueId: valueId, blockLocal: index.defBlockOf(valueId), slot: slot, placement: placement, edits: edits)
		none then panic("insertStore: value {valueId} has neither an op def nor a phi block, so it has no store anchor — `hasStoreAnchor` should have rejected it as a victim")
	end 'kind'
end 'insertStore'

// Insert `loadRegSlot(freshId, slot)` — the RELOAD serving a block's run of uses, anchored on the FIRST
// of them (use entry `useRecord`). The reload dominates every use in the run (it precedes the first one,
// and a block's edge arg is read after all its ops), and the caller rewrites those uses.
function insertReloadFor(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, index UseIndex, useRecord UseRecordIndex, valueId ValueId, freshId ValueId, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement, edits SplitEdits)
	let blockLocal = index.useBlockAt(useRecord)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: SplitPlacementOp.reload, placement: placement)
	var block = blockAt(module, topo: cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	// An OP use takes the reload immediately before it. An EDGE use means the block has no op use in this
	// run at all (op uses precede the edge use in the index), so the value is only forwarded to a phi and
	// the reload goes at the END of the block's body — after every op, ahead of the branch, and so ahead
	// of every branch edge that reads it.
	let at = match index.useKindAt(useRecord) 'k'
		op gives opUseReloadPosition(index, block: block, record: useRecord)
		edge gives beforeBranchPosition(block)
	end 'k'

	spliceOp(module, at: at, op: TargetOp.loadRegSlot(TargetVReg.virtual(freshId), slot: slot), blockLocal: blockLocal, edits: edits)
end 'insertReloadFor'

// Repoint every branch-edge arg of `blockLocal` naming `from` at `to` (the reload that now holds
// the value). The reload precedes the block's terminator, so it dominates the edge.
//
// THE ONLY IN-PLACE WRITER OF `argIds` IN THE COMPILER — which makes it the only place that must
// COPY BEFORE IT WRITES. The tiers SHARE one phi model: an edge's `argIds` reaches back through
// the Std module to the Maxon module the parse memo hands out, and `queryAllModule` returns that
// SAME module object on every cache hit. A write straight through this array would corrupt the
// memo for every later compile of the project — silently, since nothing downstream re-reads the
// Std or Maxon phi model to notice.
//
// So de-alias first: the FIRST arg an edge actually repoints swaps in a private copy, and the
// writes land there. An edge with nothing to repoint keeps sharing and copies nothing, so a
// program that never spills pays no copy at any tier. `.clone()` is exactly right here and is the
// compiler's ONE remaining clone: independent mutation is the sole thing Maxon's reference
// semantics make a clone necessary FOR.
//
// Index-based over `branchEdges` because a `for edge in ...` binding is immutable, so it could
// not host the `edge.argIds = ...` field assignment the copy needs.
function rewriteEdgeArgsIn(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, blockLocal CfgIndex, from ValueId, to ValueId)
	let block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: blockLocal)
	for e in 0 upto block.branchEdges.count() 'eachEdge'
		var edge = try block.branchEdges.get(e) otherwise panic("rewriteEdgeArgsIn: branchEdges.get OOB")
		var privatized = false
		for k in 0 upto edge.argIds.count() 'eachArg'
			let argId = try edge.argIds.get(k) otherwise panic("rewriteEdgeArgsIn: argIds.get OOB")
			if argId == from 'match'
				if not privatized 'stillShared'
					edge.argIds = edge.argIds.clone()
					privatized = true
				end 'stillShared'
				try edge.argIds.set(k, value: to) otherwise panic("rewriteEdgeArgsIn: argIds.set OOB")
			end 'match'
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachEdge'
end 'rewriteEdgeArgsIn'

// Insert `storeSlotReg(slot, valueId)` right after `afterOpIdx` — the op that DEFINES the value, so
// it is live there by construction and the store reads it from its own register.
function insertStoreAfter(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, afterOpIdx OpIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement, edits SplitEdits)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: SplitPlacementOp.store, placement: placement)
	var block = blockAt(module, topo: cfg.topo, local: blockLocal)
	let anchorPos = positionInBlock(block, opIdx: afterOpIdx)
	spliceOp(module, at: anchorPos + 1, op: TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId)), blockLocal: blockLocal, edits: edits)
end 'insertStoreAfter'

// Insert `storeSlotReg(slot, valueId)` at the very TOP of `blockLocal` — the PHI anchor. A
// block-arg has no defining op; it arrives in its register via the edge copies SSA destruction
// places at the predecessors' ends, so it is live at block entry and a store there reads it
// correctly. Its block dominates every use of it, so this store dominates every reload.
function insertStoreAtBlockEntry(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, valueId ValueId, blockLocal CfgIndex, slot SpillSlot, placement SpillPlacement, edits SplitEdits)
	assertPlacementLegal(cfg, blockLocal: blockLocal, valueId: valueId, what: SplitPlacementOp.store, placement: placement)
	spliceOp(module, at: 0, op: TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: TargetVReg.virtual(valueId)), blockLocal: blockLocal, edits: edits)
end 'insertStoreAtBlockEntry'

// The position a use sits at in `collectBlockOps` order — read straight out of the use record that
// names it, because a sequence number IS `block × SeqStride + position` (`UseIndex.usePosAt`) and
// nothing has moved yet (`SplitEdits`). Searching the block for the anchor op instead was O(block) at
// EVERY insertion, which is the Θ(insertions × block) a split used to pay.
//
// ⚠ AND IT PROVES THE IDENTITY IT RESTS ON, FOR ONE COMPARE. Two separate rules keep a sequence
// number equal to `block × SeqStride + position`: `spliceOpRefs` MOVES an op by the number of splices
// landing at or before it, and `SplitEdits.splicesAtOrBefore` PREDICTS that number for the record's
// sequence repair. They live in different files and nothing derives one from the other, so they are
// exactly the pair this project's own bugs are made of — and a drift between them is silent: every
// anchor here would then name a position one or two ops away from the op the record actually reads, a
// reload would be spliced in ahead of the wrong instruction, and the answer would simply be wrong. So
// the op at the position IS compared against the op the record names, on every read, always on. It is
// O(1) against a walk that is already Θ(uses).
function usePositionIn(index UseIndex, block IrBlock, record UseRecordIndex) returns BlockOpPos
	let pos = index.usePosAt(record)
	let bodyOps = block.opRefs.count()
	if pos > bodyOps 'pastTheTerminator'
		panic("usePositionIn: use record {record} is anchored at position {pos} of block '{String.from(block.label)}', which holds {bodyOps} body op(s) and one terminator — no record can sit past the terminator")
	end 'pastTheTerminator'

	// The TERMINATOR is numbered one past the body, so `opRefs` cannot answer for it; a record there is
	// an edge use (or a use the terminator itself reads), and `useOpAt` already returns that op.
	if pos < bodyOps 'aBodyOp'
		let at = try block.opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("usePositionIn: opRefs.get OOB at {pos}")
		if at != index.useOpAt(record) 'positionDisagreesWithTheOp'
			panic("usePositionIn: use record {record} of block '{String.from(block.label)}' claims position {pos}, but that position holds op {at} while the record reads through op {index.useOpAt(record)} — a use's sequence number has drifted from its block's layout, so the two halves of the splice-shift rule (`spliceOpRefs` and `SplitEdits.splicesAtOrBefore`) no longer agree")
		end 'positionDisagreesWithTheOp'
	end 'aBodyOp'

	return pos
end 'usePositionIn'

// ⭐⭐ **WHERE A RELOAD FOR AN *OP* USE GOES — AND THE TERMINATOR IS AN OP LIKE ANY OTHER.**
// `usePositionIn`'s own header says so (*"a record there is an edge use, OR A USE THE TERMINATOR ITSELF
// READS"*), and a `ret v` / `errorReturn v` is exactly that: the value's only reader in its block is the
// block's last instruction. Such a use is anchored one past the body, where `opRefs` has no slot — so the
// reload goes where an EDGE use's does, at the end of the body and ahead of a conditional branch if the
// block has one. That position dominates the terminator, which is the whole requirement.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT WAS A PANIC, AND SHV2'S OWN SOURCE IS WHERE IT FIRED** — *"use record 122 is anchored at
// position 4 of block 'tryok', whose body holds 4 op(s) — that position is the block's TERMINATOR, and a
// spill op cannot be spliced after one"*. The message was right that a spill may not go AFTER a terminator
// and wrong that the position implied one: `beforeBranchPosition` is the answer to where it goes instead,
// and it already existed one arm over. `bodyPositionOfUse` keeps its refusal for the caller that genuinely
// needs a body op (`rematerializeConstant` re-emits a constant BEFORE the op that reads it, and a
// terminator has no "before" inside the body).
function opUseReloadPosition(index UseIndex, block IrBlock, record UseRecordIndex) returns BlockOpPos
	let pos = usePositionIn(index, block: block, record: record)
	if pos >= block.opRefs.count() 'theTerminatorItselfReadsIt'
		return beforeBranchPosition(block)
	end 'theTerminatorItselfReadsIt'

	return pos
end 'opUseReloadPosition'

// The `opRefs` position of an OP use, which must name a BODY op. The one position a record can carry
// that `opRefs` does not hold is the block's TERMINATOR — `collectBlockOps` numbers it one past the
// body, and every EDGE use is anchored there — so reaching here with one is a caller that should have
// gone to `beforeBranchPosition`. That is the same bug the search used to report when it ran off the
// end of the body.
function bodyPositionOfUse(index UseIndex, block IrBlock, record UseRecordIndex) returns BlockOpPos
	let pos = usePositionIn(index, block: block, record: record)
	if pos >= block.opRefs.count() 'notABodyOp'
		panic("bodyPositionOfUse: use record {record} is anchored at position {pos} of block '{String.from(block.label)}', whose body holds {block.opRefs.count()} op(s) — that position is the block's TERMINATOR, and a spill op cannot be spliced after one")
	end 'notABodyOp'

	return pos
end 'bodyPositionOfUse'

// The `opRefs` position at which an op runs after the whole body but on EVERY edge out of the
// block: past the last real body op, but before the conditional branch when the block has one.
//
// "Ahead of the branch" is NOT the end of `opRefs` when the block branches two ways: a `jcc` is a BODY
// op paired with a `jmp` terminator, so an op past the end of the body sits BETWEEN them and runs only
// on the not-taken path — the then-edge would then read an unloaded register. A reload placed here goes
// immediately BEFORE the conditional branch, which dominates both outgoing edges. A `loadRegSlot` is a
// plain `mov r, [rsp+disp]` and writes no flags, so it is safe between the compare and the `jcc` that
// reads that compare's EFLAGS.
function beforeBranchPosition(block IrBlock) returns BlockOpPos
	if block.hasCondBranchOp() 'twoWay'
		return positionInBlock(block, opIdx: block.condBranchIndex())
	end 'twoWay'
	return block.opRefs.count()
end 'beforeBranchPosition'

// Append `op` to the module's op table and return its index — the one place a splitter-minted op
// enters `module.ops`, so the insertion sites above cannot drift on how they mint. APPEND-ONLY is
// load-bearing: it is why every op index the `UseIndex` snapshot holds stays valid while a split
// is being materialized.
function appendOp(module TargetModule, op TargetOp) returns OpIndex
	let idx = module.ops.count()
	module.ops.push(op)
	// A spill or a reload is the ALLOCATOR's op, not the author's: a line row at it would stop a debugger
	// on a statement whose code it is not.
	module.spans.pushNone(idx)
	return idx
end 'appendOp'

// Mint an op and LOG it for splicing into `blockLocal` at forward position `at`. Every op the splitter
// inserts goes through here; none of them may reach `insertOpRef` or `spliceOpRefs` directly.
//
// The log is both the insertion itself and what the incremental update replays to repair the sequence
// numbers of that block's use records, and to register the block as dirty (see `SplitEdits`). An
// UNLOGGED insert would leave every later use in the block carrying the sequence number of the op before
// it: Belady would rank on stale next-use distances, and the before/after-the-peak partition — which is
// stated in sequence numbers — would cut in the wrong place. Both are silent.
//
// `at` is a PRE-SPLIT position, and it stays valid because nothing is spliced until the split is done.
function spliceOp(module TargetModule, at BlockOpPos, op TargetOp, blockLocal CfgIndex, edits SplitEdits)
	edits.recordInsert(blockLocal, pos: at, opIdx: appendOp(module, op: op))
end 'spliceOp'

// ==========================================================================
// Materializing a remat (re-emit the constant before each after-peak use)
// ==========================================================================

// Rematerialize the constant `valueId` AT the peak (`peakSeq`): re-emit its `movRegImm(32)` (a
// fresh value id) right before EACH use this split rewrites, rewriting that use to the fresh copy. A
// constant is trivially rematerializable, so per-use re-emit is fine — it holds no register across
// the gap and never touches memory, so unlike a spill it needs no depth-0 restriction on its uses
// (re-emitting a constant even inside a loop is free). Returns the next free value id.
//
// The SCOPE says which uses are rewritten (see `SplitScope`). An `afterPeakUses` remat leaves the
// before-peak uses reading the ORIGINAL, so the original stays live from its def to the last of them
// — dead across the peak, which is what `killsValueAtPeak` established before choosing it. An
// `everyUse` remat rewrites all of them, which is the ONLY thing that kills a constant kept alive
// across the peak by a before-peak use AROUND A BACK EDGE. Either way the original def is dropped
// exactly when nothing reads it any more.
function rematerializeConstant(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, topo FuncTopology, valueId ValueId, peakSeq OpSeq, scope SplitScope, nextValueId ValueId, counters RegAllocCounters, lineage SplitLineage, scratch SplitScratch, opFacts OpVariantFacts) returns ValueId
	let defOpIdx = index.requireDefOp(valueId)
	// The immediate AND which ISA's const op the original def is, read ONCE: the re-emit at each use
	// must be the SAME KIND (x64 `mov`, arm64 `movz`/`movk`) so it is byte-identical to what ISel wrote.
	let (constValue, isArm64Const) = constDefInfo(module, opIdx: defOpIdx)
	let keepsOriginal = keepsOriginalBeforePeak(scope)
	var edits = scratch.edits

	var freshId = nextValueId
	var haveUnrewrittenUse = false
	var buf = scratch.splitOperands
	let useSites = index.useCount(valueId)
	if useSites > 0 'hasUses'
		var record = index.firstUseOf(valueId)
		for _ in 0 upto useSites 'eachUse'
			if index.useSeqAt(record) < peakSeq and keepsOriginal 'beforePeak'
				haveUnrewrittenUse = true
				record = index.nextUseOf(record)
				continue
			end 'beforePeak'

			// Re-emitting before an EDGE use would be silently wrong, and the wrongness compounds: an edge
			// use's op is the block's TERMINATOR (its sequence anchor), which names no virtual register,
			// so `rewriteOpAt` would be a no-op — the edge's `argIds` would go on naming the ORIGINAL,
			// while the loop counted the use as rewritten and `dropOriginalDef` below deleted the def that
			// arg still points at. It cannot happen, because `isRematerializable` refuses an edge-passed
			// value outright (there is no op-use site to re-emit before). That gate lives in
			// `chooseVictim`'s tiers, three functions away, so the invariant it guarantees is asserted
			// HERE, where it is relied on.
			match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
				op then break 'kind'
				edge then panic("rematerializeConstant: value {valueId} has a branch-edge use, but a rematerializable value must not be edge-passed — `isRematerializable` refuses one, because a constant re-emitted before a terminator would leave the edge arg naming the original and the original's def would then be dropped from under it")
			end 'kind'

			let useOpIdx = index.useOpAt(record)
			let useBlockLocal = index.useBlockAt(record)
			var block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: useBlockLocal)
			let usePos = bodyPositionOfUse(index, block: block, record: record)
			// The SAME op builder the ISel used, so the re-emitted constant is byte-identical to
			// the one it replaces — x64's `constMaterializationOp` (not a second copy of the 32-vs-64-bit
			// size choice that can drift from it), or arm64's uniform `arm64MovImm` — selected by the
			// original def's own kind (`freshConstOp`).
			spliceOp(module, at: usePos, op: freshConstOp(isArm64Const, freshId: freshId, value: constValue), blockLocal: useBlockLocal, edits: edits)
			rewriteOpAt(module, opIdx: useOpIdx, from: valueId, to: freshId, buf: buf, opFacts: opFacts)
			// The re-emitted copy derives from the original constant — record it so a
			// rematerialized id in an E5001 blocking set chases back to the constant's source
			// span (the `let`/literal the author wrote), not a Rule-3 panic (see SplitLineage).
			lineage.record(freshId, sourceId: valueId)
			freshId = freshId + 1
			record = index.nextUseOf(record)
		end 'eachUse'
	end 'hasUses'

	if not haveUnrewrittenUse 'dropDef'
		dropOriginalDef(module, topo: topo, defOpIdx: defOpIdx, blockLocal: index.defBlockOf(valueId), edits: edits)
	end 'dropDef'

	// A remat that re-emitted NOTHING changed nothing: the potential Φ did not decrease, so the next
	// iteration finds the same peak and re-picks the same value — the driver spins until the runaway
	// bound panics thousands of splits later, naming a symptom instead of the cause. Both scopes make
	// this unreachable — `afterPeakUses` because `isRematVictim`'s `killsValueAtPeak` gate guarantees
	// an after-peak use (a value live at the peak that no before-peak use keeps alive there MUST have
	// one), `everyUse` because a live value has at least one use — so assert it here and a regression
	// in either gate fails LOUDLY at the split that caused it.
	if freshId == nextValueId 'relievedNothing'
		panic("rematerializeConstant: value {valueId} was chosen at the peak (sequence {peakSeq}) but this split rewrote NO use of it, so nothing was re-emitted and the peak's pressure is unchanged — the next iteration would re-pick it forever. An `afterPeakUses` remat must not be chosen for a value whose only uses precede the peak (it is live there only around a back edge — that is what `isRematVictim`'s killsValueAtPeak gate refuses, and what the `everyUse` scope is for), and an `everyUse` remat must not be chosen for a value with no uses at all (it would not be live here).")
	end 'relievedNothing'

	counters.rematerialized = counters.rematerialized + 1
	return freshId
end 'rematerializeConstant'

// The immediate a constant-materialization def carries, and whether it is the arm64 form. Both come
// from ONE read of the def, so the re-emit selects the right ISA's const op and its value cannot drift
// from its kind: x64 `movRegImm`/`movRegImm32` (isArm64 = false), arm64 `arm64MovImm` (true).
function constDefInfo(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex) returns (MachineWord, bool)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("constDefInfo: opIdx not in module.ops")
	return match op 'k'
		movRegImm(_, value) gives (value, false)
		movRegImm32(_, value) gives (value, false)
		arm64MovImm(_, value) gives (value, true)
		prologue to jcc panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		movRegReg to negF64RegReg panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		arm64Ret to arm64Cbnz panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		arm64Prologue to arm64Fcsel panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// `leaRegFunc`/`callReg` (P1.5-A1) and the Linux `x64Syscall` are not const materializations —
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for all, so none reaches here; named rather than swallowed.
		leaRegFunc to x64Syscall panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// The jump tables are not const materializations (`opIsConstMaterialization` gives false), so
		// `constDefInfo` is never called on one — named rather than swallowed by a range arm.
		x64JumpTable to arm64JumpTable panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		callMem panic("constDefInfo: a `callMem` def is not a constant materialization")
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for both, so neither reaches here; named rather than swallowed.
		arm64StoreOutgoingArg to arm64LoadIncomingArg panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for both, so neither reaches here; named rather than swallowed.
		arm64FmovGprFp to arm64FmovFpGpr panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		leaRegSlot panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for both, so neither reaches here; named rather than swallowed.
		x64MemFill to arm64MemFill panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for every atomic, so none reaches here; named rather than swallowed.
		x64LockCmpxchg to arm64ThreadPointer panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false for every scaled-index form, so none reaches here; named
		// rather than swallowed.
		leaRegBaseIndexScale to arm64AddLsl panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		// `opIsConstMaterialization` answers false, so this never reaches here; named rather than swallowed.
		imulHighReg panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		arm64Fneg panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		arm64LoadAcquire or arm64StoreRelease panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
		arm64Smulh panic("constDefInfo: def is not a constant materialization")
	end 'k'
end 'constDefInfo'

// The fresh constant-materialization op to splice at a spilled constant's use — the arm64 form when
// its def was arm64, else the x64 form (whose builder reselects the 32-vs-64-bit encoding exactly as
// ISel did). `freshId` is the split-minted value the use is rewritten to.
function freshConstOp(isArm64 bool, freshId ValueId, value MachineWord) returns TargetOp
	let dest = TargetVReg.virtual(freshId)
	return TargetOp.arm64MovImm(dest, value: value) if isArm64 else constMaterializationOp(dest, value: value)
end 'freshConstOp'

// Log the removal of the original constant def's op ref from its block (it is now dead — every use was
// rewritten to a rematerialized copy). Applied with the split's splices, after them, exactly where it
// landed when it was removed by hand (see `SplitEdits`). The op stays in `module.ops` but unreferenced
// (harmless, exactly like the reuse-copy/edge-move ops SsaDestruction appends).
function dropOriginalDef(module TargetModule, topo FuncTopology, defOpIdx OpIndex, blockLocal CfgIndex, edits SplitEdits)
	var block = blockAt(module, topo: topo, local: blockLocal)
	edits.recordRemoval(blockLocal, pos: positionInBlock(block, opIdx: defOpIdx), opIdx: defOpIdx)
end 'dropOriginalDef'

// ==========================================================================
// Shared op / block plumbing
// ==========================================================================

// Rewrite one op's uses of `from` to `to` (a reloaded / rematerialized copy). The one op that
// reads `from` but must NOT be rewritten is a spill STORE: it deliberately reads the ORIGINAL
// value to write it to memory, and is emitted BEFORE the reload — rewriting it to `to` would
// make it read a value defined later (a use-before-def). Since a `storeSlotReg`'s source is
// ALWAYS an original spilled value (never a reload), skipping every spill store is exactly right
// and touches no genuine use. A no-op when the op does not use `from` at all.
function rewriteOpAt(module TargetModule, opIdx OpIndex, from ValueId, to ValueId, buf OperandList, opFacts OpVariantFacts)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteOpAt: opIdx not in module.ops")
	if opFacts.slotRoleOf(op) == OpSlotRole.spillStore 'readsTheOriginalDeliberately'
		return
	end 'readsTheOriginalDeliberately'
	if not opUsesValue(op, valueId: from, buf: buf) 'noUse'
		return
	end 'noUse'
	try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: substituteValueInOp(op, from: from, to: to)) otherwise panic("rewriteOpAt: ops.set OOB")
end 'rewriteOpAt'

// Is this use record the SPILL STORE of the value it names? A `storeSlotReg slot, v` reads `v`, so it
// is a use record like every other — and it is the one use a reload must never be anchored on, for
// exactly the reason `rewriteOpAt` refuses to repoint it: the store is the only WRITER of the slot,
// and a reload anchored at its position is spliced ahead of it, reading the slot unwritten.
//
// An EDGE use is anchored on its block's TERMINATOR, which is never a spill store (the splitter
// inserts stores and reloads as BODY ops), so it answers false without a lookup.
function useIsSpillStore(module TargetModule, index UseIndex, record UseRecordIndex, opFacts OpVariantFacts) returns bool
	match index.useKindAt(record) 'kind'
		op then break 'kind'
		edge then return false
	end 'kind'

	let opIdx = index.useOpAt(record)
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("useIsSpillStore: use record {record} reads through op {opIdx}, which is not in module.ops")
	return opFacts.slotRoleOf(op) == OpSlotRole.spillStore
end 'useIsSpillStore'

function opUsesValue(op TargetOp, valueId ValueId, buf OperandList) returns bool
	targetOpOperands(op, buf: buf)
	for o in buf 'eachOperand'
		if (not operandIsDef(o)) and operandIsVirtual(o) and operandValueId(o) == valueId 'use'
			return true
		end 'use'
	end 'eachOperand'
	return false
end 'opUsesValue'

// Replace virtual `from` with virtual `to` in a vreg operand; a physical reg or any other
// virtual passes through.
function substituteVReg(vreg TargetVReg, from ValueId, to ValueId) returns TargetVReg
	return match vreg 'v'
		physical gives vreg
		virtual(id) gives TargetVReg.virtual(to) if id == from else vreg
	end 'v'
end 'substituteVReg'

// Rebuild an op with every `virtual(from)` operand replaced by `virtual(to)`. EXHAUSTIVE over
// every TargetOp — the same one-place-per-op discipline as `colorTargetOp`, so a new op is a
// compile error here rather than a silently un-rewritten operand. Ops with no register operand
// (frame / control / call) pass through.
function substituteValueInOp(op TargetOp, from ValueId, to ValueId) returns TargetOp
	match op 'rewrite'
		prologue to jcc then return op
		movRegImm(dest, value) then return TargetOp.movRegImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		movRegImm32(dest, value) then return TargetOp.movRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		movRegReg(destReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.movRegReg(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		movReg32Reg32(destReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.movReg32Reg32(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		subRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.subRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		imulRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.imulRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		negReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.negReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		cmpRegReg(lhsReg, rhsReg) then return TargetOp.cmpRegReg(substituteVReg(lhsReg, from: from, to: to), rhsReg: substituteVReg(rhsReg, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegRegReg(dest, base, index) then return TargetOp.leaRegRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegRegImm32(dest, base, disp) then return TargetOp.leaRegRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), disp: disp)
		imulRegRegImm32(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.imulRegRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		cmpRegImm32(reg, imm) then return TargetOp.cmpRegImm32(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		xchgRegReg to iatCall then return op
		cqo then return op
		divideReg(divisor, signedness) then return TargetOp.divideReg(substituteVReg(divisor, from: from, to: to), signedness: signedness)
		pushReg to popReg then return op
		storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg) then return TargetOp.storeSlotReg(slot, srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		loadRegSlot(destReg, slot) then return TargetOp.loadRegSlot(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), slot: slot)
		andRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.andRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		orRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.orRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		xorRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.xorRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		notReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.notReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		andRegImm32(dest, lhs, imm) then return TargetOp.andRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		orRegImm32(dest, lhs, imm) then return TargetOp.orRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		xorRegImm32(dest, lhs, imm) then return TargetOp.xorRegImm32(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		shlRegImm8(dest, lhs, imm) then return TargetOp.shlRegImm8(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		sarRegImm8(dest, lhs, imm) then return TargetOp.sarRegImm8(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		shrRegImm8(dest, lhs, imm) then return TargetOp.shrRegImm8(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		shlRegCl(dest, lhs) then return TargetOp.shlRegCl(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to))
		sarRegCl(dest, lhs) then return TargetOp.sarRegCl(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to))
		shrRegCl(dest, lhs) then return TargetOp.shrRegCl(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to))
		setccReg(dest, cond) then return TargetOp.setccReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), cond: cond)
		// The data-section band — every vreg operand substituted, mirroring `colorTargetOp`.
		leaRegGlobal(dest, label) then return TargetOp.leaRegGlobal(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), label: label)
		loadRegBaseDisp(dest, base, disp, width) then return TargetOp.loadRegBaseDisp(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), disp: disp, width: width)
		storeBaseDispReg(base, disp, src, width) then return TargetOp.storeBaseDispReg(substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), disp: disp, src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), width: width)
		// The SSE band. Substitution is class-blind for the same reason coloring is: a fresh id
		// minted by a split INHERITS its victim's class, so replacing `from` with `to` can never
		// change which file the value lives in.
		addsdRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.addsdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		subsdRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.subsdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		mulsdRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.mulsdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		divsdRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.divsdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		ucomisdRegReg(lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.ucomisdRegReg(substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		movsdRegRip(dest, label) then return TargetOp.movsdRegRip(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), label: label)
		cvtsi2sdRegReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.cvtsi2sdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		cvttsd2siRegReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.cvttsd2siRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		// The MATH INTRINSICS (P1.0d.4-C) — two XMM operands each; the rounding MODE is not a register
		// and passes through, exactly as an immediate or a cond code does.
		sqrtsdRegReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.sqrtsdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		roundsdRegReg(dest, src, mode) then return TargetOp.roundsdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), mode: mode)
		// The BIT REINTERPRETATIONS — one def, one use each, both substitutable, like the converts. Two
		// arms and not a range: they are not adjacent (the inbound one was appended at the end of the x64
		// SSE band, past `minMaxSdRegReg`) and each rebuilds its OWN variant.
		movqGprXmm(dest, src) then return TargetOp.movqGprXmm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		movqXmmGpr(dest, src) then return TargetOp.movqXmmGpr(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		// f64 NEGATE — one def, one use, both substitutable, like `absF64RegReg` below.
		negF64RegReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.negF64RegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		// f64 ABS — one def, one use, both substitutable, like the converts. Its two-address REUSE is
		// the operand model's business, not this rename's.
		absF64RegReg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.absF64RegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		// The `.rdata` address `lea` (P1.2 wave A) — substitutes its GPR dest, exactly like `leaRegGlobal`.
		leaRegRdata(dest, label) then return TargetOp.leaRegRdata(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), label: label)
		// `minsd`/`maxsd` substitute all three XMM operands, keeping `lhs` and `rhs` in their own slots:
		// neither op is commutative on a NaN, so a rewrite that treated the two sources as
		// interchangeable would change the program's answer. `kind` is carried through untouched — it
		// names no register.
		minMaxSdRegReg(dest, lhs, rhs, kind) then return TargetOp.minMaxSdRegReg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to), kind: kind)
		// --- arm64 SCALAR-CORE band ---
		//
		// The SAME `substituteVReg`-per-operand rewrite the x64 arms use — neutral over `TargetVReg`, so
		// each arm64 op is rebuilt exactly like its x64 twin. Substitution is class-blind for the same
		// reason coloring is: a fresh id minted by a split INHERITS its victim's class. Non-register
		// fields (block ids, immediates, names, cond codes) pass through; register-free control/frame/call
		// ops pass through whole.
		arm64Ret then return op
		arm64Branch then return op
		arm64CondBranch then return op
		arm64Cbz(reg, targetBlockId) then return TargetOp.arm64Cbz(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to), targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64Cbnz(reg, targetBlockId) then return TargetOp.arm64Cbnz(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to), targetBlockId: targetBlockId)
		arm64MovImm(dest, value) then return TargetOp.arm64MovImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), value: value)
		arm64Prologue then return op
		arm64Epilogue then return op
		arm64Add(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Add(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Sub(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Sub(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Mul(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Mul(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Sdiv(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Sdiv(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Udiv(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Udiv(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Msub(dest, mulLhs, mulRhs, minuend) then return TargetOp.arm64Msub(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), mulLhs: substituteVReg(mulLhs, from: from, to: to), mulRhs: substituteVReg(mulRhs, from: from, to: to), minuend: substituteVReg(minuend, from: from, to: to))
		arm64And(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64And(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Orr(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Orr(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Eor(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Eor(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Lslv(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Lslv(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Asrv(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Asrv(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Lsrv(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Lsrv(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Neg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Neg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Mvn(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Mvn(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64AddImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64AddImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64SubImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64SubImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64AndImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64AndImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64OrrImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64OrrImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64EorImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64EorImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64LslImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64LslImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64AsrImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64AsrImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64LsrImm(dest, src, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64LsrImm(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64Cmp(lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Cmp(substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64CmpImm(reg, imm) then return TargetOp.arm64CmpImm(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to), imm: imm)
		arm64Cset(dest, cond) then return TargetOp.arm64Cset(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), cond: cond)
		arm64LeaFuncAddr(dest, funcName) then return TargetOp.arm64LeaFuncAddr(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), funcName: funcName)
		arm64LeaRdata(dest, label) then return TargetOp.arm64LeaRdata(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), label: label)
		arm64LeaGlobal(dest, label) then return TargetOp.arm64LeaGlobal(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), label: label)
		arm64BranchLink then return op
		arm64BranchLinkReg(reg) then return TargetOp.arm64BranchLinkReg(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fadd(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Fadd(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fsub(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Fsub(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fmul(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Fmul(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fdiv(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Fdiv(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fcmp(lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Fcmp(substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Scvtf(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Scvtf(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fcvtzs(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Fcvtzs(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fsqrt(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Fsqrt(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fabs(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Fabs(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Frint(dest, src, mode) then return TargetOp.arm64Frint(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), mode: mode)
		arm64FpLoadRdata(dest, label) then return TargetOp.arm64FpLoadRdata(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), label: label)
		// No register operand to substitute — both OS-call forms take their args pre-moved into x0..
		// (like `arm64BranchLink` and `iatCall`).
		arm64ImportCall to arm64Syscall then return op
		// `fcsel` substitutes its three FP operands and carries its condition through. ⚠ `ifTrue` and
		// `ifFalse` keep their own slots — exchanging them turns `min` into `max`.
		arm64Fcsel(dest, ifTrue, ifFalse, cond) then return TargetOp.arm64Fcsel(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), ifTrue: substituteVReg(ifTrue, from: from, to: to), ifFalse: substituteVReg(ifFalse, from: from, to: to), cond: cond)
		// P1.5-A1 (x64): `leaRegFunc` has one def to rewrite (like `leaRegRdata`); `callReg` has one use,
		// the callee register (unlike `callDirect`/`iatCall`, which have no explicit operand and `return op`).
		leaRegFunc(dest, funcName) then return TargetOp.leaRegFunc(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), funcName: funcName)
		callReg(reg) then return TargetOp.callReg(substituteVReg(reg, from: from, to: to))
		// The Linux `syscall` carries only its number — no register operand to substitute, like `iatCall`.
		x64Syscall then return op
		// The jump tables substitute their ONE register operand — the biased index — leaving the block-id
		// table, default and rdata label untouched.
		x64JumpTable(indexReg, defaultTarget, caseTargets, tableLabel) then return TargetOp.x64JumpTable(substituteVReg(indexReg, from: from, to: to), defaultTarget: defaultTarget, caseTargets: caseTargets, tableLabel: tableLabel)
		arm64JumpTable(indexReg, defaultTarget, caseTargets, tableLabel) then return TargetOp.arm64JumpTable(substituteVReg(indexReg, from: from, to: to), defaultTarget: defaultTarget, caseTargets: caseTargets, tableLabel: tableLabel)
		// The witness call substitutes its witness-table base register, exactly as `callReg` does its callee.
		callMem(baseReg, disp) then return TargetOp.callMem(substituteVReg(baseReg, from: from, to: to), disp: disp)
		// The stack-argument pair substitutes its ONE register operand each; the slot is a frame index.
		arm64StoreOutgoingArg(slot, srcReg) then return TargetOp.arm64StoreOutgoingArg(slot, srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		arm64LoadIncomingArg(dest, slot) then return TargetOp.arm64LoadIncomingArg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), slot: slot)
		// The arm64 BIT REINTERPRETATIONS — one def, one use each, both substitutable, like `fcvtzs`/`scvtf`.
		arm64FmovGprFp(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64FmovGprFp(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64FmovFpGpr(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64FmovFpGpr(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		leaRegSlot(destReg, slot) then return TargetOp.leaRegSlot(substituteVReg(destReg, from: from, to: to), slot: slot)
		// F1b: a fill ladder carries no virtual register, so there is nothing to substitute — returned verbatim.
		x64MemFill to arm64MemFill then return op
		// F2: every virtual operand of every atomic substitutes; the RMW's opcode is not a register and rides
		// through, exactly as a jump table's block-id table does.
		x64LockCmpxchg(addrReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.x64LockCmpxchg(substituteVReg(addrReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		x64LockXadd(dest, src, addrReg) then return TargetOp.x64LockXadd(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), addrReg: substituteVReg(addrReg, from: from, to: to))
		x64LockAdd(addrReg, srcReg) then return TargetOp.x64LockAdd(substituteVReg(addrReg, from: from, to: to), srcReg: substituteVReg(srcReg, from: from, to: to))
		x64TebSelfPointer(dest) then return TargetOp.x64TebSelfPointer(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to))
		x64GsLoadRegBase(dest, base) then return TargetOp.x64GsLoadRegBase(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to))
		arm64AtomicCas(dest, addrReg, expectedReg, replacementReg) then return TargetOp.arm64AtomicCas(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), addrReg: substituteVReg(addrReg, from: from, to: to), expectedReg: substituteVReg(expectedReg, from: from, to: to), replacementReg: substituteVReg(replacementReg, from: from, to: to))
		arm64AtomicRmw(dest, addrReg, operandReg, opcode) then return TargetOp.arm64AtomicRmw(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), addrReg: substituteVReg(addrReg, from: from, to: to), operandReg: substituteVReg(operandReg, from: from, to: to), opcode: opcode)
		arm64ThreadPointer(dest, source) then return TargetOp.arm64ThreadPointer(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), source: source)
		// EC16 — the scaled-index band. Register operands substitute; the scale/disp/width immediates ride through.
		leaRegBaseIndexScale(dest, base, index, scale) then return TargetOp.leaRegBaseIndexScale(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to), scale: scale)
		loadRegBaseIndexScale(dest, base, index, scale, disp, width) then return TargetOp.loadRegBaseIndexScale(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to), scale: scale, disp: disp, width: width)
		storeBaseIndexScaleReg(base, index, scale, disp, src, width) then return TargetOp.storeBaseIndexScaleReg(substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to), scale: scale, disp: disp, src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), width: width)
		arm64AddLsl(dest, base, index, scale) then return TargetOp.arm64AddLsl(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), index: substituteVReg(index, from: from, to: to), scale: scale)
		// EC18 — one register operand to substitute, exactly as `divideReg` has.
		imulHighReg(src) then return TargetOp.imulHighReg(substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64Fneg(dest, src) then return TargetOp.arm64Fneg(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to))
		arm64LoadAcquire(dest, base, width) then return TargetOp.arm64LoadAcquire(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), base: substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), width: width)
		arm64StoreRelease(base, src, width) then return TargetOp.arm64StoreRelease(substituteVReg(base, from: from, to: to), src: substituteVReg(src, from: from, to: to), width: width)
		arm64Smulh(dest, lhs, rhs) then return TargetOp.arm64Smulh(substituteVReg(dest, from: from, to: to), lhs: substituteVReg(lhs, from: from, to: to), rhs: substituteVReg(rhs, from: from, to: to))
	end 'rewrite'
end 'substituteValueInOp'

// The position of `opIdx` within `block.opRefs` (body ops only — spill/reload/remat are always body
// ops, never terminators), by SEARCH.
//
// It is O(block), so it is only for the anchors the `UseIndex` cannot answer for: a DEF site, which
// carries no use record, and a block's conditional branch. Both are asked at most once per split. A
// reload's anchor is a USE, and a use record already knows its own position — see `bodyPositionOfUse`,
// which is what a per-insertion search here used to cost.
function positionInBlock(block IrBlock, opIdx OpIndex) returns BlockOpPos
	for pos in 0 upto block.opRefs.count() 'each'
		if (try block.opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("positionInBlock: opRefs.get OOB")) == opIdx 'match'
			return pos
		end 'match'
	end 'each'
	panic("positionInBlock: op {opIdx} not in block '{String.from(block.label)}' opRefs (a terminator or wrong block?)")
end 'positionInBlock'

// ==========================================================================
// Rule 2 verification (the hot-overflow E5001 diagnostic lives in
// RegisterPressureDiagnostic.maxon, thrown from the `chooseVictim` -> `none` arm above)
// ==========================================================================

// Verify an inserted spill op is where its placement mode allows.
//
// A COLD spill must land outside every loop: it relieves a FULL-pool overflow by moving a value
// that is IDLE across the pressured region, so its store runs once before the loop and its reload
// once after — zero instructions in the loop body. A cold op landing at depth ≥ 1 would be a
// silent hot spill, so it panics.
//
// A FORCED spill has NO depth restriction, and that is the amended Rule 2. It relieves a
// fixed-register point (a call, an `idiv`) whose live-across set exceeds the registers the op
// leaves alone. The value CANNOT stay in a register there — memory is the only home — so the
// store/reload bracket is a forced placement, not a chosen one, and the allocator searches
// nothing. It is admitted inside a loop because the pair is cheap against the op it brackets: one
// store and one load against a call that costs far more. Refusing it would be a FALSE E5001 —
// the program fits the machine, and the "restructuring" it would demand (hoist the values into an
// array) emits the same store and load at EVERY use, i.e. strictly worse code.
// Which half of a split is being placed. An enum rather than a "store"/"reload" String
// literal: the argument is evaluated eagerly, so the literal allocated a heap String on
// EVERY placement — and the splitter places once per split, on the hottest path in the
// compiler — to name the operation in a panic that fires only on a splitter bug.
enum SplitPlacementOp
	store
	reload
end 'SplitPlacementOp'

function assertPlacementLegal(cfg FuncCfg, blockLocal CfgIndex, valueId ValueId, what SplitPlacementOp, placement SpillPlacement)
	match placement 'p'
		forced then return
		cold then break 'p'
	end 'p'

	let depth = try cfg.loopDepth.get(blockLocal) otherwise panic("assertPlacementLegal: loopDepth.get OOB")
	if depth != 0 'inLoop'
		panic("assertPlacementLegal: a COLD {what.name} for value {valueId} would land in block local {blockLocal} at loop depth {depth} — a cold spill relieves a FULL-pool overflow and must lie outside every loop. (A fixed-register point's FORCED spill is the placement that IS allowed in a loop; it goes through SpillPlacement.forced.)")
	end 'inLoop'
end 'assertPlacementLegal'

// ==========================================================================
// VERIFY MODE — the incremental update, checked against a full rebuild
// ==========================================================================
//
// Everything below runs ONLY when `VerifyIncrementalSplit` is true. It exists because a wrong
// invalidation set does not fail a test: it drops ONE clobber bit from ONE value's forbidden mask, that
// value takes a caller-saved register across a call, and the program miscompiles silently. So the check
// is EXHAUSTIVE — every live set, every mask, every dead-phi count, every block demand, every def-point
// pressure, the whole use index, and the chosen peak, at EVERY split. A spot check would find that bit
// only by luck.

// Rebuild liveness and pressure from scratch and compare them with what the incremental update
// produced. Panics on the first divergence, naming the function, the split, and the fact that moved.
function verifyIncremental(module TargetModule, cfg FuncCfg, func IrFunction, liveness LivenessResult, peak PressurePeak, scratch SplitScratch, pool RegMask, iterations EventCount)
	let name = String.from(func.name)
	// The CURRENT class column, not the lowering's original: it has grown by the ids each split minted,
	// and a fresh id's class is inherited, never re-derived (`growValueSpace`). A rebuild from the
	// original column would be short by exactly those ids and trip `assertClassColumnCovers` — so this
	// is the one place the verifier must read the incremental state rather than reconstruct it. It is
	// sound because a class cannot drift: nothing after lowering ever WRITES an existing entry.
	let fresh = computeLivenessOn(module, cfg: cfg, func: func, valueClass: liveness.valueClass, file: liveness.registerFile, opFacts: liveness.opFacts)
	var freshScratch = SplitScratch.create(cfg.topo.numBlocks)
	// `intoTree: false` — the WALK, which is the independent derivation the index is checked against.
	let freshPeak = analyzePressure(module, liveness: fresh, pool: pool, scratch: freshScratch, intoTree: false)

	if liveness.valueCount != fresh.valueCount 'valueCount'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the value space is {liveness.valueCount} but a full rebuild says {fresh.valueCount}")
	end 'valueCount'
	if liveness.wordsPerRow != fresh.wordsPerRow 'wordsPerRow'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): wordsPerRow is {liveness.wordsPerRow} but a full rebuild says {fresh.wordsPerRow}")
	end 'wordsPerRow'

	verifyUseIndex(liveness.useIndex, fresh: fresh.useIndex, name: name, iterations: iterations)

	for b in 0 upto cfg.topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		verifyLiveSet(liveness.live.liveIn(b), fresh: fresh.live.liveIn(b), onEntry: true, blockLocal: b, name: name, iterations: iterations)
		verifyLiveSet(liveness.live.liveOut(b), fresh: fresh.live.liveOut(b), onEntry: false, blockLocal: b, name: name, iterations: iterations)

		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			if liveness.deadPhiCountOf(b, class: class) != fresh.deadPhiCountOf(b, class: class) 'deadPhis'
				panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): block {b} has {liveness.deadPhiCountOf(b, class: class)} dead {class.name} phis but a full rebuild says {fresh.deadPhiCountOf(b, class: class)}")
			end 'deadPhis'
		end 'eachClass'

		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			if liveness.blockPressureOf(b, class: class) != fresh.blockPressureOf(b, class: class) 'blockPressure'
				panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): block {b} demands {liveness.blockPressureOf(b, class: class)} {class.name} registers but a full rebuild says {fresh.blockPressureOf(b, class: class)}")
			end 'blockPressure'
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'eachBlock'

	for v in 0 upto liveness.valueCount 'eachValue'
		if liveness.forbiddenOf(v) != fresh.forbiddenOf(v) 'forbidden'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} forbids {liveness.forbiddenOf(v)} but a full rebuild says {fresh.forbiddenOf(v)} — a LOST clobber bit is a caller-saved register held across a call, i.e. a silent miscompile")
		end 'forbidden'

		// The POSITIONAL INDEX's answer against the WALK's column — the two derivations of one number, one
		// maintained per split and one swept from scratch. This is what catches a repair that withdrew the
		// wrong contribution: a def-point pressure that is one too low turns a value the store cannot be
		// anchored for into an eligible victim, and the split that follows relieves nothing.
		let after = defPointPressure(liveness, pressure: scratch.pressure, valueId: v)
		let freshAfter = try freshScratch.defLiveAfter.get(v) otherwise panic("verifyIncremental: defLiveAfter.get OOB for value {v}")
		if after != (freshAfter as PressureDelta) 'defLiveAfter'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} has def-point pressure {after} in the positional index but a full sweep says {freshAfter}")
		end 'defLiveAfter'
	end 'eachValue'

	verifyPressureIndex(module, liveness: liveness, fresh: fresh, scratch: scratch, freshScratch: freshScratch, name: name, iterations: iterations)
	verifyPeak(peak, fresh: freshPeak, wordsPerRow: liveness.wordsPerRow, name: name, iterations: iterations)
end 'verifyIncremental'

// The positional index, LEAF BY LEAF, against one built from a full rebuild of liveness.
//
// It is the check the incremental repair actually needs, and neither the peak comparison nor the
// def-point one subsumes it: those read the index through a max and a suffix sum, either of which can
// come out right while an individual op's number is wrong — and the wrong one only surfaces splits
// later, at a peak that has drifted one op. Compared by POSITION rather than by slot, because the two
// indexes were laid out at different times and a slot is a coordinate, not a fact.
function verifyPressureIndex(module TargetModule, liveness LivenessResult, fresh LivenessResult, scratch SplitScratch, freshScratch SplitScratch, name String, iterations EventCount)
	let topo = liveness.cfg.topo
	var rebuilt = freshScratch.pressure
	rebuilt.rebuild(module, liveness: fresh, sweep: freshScratch.sweep, seedBuf: freshScratch.seedBuf, scratch: freshScratch.relayout)

	var held = scratch.pressure
	for b in 0 upto topo.numBlocks 'eachBlock'
		let ops = rebuilt.opCountOf(b)
		if held.opCountOf(b) != ops 'opCount'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the positional index holds {held.opCountOf(b)} op(s) for block {b} but a full rebuild lays out {ops}")
		end 'opCount'

		for classOrdinal in 0 upto RegClassCount 'eachClass'
			let class = regClassAt(classOrdinal)
			if held.blockLiveOut(b, class: class) != rebuilt.blockLiveOut(b, class: class) 'liveOut'
				panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): block {b}'s {class.name} live-out leaf reads {held.blockLiveOut(b, class: class)} but a full rebuild says {rebuilt.blockLiveOut(b, class: class)}")
			end 'liveOut'
			// The AGGREGATE, not just the leaves under it: a combine that folds the wrong way answers with
			// every leaf right, and it is the aggregate the peak-finder actually reads. Checked against a
			// SCAN of the same index's own leaves first, so a divergence says which half moved.
			let scanned = held.blockPeakByScan(b, class: class)
			let aggregated = held.blockPeak(b, class: class)
			if aggregated.pressure != scanned.pressure or aggregated.slot != scanned.slot 'aggregateVsScan'
				panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): block {b}'s {class.name} peak aggregate says {aggregated.pressure} at slot {aggregated.slot} but a scan of the same leaves says {scanned.pressure} at slot {scanned.slot} — the tree's combine has drifted from the suffix sum it summarises")
			end 'aggregateVsScan'
			if aggregated.pressure != rebuilt.blockPeak(b, class: class).pressure 'blockPeak'
				panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): block {b}'s {class.name} peak reads {aggregated.pressure} but a full rebuild says {rebuilt.blockPeak(b, class: class).pressure}")
			end 'blockPeak'
			if held.blockLiveIn(b, class: class) != rebuilt.blockLiveIn(b, class: class) 'blockLiveIn'
				panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): block {b}'s {class.name} live-in population reads {held.blockLiveIn(b, class: class)} but a full rebuild says {rebuilt.blockLiveIn(b, class: class)}")
			end 'blockLiveIn'

			for pos in 0 upto ops 'eachOp'
				let heldSlot = held.slotOfPosition(b, pos: pos)
				let freshSlot = rebuilt.slotOfPosition(b, pos: pos)
				if held.opAtSlot(heldSlot) != rebuilt.opAtSlot(freshSlot) 'opOrder'
					panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): position {pos} of block {b} holds op {held.opAtSlot(heldSlot)} in the positional index but op {rebuilt.opAtSlot(freshSlot)} in a full rebuild")
				end 'opOrder'
				if held.deltaAt(heldSlot, class: class) != rebuilt.deltaAt(freshSlot, class: class) 'delta'
					panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): op at position {pos} of block {b} contributes {held.deltaAt(heldSlot, class: class)} to the {class.name} population in the positional index but {rebuilt.deltaAt(freshSlot, class: class)} in a full rebuild")
				end 'delta'
				if held.transientAt(heldSlot, class: class) != rebuilt.transientAt(freshSlot, class: class) 'transient'
					panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): op at position {pos} of block {b} carries a {class.name} reuse transient of {held.transientAt(heldSlot, class: class)} in the positional index but {rebuilt.transientAt(freshSlot, class: class)} in a full rebuild")
				end 'transient'
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachClass'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'verifyPressureIndex'

// One block's live-in (`onEntry`) or live-out set, compared as a SET: the incremental edits add and
// remove values in place, so the ORDER of a block's list legitimately differs from a fresh build's.
// Membership and cardinality are what any consumer reads.
function verifyLiveSet(actual ValueIdArray, fresh ValueIdArray, onEntry bool, blockLocal CfgIndex, name String, iterations EventCount)
	let what = "live-in to" if onEntry else "live-out of"

	if actual.count() != fresh.count() 'cardinality'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): {actual.count()} values are {what} block {blockLocal} but a full rebuild says {fresh.count()} — a duplicate in a live list inflates that block's register demand")
	end 'cardinality'

	for v in fresh 'eachExpected'
		if not liveListHolds(actual, v: v) 'missing'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} is NOT {what} block {blockLocal}, but a full rebuild says it is")
		end 'missing'
	end 'eachExpected'

	for v in actual 'eachHeld'
		if not liveListHolds(fresh, v: v) 'stale'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} is {what} block {blockLocal}, but a full rebuild says it is not")
		end 'stale'
	end 'eachHeld'
end 'verifyLiveSet'

// The whole use index, value by value: the op count, the sequence limit, and for every value its use
// thread (record for record, in order — both are held ascending by sequence, so they must agree
// element-wise, not merely as sets), its def site, and every per-value flag a victim gate reads.
function verifyUseIndex(index UseIndex, fresh UseIndex, name String, iterations EventCount)
	if index.opCount != fresh.opCount 'opCount'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the use index counts {index.opCount} ops but a full rebuild counts {fresh.opCount}")
	end 'opCount'
	if index.seqLimit != fresh.seqLimit 'seqLimit'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the sequence limit is {index.seqLimit} but a full rebuild says {fresh.seqLimit}")
	end 'seqLimit'
	if index.valueCount != fresh.valueCount 'valueCount'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the use index spans {index.valueCount} values but a full rebuild spans {fresh.valueCount}")
	end 'valueCount'

	for v in 0 upto fresh.valueCount 'eachValue'
		let sites = fresh.useCount(v)
		if index.useCount(v) != sites 'useCount'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} has {index.useCount(v)} use sites but a full rebuild finds {sites}")
		end 'useCount'

		if sites > 0 'walkTheThreads'
			var actual = index.firstUseOf(v)
			var expected = fresh.firstUseOf(v)
			for k in 0 upto sites 'eachUse'
				if index.useOpAt(actual) != fresh.useOpAt(expected) 'useOp'
					panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): use {k} of value {v} reads through op {index.useOpAt(actual)} but a full rebuild says op {fresh.useOpAt(expected)}")
				end 'useOp'
				if index.useSeqAt(actual) != fresh.useSeqAt(expected) 'useSeq'
					panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): use {k} of value {v} sits at sequence {index.useSeqAt(actual)} but a full rebuild says {fresh.useSeqAt(expected)} — a stale sequence number moves the before/after-the-peak partition")
				end 'useSeq'
				if useIsOnEdge(index, record: actual) != useIsOnEdge(fresh, record: expected) 'useKind'
					panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): use {k} of value {v} disagrees with a full rebuild about whether it is an EDGE use")
				end 'useKind'
				actual = index.nextUseOf(actual)
				expected = fresh.nextUseOf(expected)
			end 'eachUse'
		end 'walkTheThreads'

		if not sameDefSite(index, fresh: fresh, v: v) 'defSite'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v}'s def site disagrees with a full rebuild's")
		end 'defSite'
		if index.isPhiValue(v) != fresh.isPhiValue(v) 'isPhi'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} disagrees with a full rebuild about being a phi")
		end 'isPhi'
		if index.isEdgePassed(v) != fresh.isEdgePassed(v) 'edgePassed'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} disagrees with a full rebuild about being edge-passed — `isRematerializable` reads it")
		end 'edgePassed'
		if index.hasSpillStore(v) != fresh.hasSpillStore(v) 'spillStored'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} disagrees with a full rebuild about already having a spill store")
		end 'spillStored'
		if index.isSlotResident(v) != fresh.isSlotResident(v) 'slotResident'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} disagrees with a full rebuild about memory already holding it — a spill would emit a second store into a second slot, or skip a store the reloads need")
		end 'slotResident'
		if not sameSpillSlot(index.spillSlotOf(v), b: fresh.spillSlotOf(v)) 'spillSlot'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v}'s spill slot disagrees with a full rebuild's — a later relief would reload out of the wrong slot")
		end 'spillSlot'
		if index.defAndUsesAtDepthZero(v) != fresh.defAndUsesAtDepthZero(v) 'touchesLoop'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): value {v} disagrees with a full rebuild about touching a loop — `isColdSpillable` reads it")
		end 'touchesLoop'
	end 'eachValue'
end 'verifyUseIndex'

function useIsOnEdge(index UseIndex, record UseRecordIndex) returns bool
	return match index.useKindAt(record) 'k'
		op gives false
		edge gives true
	end 'k'
end 'useIsOnEdge'

// The KIND, and — behind it — every part of the def site that kind has: an op def's own op and block,
// a phi's block. ONE function rather than the per-arm family it replaces, because the payload is now
// read through accessors that are only legal once the tag has been asked.
function sameDefSite(index UseIndex, fresh UseIndex, v ValueId) returns bool
	let kind = index.defKindOf(v)
	if kind != fresh.defKindOf(v) 'differentKind'
		return false
	end 'differentKind'

	match kind 'kind'
		none then return true
		phiDef then return index.defBlockOf(v) == fresh.defBlockOf(v)
		opDef then return index.defBlockOf(v) == fresh.defBlockOf(v) and index.requireDefOp(v) == fresh.requireDefOp(v)
	end 'kind'
end 'sameDefSite'

function sameSpillSlot(a ValueSpillSlot, b ValueSpillSlot) returns bool
	match a 'a'
		none then return spillSlotIsNone(b)
		slot(s) then return spillSlotIsSlot(b, s: s)
	end 'a'
end 'sameSpillSlot'

function spillSlotIsNone(spilled ValueSpillSlot) returns bool
	return match spilled 'k'
		none gives true
		slot gives false
	end 'k'
end 'spillSlotIsNone'

function spillSlotIsSlot(spilled ValueSpillSlot, s SpillSlot) returns bool
	return match spilled 'k'
		none gives false
		slot(other) gives other == s
	end 'k'
end 'spillSlotIsSlot'

// The chosen peak, field for field — INCLUDING its live-before row, which is the candidate pool
// `chooseVictim` draws from. Everything the split's decision rests on has to be identical, not merely
// close: the same peak, the same victim, the same order.
function verifyPeak(peak PressurePeak, fresh PressurePeak, wordsPerRow BitWordCount, name String, iterations EventCount)
	if peak.found != fresh.found 'found'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the incremental peak-finder and a full sweep disagree about whether ANY point is over its pool")
	end 'found'
	if not peak.found 'noPeak'
		return
	end 'noPeak'

	if peak.pressure != fresh.pressure 'pressure'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): peak pressure {peak.pressure} but a full sweep says {fresh.pressure}")
	end 'pressure'
	if peak.blockLocal != fresh.blockLocal 'blockLocal'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the peak is in block {peak.blockLocal} but a full sweep says block {fresh.blockLocal}")
	end 'blockLocal'
	if peak.peakSeq != fresh.peakSeq 'peakSeq'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the peak sits at sequence {peak.peakSeq} but a full sweep says {fresh.peakSeq}")
	end 'peakSeq'
	if peak.peakOpIdx != fresh.peakOpIdx 'peakOpIdx'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the peak op is {peak.peakOpIdx} but a full sweep says {fresh.peakOpIdx}")
	end 'peakOpIdx'
	if peak.clobberOnly != fresh.clobberOnly 'clobberOnly'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the incremental peak-finder and a full sweep disagree about whether the overflow is CONFINED — the two have different eligibility tiers")
	end 'clobberOnly'
	if peak.witness != fresh.witness 'witness'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the peak's witness is {peak.witness} but a full sweep says {fresh.witness}")
	end 'witness'
	if peak.atFixedRegisterOp != fresh.atFixedRegisterOp 'atFixedRegisterOp'
		panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the incremental peak-finder and a full sweep disagree about whether the peak op itself clobbers")
	end 'atFixedRegisterOp'

	for w in 0 upto wordsPerRow 'eachWord'
		let actual = try peak.liveBefore.get(w) otherwise panic("verifyIncremental: liveBefore.get OOB at word {w}")
		let expected = try fresh.liveBefore.get(w) otherwise panic("verifyIncremental: liveBefore.get OOB at word {w}")
		if actual != expected 'liveBefore'
			panic("verifyIncremental ('{name}', after split {iterations}): the peak's live-before row differs from a full sweep's at word {w} — the candidate pool the victim is chosen from is wrong")
		end 'liveBefore'
	end 'eachWord'
end 'verifyPeak'
